entry_str:
off, from;
G. hljóp af hesti sínum, G. jumped off his horse;
ganga af mótinu, to go away from the meeting;
Flosi kastaði af sér skikkjunni, threw off his cloak;
Gizzur gekk af útsuðri at gerðinu, from the south-west;
hann hafði leyst af sér skúa sína, he had taken off his shoes;
Steinarr vildi slíta hann af sér, throw him off;
tók Gísli þá af sér vápnin, took off his arms;
bréf af Magnúsi konungi, a letter from king Magnus;
land af landi, from one land to the other;
hverr af öðrum, one after another, in succession;
vil ek þú vinnir af þér skuldina, work off the debt;
muntu enga sætt af mér fá, no peace at my hand;
rísa af dauða, to rise from the dead;
vakna af draumi, to awaken from a dream;
lúka upp af hrossi, to open a gate from off a horse;
vindr stóð af landi, the wind blew from the land;

out of;
verða tekinn af heimi, to be taken out of the world;
gruflar hón af læknum, she scrambles out of the brook;
Otradalr var mjök af vegi, far out of the way.
Connected with út; föstudaginn fór út herrinn af borginni, marched out of the town.

Of time; past, beyond:
af ómagaaldri, able to support oneself, of age;
ek em nú af léttasta skeiði, no longer in the prime of life;
þá er sjau vikur eru af sumri, when seven weeks of summer are past;
var mikit af nótt, much of the night was past.

þiggja lið af e-m, to receive help from one;
hafa umboð af e-m, to be another’s deputy;
vera góðs (ills) maklegr af e-m, to deserve good (bad) of one;
féll þar lið mart af Eyvindi, many of Eyvind’s men fell there;
þá eru þeir útlagir ok af goðorði sínu, have forfeited their goðorð;
þá skalt þú af allri fjárheimtunni, forfeit all the claim;
ek skal stefna þér af konunni, summon thee to give up;

off, of;
höggva fót, hönd, af e-m, to cut off one’s foot, hand;
vil ek, at þú takir slíkt sem þér líkar af varningi, whatever you like of the stores;
þar lá forkr einn ok brotit af endanum, with the point broken off;
absol., beit hann höndina af, bit the hand off;
fauk af höfuðit, the head flew off;

on account of, by reason of, by;
úbygðr at frosti ok kulda, because of frost and cold;
ómáli af áverkum, speechless from wounds;
af ástæld hans, by his popularity;
af því, therefore;
af hví, wherefor why;
af því at, because;

by means of, by;
framfœra e-n af verkum sínum, by means of his own labour;
af sínu fé, by one’s own means;
absol., hann fekk af hina mestu sœmd, derived great honour from it;

with adjectives, in regard to;
mildr af fé, liberal of money;
góðr af griðum, merciful;
fastr af drykk, close (stingy) in regard to drink;

used absol. with a verb, off away;
hann bað hann þá róa af fjörðinn, to row the firth off;
ok er þeir höfðu af fjörðung, when they had covered one forth of the way;
sofa af nóttina, to sleep the night away.

age, life-time;
sautján vetra at ~i, seventeen years of age;
ungr at ~i, young;
á ungum ~i, in (one’s) youth;
á gamals ~i, in (one’s) old age;
hniginn at ~i, in the decline of life;
hniginn á efra ~, stricken in years;
á léttasta ~i, in the prime of life;
lifa langan ~, to enjoy a long life;
nema, ræna e-n ~i, to take one’s life;
týna (slíta) ~i, to lose one’s life;

long period, age (eptir marga vetr ok mikinn ~);
allan ~, through all time, always;
of ~, um ~ (ok æfi), for ever and ever;
after a negation, ever (þvílíka gersemi höfum vér eigi sét um ~);
hverr um ~ var svá djarfr, who ever was so bold?.

all, entire, whole;
hón á allan arf eptir mik, she has all the heritage after me;
af öllum hug, with all (one’s) heart;
hvítr ~, white all over;
bú allt, the whole estate;
allan daginn, the whole day;
í ~i veröld, in the whole world;
allan hálfan mánuð, for the entire fortnight;
with addition of ‘saman’;
allt saman féit, the whole amount;
um þenna hernað allan saman, all together;

used almost adverbially, all, quite, entirely;
klofnaði hann ~ í sundr, he was all cloven asunder, kváðu Örn allan villast, that he was altogether bewildered;
var Hrappr ~ brottu, quite gone;
~ annarr maðr, quite another man;

gone, past;
áðr þessi dagr er ~, before this day is past;
var þá óll þeirra vinátta, their friendship was all over;
allt er nú mitt megin, my strength is exhausted, gone;

neut. sing. (allt) used. as a subst. in the sense of all, everything;
eigi er enn þeirra allt, they have not yet altogether won the game;
þá var allt (all, everybody) við þá hrætt;
hér er skammætt allt, here everything is transient;
with a compar. all the more (því öllu þungbærri);
with gen., allt missera (= öll misseri), all the year round;
allt annars, all the rest;
at öllu annars, in all other respects;
alls fyrst, first of all;
alls mest, most of all;
in adverbial phrases: at öllu, in all respects, in every way;
í öllu, in everything;
með öllu, wholly, quite;
neita með öllu, to refuse outright;

pl. allir (allar, öll), as adj. or substantively, all (þeir gengu út allir);
ór öllum fjórðungum á landinu, from all the quarters of the land;
allir aðrir, all others, every one else;
flestir allir, nearly all, the greatest part of;
gen. pl. (~a) as an intensive with superlatives, of all things, all the more;
nú þykkir mér þat ~a sýnst, er, all the more likely, as;
~a helzt, er þeir heyra, particularly now when they hear;
~a sízt, least of all.

a. ekki (eigi), not at all, by no means;
þeir ugðu a. ekki at sér, they were not a bit afraid;
a. engi, no one at all;
sometimes without a negative following it, quite, altogether;
ær a. geldar, ewes quite barren;
a. vesall, altogether wretched;
hann var eigi a. tvítugr, not quite twenty years old;

one of the two, the one (of two);
Egill þessi hefir aðra hönd (only one hand) ok er kaliaðr einhendr;
á aðra hönd, on the one side;
a. … a. one … the other (hét a. Sörli, en a. Þorkell);

the next following;
á öðru hausti, the next autumn;
annat sumar eptir;
fig., next in value or rank;
annat mest hof í Noregi, the next greatest temple;
fjölmennast þing annat eptir brennu Njáls, the fullest assembly next to that after the burning of N.;

in various combinations;
~ slíkr, such another, another of the same sort;
gekk a. til at öðrum (one after another) at biðja hann;
hverja nótt aðra sem aðra, every night in turn;
annat var orð Finns harðara en annat, each word of Finn was harder than another;
aðrir … aðrir, some … others;
einir ok aðrir, various;
ymsir ok aðrir, now one, now another (nefna upp ymsa ok aðra);
hvárr (or hverr) … annan, each other, one another (hétu hvárir öðrum atförum);
við þau tíðindi urðu allir giaðir ok sagði hverr öðrum, one told the news to another, man to man.

  n., e-m er ~, eager, anxious;
mörgum var ~ heim, many were eager to get home;
hví mun honum svá ~ at hitta mik, why is he so eager?
ekki er ~ um þat, it is not pressing;
hvat er nú ~ mínum einga syni, what has my only son at heart?
sagði sér ~ (that he was busy) um daga;
vera ~ til e-s, to be eager for.

along (= eptir);
ganga at stræti, to walk along the street;
dreki er niðr fór at ánni (went down the river) fyrir strauminum;
refr dró hörpu at ísi, on the ice;

denoting presence at, near, by, upon;
at kirkju, at church;
at dómi, in court;
at lögbergi, at the hill of laws;

with proper names of places (farms);
konungr at Danmörku ok Noregi, king of;
biskup at Hólum, bishop of Holar;
at Helgafelli, at Bergþórshváli;

used ellipt. with a genitive, at (a person’s) house;
at hans (at his house) gisti fjölmenni mikit;
at Marðar, at Mara’s home;
at hins beilaga Ólafs konungs, at St. Olave’s church;
at Ránar, at Ran’s (abode).

at, in;
at upphafi, at first, in the beginning;
at skilnaði, at parting, when they parted;
at páskum, at Easter;
at kveldi, at eventide;
at þinglausnum, at the close of the Assembly;
at fjöru, at the ebb;
at flœðum, at the floodtide;

adding ‘komanda’ or ‘er kemr’;
at ári komanda, next year;
at vári, er kemr, next spring;
generally with ‘komanda’ understood;
at sumri, hausti, vetri, vári, next summer, ;

used with an absolute dative and present or past part.;
at sér lifanda, duing his lifetime;
at öllum ásjándum, in the sight of all;
at áheyranda höfðingjanum, in the hearing of the chief;
at upprennandi sólu, at sunrise;
at liðnum sex vikum, after six weeks are past;
at honum önduðum, after his death;

denoting uninterrupted succession, after;
hverr at öðrum, annarr at öðrum, one after another;
skildu menn at þessu, thereupon, after this;
at því (thereafter) kómu aðrar meyjar.

by;
taka sverð at hjöltum, by the hilt;
draga út björninn at hlustunum, by the ears;
kjósa at afli, álitum, by strength, appearrance;

with adjectives denoting colour, size, age, of;
hvítr, svartr, rauðr at lit, while, black, red of colour;
mikill, lítill at stœrð, vexti, tall, small of stature;
tvítugr at aldri, twenty years of age;
kýr at fyrsta, öðrum kálfi, a cow that has calved once, twice;

determining the source from which anything comes, of, from;
Ari nam ok marga frœði at Þuríði (from her);
þiggja, kaupa, geta, leigja e-t at e-m, to receive, buy, obtain, borrow a thing from one;
hafa veg (virðing) styrk at e-m, to derive honour, power, from one;

according, to, after (heygðr at fornum sið);
at ráði allra vitrustu manna, by the advice of;
at landslögum, by the law of the land;
at vánum, as was to be expected;
at leyfi e-s, by one’s leave;

in adverbial phrases;
gróa (vera grœddr) at heilu, to be quite healed;
bíta af allt gras at snøggu, quite bare;
at fullu, fully;
at vísu, surely;
at frjálsu, freely;
at eilífu, for ever and ever;
at röngu, at réttu, wrongly, rightly;
at líku, at sömu, equally, all the same;
at mun, at ráði, at marki, to a great extent.

with acc., after, upon (= eptir);
sonr á at taka arf at föður sinn, to take the inheritance after his father;
eiga féránsdóm at e-n, to hold a court of execution upon a person;
at þat (= eptir þat), after that, thereafter;
connected with a past part. or a., at Gamla fallinn, after the fall of Gamli;
at Hrungni dauðan, upon the death of Hrungnir.

demonstrative particle before a comparative, the, all the, so much the;
hón grét at meir, she wept the more;
þykkir oss at líkara, all the more likely;
þú ert maðr at verri (so much the worse), er þú hefir þetta mælt;

connected with þó, því, svá;
þó at (with subj.), though, although;
því at, because, for;
svá at, so that;

temp., þá at (= þá er), when;
þegar at (= þegar er), as soon as;
þar til at (= þar til er), until, till;
áðr at (= á. en), before;

incident, circumstance;
Ó. sagði honum alla atburði (all the circumstances, particulars) um sitt mál;
í hverjum atburðum, under what circumstances;
með hverjum atburðum, in what manner, how;

chance, accident;
slíkt kalla ek atburð, en eigi jartegn, such a thing I call an accident, and not a miracle;
af atburð, by chance; accidentally;
með atburð, by chance; per adventure, perhaps;

 n. of an obs. verb;
e-m verðr e-s ~, it falls to one’s lot;
oss varð eigi þeirrar hamingju ~, this good fortune was not destined for us;
þó at mér verði lífs ~, though life be granted to me;
hafði ~, they had won the day;
varð þeim eigi erfingja ~, to them no heir was granted;
~ fé, means possessed.

(að),  impers. to fall out by fate;
ef honum ~ði eigi aptr at koma, if he did not have the good fortune to return;
ef guð vill, at þess auðni, that it shall succeed;
sem ~r, as luck decides.

 thanks, gratitude, satisfaction, pleasure;
kunna e-m aufúsu fyrir e-t, to be thankful, obliged to one, for a thing;
gefa e-m aufúsu, to thank;
e-m er ~ á e-u, one is pleased, gratified, with;
ef yðr er þar nökkur ~ á, if it be any pleasure to you;
var mönnum mikil ~ á því, were much pleased by it.

eye;
lúka (bregða) upp augum, bregða augum í sundr, to open (lift up) the eyes;
lúka aptr augum, to shift the eyes;
renna (bregða, leiða) augum til e-s, to turn the eyes to;
leiða e-n augum, to measure one with the eyes;
berja augum í e-t, to take into consideration;
koma augum á e-t, to set eyes on, become aware of;
hafa ~ á e-u, t have, keep, an eye upon;
segja e-t í augu upp, to one’s face, right in the face;
unna e-m sem augum í höfði sér, as one’s own eye-balls;
e-m vex e-t í augu, one has scruples about;
náit er nef augum, the nose is neighbor to the eyes;
gløggt er gests ~t, a guest’s eye is sharp;
mörg eru dags augu, the day has many eyes;
eigi leyna augu, ef ann kona manni, the eyes cannot hide it if a woman loves a man;

to augment, increase (~ virðing e-s; ~ ætt sína; ~ vandræði);
to prolong (~ þing);
~nda ferr am e-t, something goes on increasing;
eigi er þat aukit (it is no exaggeration) þó at hann sé sagðr ríkastr maðr á Íslandi;
orðum aukit, exaggerated;
aukin (more than) þrjú hundruð manna;

to add, with dat. (jók ek því es mér varð síðar kunnara);
~ synd á synd ofan, to heap sin upon sin;
~ e-u við, to add;
~st orðum við, to come to words, to converse;
with acc., ~ ný vandræði (= nýjum vandræðum) á hin fornu, to add new difficulties to old ones;
impers., jók stórum hans harm, his grief increased greatly;

addition, increase (~ fagnaðar, pínsla);
verða at moldar auka, to become dust, to die;
til auka, in addition, to boot;
fœrast í alla auka (afls síns), to exert to the utmost one’s bodily strength;

to sprinkle, pour, with dat.;
þær taka hvern dag vatn í brunninum ok ~ (viz. því) upp yfir askinn, pour it over the ash-tree;
~ síld ór netjum, to empty the nets of the herrings;
fig., ~ sauri á e-n, to bespatter with abuse;
~ e-m e-u í augu upp, to throw … in one’s face;

on, upon, in;
á gólfi, on the floor;
á hendi, on the hand (of a ring);
á sjá ok landi, on sea and land;
á bókum enskum, in English books;
á himni, in heaven;
á jörðu, on earth;
hafa lykil á sér, … on one’s person;
vera á þingi, to be present at a meeting;
vera í skógi, to be out in a forest (of a hunter, robber, deer);
þær eyjar liggja á Breiðafirði, … in Broad firth;

during, in the course of;
á nótt, by night;
á degi, by day;
á því ári, vári, sumri, in that year, spring, summer;
á þrem sumrum, in the course of three summers;
var hann á fám dögum heill, within few days;
á dögum e-s, in the days of, in his reign or time;

used of a fixed recurrent period or season;
á várum, sumrum, haustum, vetrum, every spring, summer, ;
á kveldum, in the evening;
þrisvar á ári, mánuði, three times a year, a month.

denoting personal relation, in, to, towards;
bœta e-t á e-m, to make amends to one (personally);
sjá á e-m, to read one or in one’s face;
hafa elsku (hatr) á e-m, to bear love (hatred) to one;
rjúfa sætt á e-m, to break a truce on one;

with a reflexive pron., denoting personal appearance, temper, look;
vera þungr, léttr á sér, to be heavy or light (either bodily or mentally);
kátr ok léttr á sér, of a gay and light disposition;
þat bragð hafði hann á sér, he looked as if;
sýna fáleika á sér, to show displeasure;

as periphrasis of the possessive pron. or a genitive;
hann rétti á sér fingrna, he stretched his fingers;
fótrinn á honum, his foot;
í vörunum á honum, in his lips;
stafn, skutr, árar, segl á skipi, the stem, stern, oars, sail of a ship;
blöð á tré, leaves of a tree;
egg á sverði = sverðsegg;

denoting instrumentality, by, by means of;
afla fjár á hólmgöngum, to make money by duelling;
fella e-n á bragði, … by a trick (in wrestling);
komast undan á flótta, to escape by flight;

with numerals;
á þriðja tigi manna, between twenty and thirty;
á níunda tigi, between eighty and ninety years of age;
á öðru hundraði skipa, with above a hundred sail;

in many adverbial phrases;
á lífi, alive;
á brautu, away;
á floti, afloat;
á milli, between;
á laun, secretly;
á móti, against;
á enda, at an end;
á flugi, a-flying, in the air;
á gangi, walking;
á skjálfi, a-shivering;
á hleri, a-listening;
á veiðum, a-hunting;
á beit, a-grazing;

used absol. in reference to the air or the weather;
þoka var á mikil, a thick fog was on;
allhvast á norðan, very strong breeze from the north;
gørði á regn, rain came on.

indicating direction with or without motion, towards, to, on, upon;
ganga á land, to go ashore;
ganga á skóg, into the wood;
stíga á skip, to go on board;
á bæði borð, on both sides (of the ship);
á hlið, sidewise;
höggva á tvær hendr, to hew or strike right and left;
stefna á fótinn, to aim at the foot;
gekk veðrit á vestr, the wind veered to the west;
hárit tók ofan á belti, reached down to the belt;
reka austr á haf, to drift eastwards on the sea;

in some cases the acc. is used instead of the dat., esp. with verbs denoting to hear or to see;
þeir sá boða mikinn inn á fjörðinn, they saw great breakers away in the firth;
with phrases denoting to be placed, seated, to sit, the seat is freq. in acc.;
sitja á miðjan bekk, to be seated on the middle bench;
skyldi konungs sæti vera á þann bekk;

denoting parts of the body;
bíta e-n á barka, to bite one in the throat;
skera e-n á háls, to cut one’s throat;
kalinn á kné, frozen (frost-bitten) up to the knee.

connected with ‘evening, morning, the seasons’ with the article;
á kveldit, every evening;
á sumarit, every summer;
á vetrinn, in the winter-time;
the pl., however, is much more common in such cases;
á nætrnar, by night, everynight;
á morgnana, in the morning, every morning;
á kveldin, in the evening;
á várin, in spring;
á sumrin, in summer;
á haustin, in autumn (cf. A. II. 1.);
‘dagr’ is always used in sing. with the art. suffixed;
á daginn, during the day-time, every day in turn;

on, upon, to;
bjóða vöxtu á féit, to offer interest on the money;
fá á e-n, to make an impression upon;
koma á rœðu við e-n, to enter into talk with, to speak with;

denoting skill, dexterity;
hagr á tré, a good carpenter;
hagr á járn, málm, an expert worker in iron, metals;
fimir á boga, good at the bow;
meistari á hörpuslátt, a master in harp-playing;
mikill á íþrótt, highly accomplished;
frœðimaðr á landnámssögur ok forna frœði, a great scholar in history and antiquities;

denoting instrumentality;
bjargast á sínar hendr, to live on the work of one’s own hands;
vega á skállir, to weigh in scales;
nefna e-n á nafn, by name;

denoting manner;
á þessa lund, in this wise;
á marga (ymsa, alla) vega, in many (various, all) respects;
esp. of a language; mæla, rita á e-a tungu, to speak, write in a tongue;
á Írsku, in Irish;

connected with nouns, in prepositional and adverbial phrases;
á hendr e-m, against;
á hœla e-m, at one’s heels, close behind;
á bak e-m, at the back of, behind;
á braut, away;
á víxl, so as to cross, cross-wise.

 responsibility;
vera í ~ um e-t, to be responsible for;
vera ór ~ um e-t, to be exempt from responsibility in a case;
hafa mikit í ~, have much at stake;
e-t er í ~um við mik, I am responsible for it.

 forward, onward;
féll hann ~, on the face;
komst aldri lengra ~ fyrir honum um skáldskapinn, he never got any further in his verse making;
héldu þeir ~ leiðina, they held forward on their way.

onwards forwards (fóru ~ til skipa sinna);
vildu snúa ~ för sinni, proceed on their journey;
fig., koma e-u ~, to bring about;
víkja ~ með e-m, to side with one;

to intend, purpose (ek ~ ok styrkja Gunnar at nökkuru);
þeir ætluðu ekki lengra í kveld, they did not intend to go any farther to-night;
þat verðr hverr at vinna, er ~t er, every one must do the work that is set before him;
~ e-m e-t, to set apart for one, allot to one (hann ~ði þrælum sínum dagsverk);
to intend a thing for one (hann keypti þar pell ágætt, er hann ~ði Ólafi konungi);
to expect from one, suspect one of (þér mundi þat engi maðr ~);
~ sér hóf, to keep within bounds;
~ sér (with infin.), to intend, purpose (ef hann ~r sér at keppa við oss);
suðr ~ ek mér at ganga, I intend to go south (to Rome);
~ fyrir (with infin.), to intend (eru þat hin mestu firn, at þér ætlið fyrir at leggja á allt fólk ánauðarok);
~ e-t fyrir, to foredoom, foreordain (þat mun verða um forlög okkur sem áðr er ~t fyrir);
~ fyrir sér, to think beforehand, expect (mart verðr annan veg en maðrinn ~r fyrir sér);
~ til e-s staðar, to intend to go to a place (þeir sigldu nú ok ~, til Miklagarðs);
~ til e-s, to reckon upon, expect (eigi þarftu til þess at ~, at ek ganga við frændsemi við yðr);
hann tók sér bústað ok ~ði þar landeign til, he destined a piece of land for that purpose;

back (binda bagga á ~ sér);
fig. bera sök á ~i, to be guilty;
leggja bleyðiorð á ~ e-m, to charge one with being a coward;
hafa marga vetr á ~i, to be advanced in years;
snúa ~i við, to turn the back, flee;
ganga á ~ heitum, orðum, to go back on one’s word;

the backside of a thing (cf. handar~, hurðar~, hús~);
á ~i húsunum, at the back of the houses;
gøra e-t á ~i e-m, in one’s absence, behind one’s back;
ríða at ~i e-m, behind him on the same horse;
berr er hverr at ~i nema sér bróður eigi, bare is one’s back without a brother behind it;
á ~,
a) behind;
koma á ~ e-m, to attack in the rear;
b) after;
á ~ jólum, after Christmas;
hvat sem á ~ kemr, whatever may come after or follow;
c) absol., falla á ~ aptr, to fall backwards;
brjóta á ~ orð e-s, to refute, make them null and void;
brjóta á ~ Rómverja, to defeat them.

 dangerous, dire;
böll ráð, fatal schemes;
ballir draumar, bad, ill-boding dreams;
böll þrá, heavy grief.

in pl.:
a) bonds, fetters (hafa e-n í böndum);
b) bond, confederacy (ganga í bönd ok eið);
c) poet., the gods, cf. höpt;
blóta bönd, to worship the gods;
at mun banda, at the will of the gods.

death;
fá (bíða, hafa, taka) bana, to die;
ráða sér bana, to commit suicide;
leiða e-n til bana, to cause one’s death (of an illness);
kominn at bana, sinking fast;

(-dda, -ddr),  to ask, beg, request;
~ e-n e-s, or ~ e-m (for one) e-s;
~ e-s af e-m, to ask a thing of or from a person;
~ e-n máls, orða, to address one;
with acc., ~ lögbeiðing, to make a lawful request;
refl., ~st, to request on one’s own behalf (~ laga, griða).

to stretch out, put into motion;
~ flug, to stretch the wings for flight;
~ skrið sinn, to creep, of a serpent;
~ raustina, to raise the voice, speak aloud;

to further, promote;
~ för (ferð) e-s, to help one forwards;
~ at or til með e-m, to lend one help, to assist one;
~ e-u til e-s, to contribute to a thing;
~ at e-u, to lend a hand to;
~ fyrir e-m, to support, entertain.

to ‘cause to bite’, graze, with the animals in dat. (~ nautum), the pasture in acc. (~ haga, land, engi);
absol. to pasture cattle (~ í skógi);
~ upp land, to exhaust by grazing;
~ upp (to consume) engjum ok heyjum;

to harness (a horse, ) to a vehicle (~ hest fyrir vagn);
beittu enn blakka mar, saddle thy black steed;
fig., ~ e-n fyrir e-t, to put one at the head of;
refl., ~st fyrir e-t or e-u, to lead the cause;

(-nda, -ndr),  to bend (~ sverð um kné sér);
~ boga, to bend a bow;
~ höfuðit, to bow the head;
refl., ~st á um e-t, to dispute, contest about.

to bear, carry, convey (bar B. biskup í börum suðr í Hvamm);
~ (farm) af skipi, to unload a ship;
~ (mat) af borði, to take (the meat) off the table;
~ e-t á hesti, to carry on horseback;

to bear, give birth to, esp. of sheep and cows;
kýr hafði borit kálf, had calved;
absol., ván at hón mundi ~, that the cow would calve;
the pp. is used of men; hann hafði verit blindr borinn, born blind;
verða borinn í þenna heim, to be born into this world;
þann sóma, sem ek em til borinn, born to;
borinn e-m, frá e-m (rare), born of;
Nótt var Nörvi borin, was the daughter of N.;
borinn Sigmundi, son of S.;

~ e-n afli, ofrafli, ofrliði, ofrmagni, ofríki, to bear one down, overcome, oppress, one by odds or superior force;
~ e-n ráðum, to overrule one;
~ e-n málum, to bear one down (wrongfully) in a lawsuit;
~ e-n sök, to charge one with a fault;
~ e-n bjóri, to make drunk with beer;
verða bráðum borinn, to be taken by surprise;
borinn verkjum, overcome by pains;
þess er borin ván, there is no hope, all hope is gone;
borinn baugum, bribed; cf. ~ fé á e-n, to bribe one;

to lear, be capable of bearing (of a ship, horse, vehicle);
þeir hlóðu bæði skipin sem borð báru, with as much as they could carry;
fig., to sustain, support (svá mikill mannfjöldi, at landit fekk eigi borit);
of persons, to bear up against, endure, support (grief, sorrow, etc.);
absol., bar hann drengiliga, he bore it manfully;
similarly, ~ (harm) af sér, ~st vel (illa, lítt) af;
bar hon sköruliga af sér, she bore up bravely;
hversu berst Auðr af um bróðurdauðann, how does she bear it?
hon berst af lítt, she is much cast down;
~ sik vel upp, to bear well up against;

~ e-t á, e-n á hendr e-m, to charge or tax one with (eigi erum vér þess valdir, er þú berr á oss);
~ (kvið) á e-n, to give a verdict against, declare guilty (í annat sinn báru þeir á Flosa kviðinn);
~ af e-m (kviðinn), to give a verdict for;
~ e-t af sér, to deny having done a thing;
~ or ~ vitni, vætti, to bear witness, testify;
~ or ~ um e-t, to give a verdict in a case;
~ e-n sannan at sök, to prove guilty by evidence;
~ e-n undan sök, to acquit;
~ í sundr frændsemi þeirra, to prove (by evidence) that they are not relations;
~ e-m vel (illa) söguna, to give a favourable (unfavourable) account of one;
refl. (pass.), ~st, to be proved by evidence (þótt þér berist þat faðerni, er þú segir);

to set forth, report, tell;
~ e-m kveðju (orð, orðsending), to bring one a greeting, compliments (word, message);
~ or ~ fram erindi sín fyrir e-n, to state (tell) one’s errand or to plead one’s case before one;
~ e-m njósn, to apprise one;
~ e-t upp, to produce, mention, tell;
~ upp gátu, to give (propound) a riddle;
~ upp erindi sín, to state one’s errand;
~ saman ráð sín, to consult together;
eyddist það ráð, er þeir báru saman, which they had designed;

to keep, hold, bear, of a title (~ jarlnafn, konnungsnafn);
~ (eigi) giptu, gæfu, hammingju, auðnu til e-s, (not) to have the good fortune to do a thing (bar hann enga gæfu til at þjóna þér);
~ vit, skyn, kunnáttu á e-t, to have knowledge of, uniderstanding about;
vel viti borinn, endowed with a good understanding;
~ hug, áræði, þor, traust til e-s, to have courage, confidence to do a thing;
~ áhyggju fyrir e-u, to be concerned about;
~ ást, elsku, hatr til e-s, to bear affection, love, hatred to;

to bear off or away, carry off (some gain);
~ sigr af e-m, af e-u, to carry off the victory from or in;
hann hafði borit sigr af tveim orustum, he had been victorious in two battles;
~ hærra (lægra) hlut to get the best (the worst) of it;
~ efra (hærra) skjöld, to gain the victory;
~ hátt (lágt) höfuðit, to bear the head high (low), to be in high (low) spirits;
~ halann bratt, lágt, to cock up or let fall the tail, to be in high or low spirits;

with preps.:
~ af e-m, to surpass;
en þó bar Bolli af, surpassed all the rest;
~ af sér högg, lag to ward off, parry a blow or thrust;
~ eld at, to set fire to;
~ fjötur (bönd) at e-m, to put fetters (bonds) on one;
~ vápn á e-n, to attack one with sharp weapons;
~ á or í, to smear, anoint (~ vatn í augu sér, ~ tjöru í höfuð sér);
~ e-t til, to apply to, to try if it fits (~ til hvern lykil af öðrum at portinu);
~ e-t um, to wind round;
þá bar hann þá festi um sik, made it fast round his body;
~ um með e-n, to bear with, have patience with;
~ út barn, to expose a child;

refl., ~st mikit (lítit) á, to bear oneself proudly (humbly);
láta af ~st, to die;
láta fyrir ~st e-s staðar, to stay, remain in a place (for shelter);
~st e-t fyrir, to design a thing (barst hann þat fyrir at sjá aldregi konur);
at njósna um, hvat hann bærist fyrir, to inquire into what he was about;
~st vápn á, to attack one another;
~st at or til, to happen;
þat barst at (happened) á einhverju sumri;
ef svá harðliga kann til at ~st, if that misfortune does happen;
~st í móti, to happen, occur;
hefir þetta vel í móti borizt, it is a happy coincidence;
~st við, to be prevented;
ok nú lét almáttugr guð við ~st kirkjubrunann, prevented, stopped the burning of the church;

with acc., it bears or carries one to a place;
alla berr at sama brunni, all come to the same well (end);
bar hann (acc.) þá ofan gegnt Ösuri, he happened to come down just opposite to Ö.;
esp. of ships and sailors; berr oss (acc.) til Íslands eða annarra landa, we drift to Iceland or other countries;
þá (acc.) bar suðr í haf, they were carried out southwards;
Skarpheðin (acc.) bar nú at þeim, S. came suddenly upon them;
ef hann (acc.) skyldi ~ þar at, if he should happen to come there;
e-n berr yfir, one is borne onwards, of a bird flying, a man riding;
hann (acc.) bar skjótt yfir, it passed quickly (of a flying meteor);

followed by preps.:
Gunnar sér, at rauðan kyrtil bar við glugginn, that a red kirtle passed before the window;
hvergi bar skugga (acc.) á, there was nowhere a shadow;
e-t berr fram (hátt), is prominent;
Ólafr konungr stóð í lyptingu ok bar hann (acc.) hátt mjök, stood out conspicuously;
e-t berr á milli, comes between;
leiti (acc.) bar á milli, a hill hid the prospect;
fig. e-m berr e-t á milli, they are at variance about a thing;
mart (acc.) berr nú fyrir augu mér, many things come now before my eyes;
veiði (acc.) berr í hendr e-m, game falls to one’s lot;
e-t berr undan, goes amiss, fails;
~ saman, to coincide;
bar nöfn þeirra saman, they had the same name;
fig., with dat.; bar öllum sögum vel saman, all the stories agreed well together;
fund várn bar saman, we met;

~ at, til, við, at hendi, til handa, to befall, happen, with dat. of the person;
svá bar at einn vetr, it happened one winter;
þó at þetta vandræði (acc.) hafi nú borit oss (dat.) at hendi, has befallen us;
bar honum svá til, it so befell him;
þat bar við (it so happened), at Högni kom;
raun (acc.) berr á, it is proved by fact;

denoting cause;
e-t berr til, causes a thing;
konungr spurði, hvat til bæri úgleði hans, what was the cause of his grief;
ætluðu þat þá allir, at þat mundi til ~, that that was the reason;
berr e-m nauðsyn til e-s, one is obliged to do a thing;

e-t berr undir e-n, falls to a person’s lot;
hon á arf at taka, þegar er undir hana berr, in her turn;
e-t berr frá, is surpassing;
er sagt, at þat (acc.) bæri frá, hvé vel þeir mæltu, it was extraordinary how well they spoke;

to beat, strike, smite (~ e-n);
hár svá fagrt sem barit gull, as beaten gold;
~ korn af hálmi, to thresh;
~ húð af e-m, to scourge severely;
~ e-n grjóti, to stone (= grýta);
~ e-n illyrðum, ávítum, to abuse, reproach one;
~ á e-m, to attack one with blows, give one a thrashing;
~ á hurð, dyrr, at hurðu, at dyrum, to knock, rap at a door;
~ sér á brjóst, to smite one’s breast (in repentance);
~ til e-s = ~ á e-m;
~ e-n til e-s, to drive one with blows to do a thing (verða barðr til bœkr);

with dat., ~ grjóti í andlit e-m, to throw stones in one’s face;
~ saman vápnum, skjöldum, to dash weapons, shields against each other;
~ (to neglect, slight) guðs boðum;
~ e-u niðr (opp. to ljósta e-u upp), to hush up;

better;
~ þœtti mér, I would rather;
vánu ~, better than expected;
hafa ~, to get the better of it;

more;
leggit fram ~ hit mikla skipit, bring further forward;
þrjú hundruð ok þrír tigir ok sex ~, to boot;
ef hann orkar (or má) ~, if he can do more;
ekki máttu sumir inenn ~ en fá staðizt, they were just able to keep up against him;
svá hár, at engi annarra tók ~ en í öxl honum, reached higher than to his shoulder.

(older baztr),  a. superl. (see betri) best;
~ bóndi, an excellent farmer;
er mér hefir ~ verit, best (kindest) towards me;
with gen., meðan bezt er sumars, during the best part of the summer.

to ask, beg a thing of one (~ e-n e-s);
with infin., to beg one to do a thing;
inn bið þú hann ganga, ask him to step in;
with the infin. sign ‘at’, to beg leave;
biðr hann at fara norðr á Hálogaland, asked for furlough to go to H.;
with ‘at’ and a subj. (~ viljum vér, at þú sér í liðsbóninni með oss);
~ e-s, to beg for a thing (~ miskunnar, matar);
~ e-m e-s, to beg for a thing on behalf of one;
~ e-m lífs, griða, to beg for one’s life, to sue for quarter for another;
~ sér ölmusu, to ask for alms;
~ (sér) konu, to ask in marriage;
~ fyrir e-m, to intercede (pray) for one;

to bind, tie, fasten, tie up;
~ hest, hund, to tie up a horse, dog;
~ skó, þvengi, to tie shoes, thongs;
~ stein við háls e-m, to fasten a stone to one’s neck;
~ fyrir augu e-m, to blindfold one;

to bind in parcels, to pack up (~ varning til skips);
~ hey á hest, to truss hay on a horse’s back;
~ korn, to tie up sheaves of corn;
~ klyf, to tie up a pack;

~ e-t um e-t, to bind round;
hann batt silkiræmu um fót sér, he bound a strip of silk round his leg;
~ um e-t, to put a bandage on;
batt Yngvildr um fót honum, bound up his (wounded) leg;
~ um sár, to bind up a wound;
fig., hefir margr hlotit um sárt at ~ fyrir mér, many a man has had wounds to tie up by my means (i. e. inflicted by me);
þykkir mér bezt um heilt at ~, to bind a sound limb, to keep safe and sound;
~ sár = ~ um sár;
~ hönd e-s, to bind his hand;

to make, form, contract, enter into (~ samfélag, vináttu, tengdir, hjúskap);
~ sætt ok frið, to make a reconciliation and peace;
~ ráð, to resolve;
absol. with infin., to fix, engage (bundu þeir Þórir at hittast á ákveðnum stáð);

refl., to bind or engage oneself;
em ek þó eigi þess búinn, nema fleiri bindist, unless more persons bind themselves, enter the league;
~st á hendi (höndum) e-m, to bind oneself to serve another (~st á hendi konungum);
~st fyrir e-u, to put oneself at the head of an undertaking;
~st (= ~ sik) í e-u, to engage in a thing (~st í heitum);
~st í banns atkvæði, to bring on oneself the sentence of excommunication;
with gen. (~st e-s = ~st af or frá e-u), to refrain from a thing;
eigi bazt hann ferligra orða, he did not refrain from bad words.

to brighten, light up, illuminate;
var birt allt hlýrit (the bow of the vessel was made bright) bæði hvítum steini ok rauðum;
stjörnur ~ náttina, light up the night;
~ blinda, to make the blind see;
~ hjörtu vár, to enlighten our hearts;
~ rœðu, to elucidate, make it clearer;
impers., veðrit (acc.) birtir, it clears up;
þokunni (dat.) birtir, the fog lifts;

to reveal, manifest, show (skjótt mun ek ~ mitt skaplyndi);
with dat., ~ ást sinni, to manifest one’s love;
refl., ~st, to appear (birtist þá skaði þeirra);
dagrinn birtist, the day dawns;
~st e-m, to appear to one.

to abide, wait for, with gen., bíð þú mín hér, wait for me here;
þeir biðu byrjar, they waited for favourable wind;
~ sinnar stundar, to bide one’s time;
~ e-s ór stað (þaðan, héðan), to stand waiting in the same place (there, here);
var eigi langt at ~, at (it was not long before) sannaðist saga ábóta míns;

to suffer, undergo, sustain, with acc.;
~ bana (dauða, hel), skaða, to suffer death, loss;
~ ámæli, to be blamed;
~ ósigr, to be defeated;
~ enga ró, to feel no peace, be uneasy;
~ bót (bœtr) e-s, to recover, overcome;

to bite with the teeth (hundr bítr mann; E. beit skarð ór horninu);
~ gras, to graze, also absol. (naut bitu á velli);
~ af e-m eyrun, to bite his ears;
~ e-t í sundr, to bite asunder or through;
~ á vörrinni, kampinum, to bite the lip, the beard;
impers., of fish, to bite, take the bait (bítr vel á um daginn);

of sharp instruments, weapons; þeir er eigi bitu járn, those whom iron could not bite;
sverðit beit eigi, did not cut;
e-m bítr, one’s weapon (scythe) cuts well (allt bitu honum annan veg vápnin);
fig., beit þetta ráð, was effective;

fig., to bite, sting, hurt (þik skulu allir eiðar ~, þeir er Helga hafðir unna);
sekt, sök bítr e-n, one is convicted or found guilty;
þau mál, sem sekt bítr, matters liable to punishment;
~ á e-n, to affect, make an impression on one;
þetta lét Kjartan á sik ~, K. took this to heart;
láta e-t eigi á sik ~, to be proof against, to be unaffected by a thing;

to help, save, with dat.;
nema Þ. byrgi honum, unless Th. helped him;
sá er öldum bergr, who saves mankind (viz. against the giants, i. e. Thor);
guðs son er öllum heimi barg, who saved the whole world;
impers., e-m er borgit, one is saved, comes safe and sound out of danger (brutu skip sitt ok týndu fé öllu, en mönnum varð borgit flestum);
~ skipshöfn, to rescue the shipwrecked;
~ skipi, to haul a ship out of the reach of the tide;
~ hval, to secure a dead whale (by dragging it ashore);
~ konum, to help labouring women (cf. bjargrúnar);
~ kúm, to attend cows calving;
~ nám, to render the last service to dead bodies (cf. nábjargir);
~ sök, máli, to succeed in winning a case, a suit;

refl., ~st, to keep up the heart, esp. against cold or hunger;
Oddr bargst vel á fjallinu (in a snow storm);
~st sjálfr, to gain one’s bread;
~st á sínar hendr, to support oneself with one’s own hands;
~st úti, to find one’s food (graze) in the field (of cattle);
Snorri góði fann, at nafni hans bargst lítt við ostinn, that he got on slowly with eating the cheese;
verði þér nú at ~st við slík sem til er, you must now put up with what you can get.

to offer;
þeir höfðu boðit honum laun, they had offered him rewards;
Þ. bauð at gefa (offered to give) Gunnlaugi hestinn;
~ grið, to offer pardon;
~ e-t til lífs sér, as a ransom for one’s life;
~ e-t fram, to proffer, produce (~ fram vitni);
~ e-t upp, af hendi, to give up, leave off;
þá býðr hann upp hornit, gives up the horn, will not drink more;
~ e-t undan e-m, to offer to take a thing off one’s hands (er þá kostr at ~ undan þeim manni varðveizluna fjárins);
~ e-t við, to make a bid;
~ við tvenn verð, to bid double;
refl., ~st, to offer oneself, volunteer one’s service;
Þóroddr bauzt (offered himself) til þeirrar farar;

to do a thing to one, in a bad sense;
~ e-m ógn, to wage war against one;
~ e-m ójöfnuð, ofriki, to treat unfairly, oppress;
~ e-m ógn, to affright, terrify;
~ e-m rangt, to treat one unjustly;

to bid, order;
sem lög buðu, as the law prescribed;
~ e-m erendi, to commit a thing to one’s charge;
~ e-m varnað á e-u, to forbid;
~ e-m af landi, to order one out of the land;
~ e-m af embætti, to depose one;
~ út liði, skipum, to levy, troops, ships;
~ e-m um, to delegate to one, to commit to one’s charge (þeim manni er biskup hefir um boðit at nefna vátta);

of a mental state, to have presentiment of;
e-m býðr e-t í hug (skap), one has a fore boding, presentiment of;
mér býðr e-t fyrir, I forebode;
mér býðr hugr við e-u, I abhor, dislike;
impers., mér býðr ávallt hita (acc.), er ek kem í þeirra flokk, I feel uneasy whenever …;
mér bauð ótta, I felt a fear;
bauð þeim mikla þekt (they felt much pleasure), er þeir sá líkit;
ef yðr býðr svá við at horfa, when you are in such a frame of mind;

with dat., to set in motion by blowing or breathing;
blés mœðiliga öndinni, breathed hard;
~ e-m e-u í brjóst, to inspire, suggest a thing to one (guð blés henni því í brjóst);
~ eldi, eitri, of serpents;

to blow with a musical instrument;
~ lúðri, horni, to blow the trumpet, horn;
~ liði (troops) til landgöngu;
~ til stefnu, to a meeting;
~ herblástr, to sound an alarm;

bid, offer;
bjóða ~ fyrir e-n, to make bids or offers for one;
hvat er í boði, what is the offer;
eiga ~ á jörð, to have the right of being offered an estate, before it is sold to another;

the side of a ship (hlóðu skipin sem ~ báru);
~ á stjórn = stjórnborði;
hlaupa (steypast, detta) fyrir ~, overboard;
mikill ~i, prop. = borðhár, borðmikill, of a ship floating high out of the sea, fig., proud of mind (= skapstórr);
bera e-n (hlut e-s, mál e-s) fyrir ~, to neglect, slight one;
verða (allr) fyrir ~ borinn, verða allr fyrir ~i, to be (quite) thrown overboard, i. e. slighted;
ganga at ~i við e-n, to submit, yield to, to come to terms with one;
e-n brestr á ~i, one fails, is beaten;
á annat ~, on the other hand; otherwise, else;
þykkir vera harðr á annat ~, a hard one to pull against;

board, table (used for meals);
fara, ganga, koma til ~s or undir ~, to go, come to table;
setjast yfir ~, undir ~, to sit down at table;
sitja yfir ~ or ~um, to sit at table;
rísa (standa upp) frá ~i, to rise from table;
ryðja ~, to clear the table;
þjóna fyrir ~um, to wait at table;
setja e-m ~, to set a table before one;
~ eru uppi or upp tekin, the tables are set up; but ‘~ eru upp tekin’ may also mean the tables are removed, = ~ eru ofan;

book;
lesa á ~, to read a book;
rita á ~, setja á bœkr, to set down in writing, to put on record;
kunna (festa) et útan ~ar, to know (to get) by heart;
heilög ~, the divine book, the bible;

Latin kenna em á ~, to teach one Latin;
nema á ~, to learn Latin;
setja en til ~ar, to put one to school (in order to make him a priest);
hann heitir á ~ (in Latin) Jaskonius;
svá segir (er sagt) í ~um (in Latin books);

to cause to move (quickly);
~ sverði, knífi, to draw a sword, knife;
~ fingri, hendi í e-t, to put (thrust) the finger, hand, into;
hón brá hárinu undir belti sér, she put (fastened) her hair under her belt;
~ kaðli um e-t, to pass a rope round a thing;
~ augum sundr, to open the eyes;
~ e-m á eintal, to take one apart;
~ sér sjúkum, to feign illness;

to break up, leave off, give up;
~ tjöldum, to strike the tents;
~ samvist, to leave off living together;
~ ráðahag, to break off an engagement (wedding);
~ boði, to countermand a feast;
~ sýslu, to leave off working;
~ svefni, blundi, to awake;
~ tali, to break off talking;
~ orrustu, kaupi, to break off a battle, bargain;

with prepp.,
~ e-m á loft, to lift one aloft;
~ e-u á, to give out, pretend (hann brá á því, at hann mundi ríða vestr til Miðfjarðar);
absol., ~ á e-t, to begin (suddenly) doing a thing;
~ á leik, to begin playing or sporting;
Kimbi brá á gaman, took it playfully, laughed at it;
þeir brugðu á glímu ok á glens, they started wrestling and playing;
hestrinn brá á leik, broke into play, ran away;
hönd bregðr á venju, is ready for its old work;
þá brá Ingimundr til útanferðar, I. started to go abroad;
~ e-u undan, to put it out of the way, to hide it;
~ upp hendi, höndum, to hold up the hand;
~ e-u við, to ward off with (~ við skildi); fig. to put forth as an example, to praise, wonder at (þínum drengskap skal ek við ~);
absol., ~ við, to start off, set about a thing without delay;
brá hann við skjótt ok fór, he started off at once and went;

impers., e-u bregðr, it ceases, fails;
svá hart, at nyt bregði (to drive the ewes) so fast that they fail to give milk;
veðráttu brá eigi, there was no change in the weather;
of a sudden appearance, kláða brá á hvarmana, the eye-lids began to itch;
þá brá ljóma af Logafjöllum, then from L. there burst flashes of light;
ljósi bregðr fyrir, a light passes before the eye;
with preps., bregðr af vexti hans frá öðrum selum, his shape differs from that of other seals;
e-m bregðr í brún, one is amazed, startled (nú bregðr mönnum í brún mjök);
e-m bregðr til e-s, one person takes after, resembles another;
en því bregðr mér til foreldris míns, in that I am like my father;
þat er mælt, at fjórðungi bregði til fóstrs, the fostering makes the fourth part of a man;
e-m bregðr við e-t = e-m bregðr í brún;
brá þeim mjök við, er þeir sá hann inn ganga, it startled them much when they saw him come in;
en þó brá fóstru Melkorku mest við þessi tíðindi, this news most affected M.’s nurse.

 to fail, come to naught (þat mun eigi ~);
~ e-m, to deceive, disappoint one (Gunnar kvaðst aldri skyldu ~ Njáli né sonum hans);
þeim brást frumhlaupit, they failed in the onslaught;
~ brögðum, to play one another tricks;
~ orðum, brigzlum, to upbraid one another;
~ við, to make a sudden motion with the body (Hrútr brást skjótt við undan högginu);
~ við fast, to turn sharply;
~ á beina við e-n, to show hospitality towards;
~ reiðr við e-t, to get angry at;
~ úkunnr við e-t, to be startled at the novelty of a thing;

to be consumed by fire (á Flugumýri brann fé mikit);
nú breðr (= brennr) víðara en hann vildi, the fire spreads wider than he wished;
~ inni, to perish by fire;
~ upp, to be burnt up (á þeiri nátt brann upp allt Danavirki);
hlutr e-s brennr við, one gets the worst of it;
brann brátt þeirra hlutr við, it soon grew too hot for them;
rautt mun fyrir ~, mun nökkut fyrir ~, things will brighten up or improve.

to cauterize (as a surgical operation);
~ e-n við bölvi, to burn one to cure his malady;
~ e-m díla, to burn spots on one’s back; fig. to brand one’s back;
~ e-m illan díla, to inflict a severe injury upon one;

to break;
~ fót sinn, to break one’s leg;
~ tennr ór höfði manns, to break the teelh out of the head;
~ mann um stein, to break a man on a stone;
~ e-n í hjóli, to break on the wheel;

with preps.:
~ af brúna, to break off the bridge;
~ af við e-n, to wrong one;
~ á bak, to force or drive back (~ fylking á bak);
to neglect, disregard (~ á bak ráð e-s);
~ niðr, to demolish, break down (~ niðr hús);
~ niðr blótskap, villu, to put down, abolish;
~ sik niðr við jörðu, to bow down to the earth;
~ saman, to fold (~ saman skikkju);
to unite (~ saman et forna lögmál ok nýja);
~ sundr, í sundr, to break asunder (~ sundr silfrker);
to unfold (clothes);
~ (land, þjóð) undir sik, to subdue;
~ upp, to break up (þeir brutu upp þilit);
to force or break open (~ upp hurð, búr, kirkju, bréf);
to unpack (~ upp gersemar sínar);
~ upp vistir, to bring out the victuals (for the mess);
~ upp vápn, to get out the weapons, prepare for battle;

refl., ~st á e-t, to break in upon;
Önundr brauzt á hurðina, tried to break in the door;
~st á milli, to break out between;
~st fram, to break forth;
~st í haug, to break into a cairn;
~st í e-u, to exert oneself in a thing;
þessi maðr brýzt í miklu ofrefli, struggles against great odds;
~st um, to make a hard struggle (björn brauzt um í vök);
~st við e-t, to fight or struggle hard against;
~st við ofrefli, to fight against odds;
~st við borgargørðina, to exert oneself in making the burg;
~st við e-u, to struggle against (~st við forlögunum, gæfu sinni);

impers. in a passive sense;
skipit (acc.) braut í spán, the ship was broken to pieces;
þá braut kirkju (acc.), the church was blown down;
strauminn braut á öxlinni, the current broke against his shoulder.

household, farming;
þat er bú, er maðr hefir málnytan smala, it is ‘bú’, if a man has a milking stock;
gøra, setja, reisa bú, to set up a home for oneself;
bregða búi, to give up farming;
eiga bú við e-n, to share a household with one;
fara búi, to remove one’s household;
vera fyrir búi, to manage a household;
búa búi sínu, to have one’s own household;
búa úmegðarbúi, to have many dependants (unable to work);

to prepare, make ready;
~ skip í för, to make a ship ready for a voyage;
~ ferð sína, to make ready for a journey (voyage);
~ veizlu, to prepare (make preparations) for a feast;
~ mál á hendr e-m, to take out a summons against one, be in a lawsuit;

to dress, attire, adorn, ornament;
bjó hón hana sem hón kunni bezt, she dressed her as well as she could;
sá þeir konur vel búnar, well dressed;
~ beð, rekkju, to make a bed;
~ öndvegi, hús, to make a high seat, adorn a house (for a feast);
öll umgjörðin var búin gulli ok silfri, adorned (mounted) with gold and silver;
vápn búit mjök, much ornamented;

with preps.:
~ af e-u, to lose;
láta e-n af baugum ~, to let him be deprived of his riches;
~ at e-u, to treat, = ~ e-u (cf. 4);
þeir höfðu spurt hvern veg Þórólfr hafði búit at herbergjum þeirra, how Th. had treated their premises;
~ e-t fyrir, to prepare (þeir hlutir, er guð hefir fyrir búit sínum ástvinum);
~ fyrir, to be present (hann ætlar, at Selþórir muni fyrir ~ í hverju holti);
~ hjá konu, to lie with a woman;
~ í e-u, to be at the bottom of, = ~ undir e-u (en í þessu vináttumerki bjuggu enn fleiri hlutir);
~ með e-m or e-rri, to cohabit with;
~ með konu, to lie with;
~ saman, to live together (as husband and wife, as friends); to have a common household (ef menn ~ saman);
~ e-t til, to prepare, take the preparatory steps in a case (~ sök, mál, vígsmál til, cf. 1);
~ til veizlu, to prepare for a feast;
~ til seyðis, to get the fire ready for cooking;
~ til vetrsetu, to make preparations for a winter abode;
~ um e-n, to make one’s bed (var búit um þá Þórodd á seti ok lögðust þeir til svefns);
Þórólfr lét setja upp skip sitt ok um ~, he had his ship laid up and fenced round;
kváðu nú Guðrúnu eiga at ~ um rauða skör Bolla, said that G. would have to dress B.’s (her husband’s) bloody head;
~ um andvirki, to fence and thatch hayricks;
at ~ svá um, at aldri mátti vökna, to pack it up so that it could not get wet;
~ svá um, at (with subj.), to arrange it so, that;
~ eigi um heilt við e-n, to be plotting something against one;
~ um nökkurn skoll, to brood over some mischief (deceit);
~ um grun, to be suspicious;
~ um hverfan hug, to be fickleminded;
gott er um öruggt at ~, to be in a safe position;
~ undir e-u, to be subject to, suffer, endure (hart mun þykkja undir at ~);
eiga undir slíkum ofsa at ~, to have to put up with such insolence; to be the (hidden) reason of, to be at the bottom of (þat bjó þar undir, at hann vildi taka ríkit undir sik);
þér vitið gørst, hvat yðr býr undir (what reason you have) at girnast eina útlenda mey;
~ e-n veg við e-n, to behave or act so and so towards one;
sárt býr þú nú við mik, Þóra, thou treatest me sorely;
~ við e-t, to enjoy (þú býr við eilífa ást ok bíðr eilífra ömbuna); to submit to, put up with;
ok mun eigi við þat mega ~, it will be too hard to bide;
~ yfir e-u, to hide, conceal;
framhlutr ormsins býr yfir eitri, is venomous;
lítill búkr býr yfir miklu viti, little body holds mickle wit;
~ yfir brögðum, flærð ok vélum, to brood over tricks, falsehood, and deceit;

 to make oneself ready, get ready (bjuggust þeir at riða austr);
~ ferðar sinnar, to make oneself ready for a journey; = ~ at fara (~ ór Þrándheimi; ~ út til Íslands, ~ á land upp);
bjuggust þeir fóstbrœðr í hernað, they prepared to go on a freebooting trip;
~ til bardaga, to make oneself ready for a battle;
~ fyrir, to have one’s own household (þat þing skal sœkja bóndi hverr, er býst fyrir ok bóndanafn berr);
~ um (= búa um sik), to make one’s own bed, to make oneself comfortable, to encamp (var hörð veðrátta, svá at ekki mátti úti um ~);
~ við e-u, to prepare for a thing (~ við veizlu, bóði);
~ (vel, kristiliga) við dauða sínum, to prepare for one’s death;
~ við vetri, to provide for the winter;
absol., ~ við, to make preparations (nú ríða hér úvinir þínir at þér; skaltu svá við ~, get ready to meet them).

ready, willing;
margir munu búnir at kaupa, ready, willing to buy;
engir munu sínu sik búnari (more willing) til liðveizlu;
with gen., ~ þeirrar ferðar, ready to make that journey;

svá búit, as matters stand, thus;
eigi má hlýða svá ~, it will not do thus, something else must be done;
stendr nú svá ~ um hríð, matters stand thus (unchanged) for a while;
þeir segja Eyjólfi til svá búins, they tell Eyolf the present state of things;
þér skulut ganga með vápnum, en berjast eigi svá búit, not fight as yet;
at svá búnu, as matters stand (hann kvaðst ekki fýsast til Íslands at svá búnu); at present, as yet (þenna draum segjum vér engum at svá búnu);
við svo búit, thus, things being so (þeir skildu við svá búit).

(-jar, -ir),  fair wind;
e-m gefr vel byri (acc. pl.), one gets a fair wind;
bíða byrjar, liggja til byrjar, to wait, lie by, for a fair wind;
~ rennr á, a fair breeze begins to blow;
~ tekr at vaxa, the wind freshens.

(pl. -ir),  f. request, prayer;
er sú ~ allra vár, at, we all beg, that;
skaltu veita mér ~ þá (grant me the request), er ek mun biðja þik;
fella ~ at e-m, til e-s, to entreat one;
gera e-t at ~ e-s, at his entreaty or prayer;
vera (liggja) í ~um, to be at prayers;
biðja ~ sinni, ~ar sinnar til guðs, to pray to God;
góðr ~a, ready or willing to grant a request (konungr var góðr ~a).

to better, improve (ár þær, sem mikit ~ landit);
~ ráð sitt (to better one’s condition) ok biðja konu;
~ aptr, to restore;
~ at e-u, to repair;
~ upp borg, to repair it;

to make up for, compensate;
ef þér vilit eigi ~ (make up for) þat er þér hafit brotit;
~ glœp sinn syndir sínar, to atone for one’s crime, sins;
~ e-m e-t, to compensate one for a thing (= ~ e-t við e-n);
Styrr vá mörg víg, en bœtti engi (viz. víg), S. slew many men, but paid for none;
~ mann fé (dat.), to pay weregild for one slain (Hrafnkell bœtti engan mann fé);
~ sál sína, to devote one’s efforts to the saving of one’s soul;
~ um e-t, to improve a thing;
ekki bœtist um, matters grow worse;
~ yfir e-t, to make good again, redress;

to heal, restore to health;
guð bœtti honum af þessi sótt, God healed him of his disease; with gen. of the disease;
~ e-m sinnar vanheilsu, to restore one to health;
refl., e-m bœtist, one gets better, is restored to health; with gen. of the disease (bœttist Búa augnaverkjarins).

day;
at kveldi skal dag leyfa, at eventide shall the day be praised;
dagr kemr upp í austri, sezt í vestri, the day rises in the east, sets in the west;
öndverðr ~, the early day, forenoon;
miðr ~, midday;
hallandi ~, declining day;
at kveldi dags, síð dags, late in the day;
sannr sem ~, true as day;
í dag, today;
á (or um) daginn, during the day;
sama dags, the same day;
annan dag, the next day;
annars dag, another day;
hindra dags, the day after, tomorrow;
dag frá degi, hvern dag frá öðrum, from day to day;
dag eptir dag, day after day;
nótt ok dag, night and day;
dögunum optar, more times than there are days, over and over again;
á deyjanda degi, on one’s death-day;

esp. pl., lifetime;
á dögum e-s, um daga e-s, in the days of, during or in the reign of;
eptir minn dag, when I am dead (gaf honum alla sína eign eptir sinn dag);
mátti hann eigi lengr gefa en um sína dagi, than for his lifetime;
ráða (taka) e-n af dögum, to put to death.

 death;
taka dauða, to meet one’s death, die;
~ ferr á e-n, one is surprised by death;
draga e-m til dauða, to cause one’s death;
sá vegr, er til dauða dregr, that leads to death;
liggja fyrir dauðanum, to be dying or on the point of death.

dead;
verða, vera, ~, to become dead, die;
falla niðr ~, to drop down dead;
ganga ~, to reappear (of ghosts), = ganga aptr;

to divide into parts;
sú á, er deilir með jötna sonum grund ok með goðum, that river which parts the giants and the gods;
alit þat land, er vatnsföll ~ til sjófar, of which the rivers form the boundaries down to the sea;
vildi H. bæði kjósa ok ~, H. would both choose and deal (viz. divide the catch in shares and choose for himself the share he liked best);
láta en kjósa ok ~, to give one an arbitrary power in a case;
with dat. (hversu má keisarinn ~ sér í tvá staði);

to deal out, apportion, allot;
deildr hlutr, a share allotted to one;
~ dögurð, mat á málum, to deal out portions of food in a household;
~ víg með verum, to deal victory fairly among men;

to distinguish, discern, = greina;
eptir þat sá sól ok mátti þá ~ ættir, they could then discern the quarters of heaven;
~ liti, to discern colours;
eigi deilir litr kosti (acc. pl.), colour is no sure test of the quality;

to busy or occupy oneself with, deal with (engi maðr á önnur mál at ~ í kirkju, nema biðja fyrir sér);
hann við Ríg rúnar deildi, he capped ritnes (spells) with R.;
~ orðspeki við e-n, to contend in learning with one;
þótt hringbrotar heiptir deili, though men hate one another;
~ kníf ok kjötstykki, to share knife and meat;

~ við e-n, to quarrel with one (~ við heimska hali);
deili gröm við þik, may the fiends bandy words with thee;
~ um e-t, to quarrel, contest about;
þeir deildu um (they have a lawsuit about) jarðir;
~ á e-n, to contend against one;
~ illyrðum, illdeildum, to chide, abuse one another;
~ afli, ofríki, við e-n, to deal harshly and overbearingly with one;
impers., ef í þat deilir, if there be dissent on that point;
ef í deilir með þeim, if they disagree;

dole, share;
fara at ~um, to be parcelled out;
fá illt ór ~um, to get a bad share, be worsted;
í nökkuri ~, partly;
í aðra ~, í þriðju ~, secondly, thirdly;

(dett; datt, duttum; dottinn),  to drop, fall;
~ niðr dauðr, to drop down dead;
duttu þær ofan, they tumbled down;
sauðfénaðr datt niðr unnvörpum (dropped down, died suddenly, in heaps) í megrð;
spjót dettr ór hendi e-m, the spear drops out of one’s hand;
dauðinn dettr á, comes on suddenly;
datt norðanveðrit í logn, the north wind fell altogether;
láta e-t ~ niðr, to let a matter drop.

to draw, drag, pull;
~ heim viðinn, to drag the logs home;
~ árar, to pull the oars;
absol., drógu þeir skjótt eptir, they soon pulled up to them;
~ boga, to draw the bow;
~ segl, to hoist sails (= ~ upp segl);
~ fisk, to catch, pull up fish with a line;
~ kvernstein, to turn the millstone, to grind;
við ramman mun reip at ~, it will be pulling a rope against a strong man, i. e. it will be a difficult task;

with preps.:
~ föt, skóklædi af e-m, to pull off one’s clothes, shoes;
~ hring af hendi sér, to take off a ring from one’s hand;
dró hann þá grunninu, he pulled them off the shallow;
~ e-t af e-u, to draw, derive from a source;
~ e-t af, to take off (Þ. hafði látit af ~ brúna);
~ e-t af við e-n, to keep back, withhold, from one;
man héðan af eigi af dregit við oss, henceforth we shall no be neglected, stinted;
Egill dró at sér skipit, E. pulled the ship close up to himself;
~ vél at e-m, to draw wiles around one;
~ spott, skaup, at e-u, to hold a thing up to ridicule;
~ at lið, föng, to collect troops, stores;
dró at honum sóttin, the illness drew closer to him, he grew worse;
impers., dró at mætti hans, dró at um matt hans, his strength declined (fell off);
til þess er dró at degi, till the day drew near;
þá er dregr at jólum, when Yule drew near;
dró at því (the time drew near). at hann væri banvænn;
tók þá at ~ fast at heyjum hans, his stock of hay was rapidly diminishing;
svá dregr at mér af elli, svengd, þorsta, I am so overcome by old age, hunger, thirst;
nú þykki mér sem fast dragi at þér, that thou art sinking fast;
~ hring á hönd sér, to put a ring on one’s hand;
~ (grun) á e-t, to suspect;
~ á vetr, to rear through the winter (Hrafnkell dró á vetr kálf ok kið);
impers., dregr á tunglit, the moon is obscured (= dregr myrkr á tunglit);
dimmu þykkir ~ á ráðit Odds, it looks as if a cloud was drawing over Odds’ affairs;
dregr á gleði biskups, the bishop’s gladness was obscured;
~ eptir e-m, to gain on one (Þórarinn sótti ákaft róðrinn ok hans menn, ok drógu skjótt eptir þeim Steinólfi ok Kjallaki);
~ eptir e-m um e-t, to approach one, to be nearly equal to one, in a thing;
um margar íþróttir (in many accomplishments) dró hann fast eptir Ólafi konungi;
~ e-t fram, to produce, bring forward (~ fram athugasamlig dœmi); to further, promote (~ fram hlut e-s);
~ fram kaupeyri sinn, to make money;
~ fram skip, to launch a ship;
impers., dregr frá, (cloud darkness) is drawn off;
hratt stundum fyrir, en stundum dró frá, (clouds) drew sometimes over, sometimes off;
dregr fyrir sól, tungl, the sun, moon is obscured by clouds or eclipse (tunglskin var ljóst, en stundum dró fyrir);
ok er í tók at ~ skúrirnar, when showers began to gather;
~ e-ð saman, to collect, gather (~ lið, her, skip saman);
impers., saman dró kaupmála með þeim, they struck a bargain;
saman dró hugi þeirra, their hearts were drawn together;
dregr þá saman or dregr saman með þeim, the distance between them grows less;
~ e-t í sundr, to draw asunder, disjoin (vil ek eigi ~ í sundr sættir yðrar);
impers., dregr þá í sundr or dregr í sundr með þeim, the distance between them increases;
~ e-n til e-s, to move, prompt, induce;
engi ofkæti dregr mik til þessarar ferðar, it is not from wantonness that I undertake this journey;
slíkt dregr hann til vinsældar, this furthers his popularity;
ef hann drógi ekki til, if he was not concerned;
~ e-t til dœmis um e-t, to adduce as a proof of;
hann hét at ~ allt til sætta (to do everything in his power for reconciliation) með þeim Skota konungi;
impers., nema til verra dragi, unless matters turn out for the worse;
with dat., þat samband þeirra, er þeim dregr báðum til bana, which will prove fatal to both of them;
at hér mundi til mikillar úgiptu ~ um kaup þessi, that much mischief would arise from this bargain;
dró þá enn til sundrþykkju með þeim Svíum, the old feud with the Swedes began all over again;
svá er þat, segir R., ef ekki dregr til, unless some unforesceen thing happens;
~ e-t undan e-m, to seek to deprive one of a thing (þeir hafa bundizt í því at ~ bœndr undan þér);
~ e-t undan, to delay (drógu Skotar undan sættina);
hví dregr þú undan at bjóða mér til þín? why dost thou put off inviting me to come?;
~ rót undan (tölu), to extract the root;
~ undan e-m, to escape from one (nú lægir seglin þeirra ok ~ þeir undan oss);
impers., hann (acc.) dró undan sem nauðuligast, he had a narrow escape;
lítt dró enn undan við þik, there was little chance of drawing out of thy reach;
~ e-t undir sik, to apropriate or take fraudulently to oneself (hafði dregit undir sik finnskattinn);
impers., dró yðr (acc.) undir hrakningina, en oss (acc.) undan, you came in for hard uasge but we escaped;
~ upp skip, to drag a ship ashore;
~ upp segl, to hoist a sail (sails);
~ upp fisk, to pull up fish with a line;
impers., þoku dregr upp, fog is coming on;

 to draw oneself, move;
~ aptr á leið, to remain behind;
~ á hendr e-m, to gather around one;
~ á e-t, to give a partial promise (Þorvarðr dróst á at ljá Sturlu sverðit, ok fórst þat fyrir);
~ á legg, to grow up;
þegar honum dróst aldr, when her grew up;
e-m ~ penningar, one maks money;
herr, lið, dregst e-m, troops gather round one;
~ vel, illa, to do well, ill;
dróst þá liðit mjök af kulda, the host suffered much from cold;
þau drógust (they pulled against each other, fought) um einn gullhring;
Sigvaldi dregst út frá flotanum, S. drains away from the fleet;
~ við e-t, to become discouraged.

to drink (~ mjöð, öl, mungát);
~ full, minni, horn, to drink a toast, cup, horn;
~ drykk, to drink a draught (þú skalt ~ af tvá drykki);
~ brjóst spena, to suck;
~ úmælt, without measure;
~ fast (mjök), to drink hard;
~ e-n af stokki, to drink one under the table;
~ sér lítit vit, ~ frá sér vit ilit, to drink away one’s reason;

with preps.:
~ af keri, to drink out of a vessel (drukku þeir af einu silfrkeri);
to drink off (empty) a vessel, cup (hann tók við horninu ok drakk af);
~ á e-n, to drink to a person;
refl., ~st á, to drink to one another;
impers., drekkr á e-n, one ships a sea;
~ til e-s = ~ á e-n;
~ e-t út, to consume or spend in drinking.

to stike, beat, knock;
~ e-n vendi, to strike one with a rod;
hann tók hörpu sína ok drap strengi (struck the strings) til sláttar; ~ járn, to hammer iron;
~ or ~ högg á dyrr, to knock at the door;
~ botn úr keraldi, to knock the bottom out of a tub;
at eigi drepir þú mik í dúp, that you knock me not into the deep;
~ í hel, í dauða, til heljar, to smite to death;

with prep., ~ af, to kill, slaughter (cattle);
~ niðr, to kill off (þótt hirðmenn þínir sé drepnir niðr sem svn);
~ sik ór dróma, to get rid of (throw off) a fetter;
~ til e-s, to strike, hit, at one;
~ e-t undir sik, to knock or drag down (skaltu standa hiá, er fjándi sá drepr mik undir sik);
~ upp eld = ~ eld;
~ e-t út, to divulge a thing;
~ yfir e-t, to hide, suppress;
drap hann brátt yfir (he soon mastered) harm sinn;

impers., drepr honuin aldregi ský (acc.) í augu, his eyes never get clouded;
ofrkappit (acc.) drepr fyrir þeim (their high spirits break down), þegar haminjan brestr; drap þó heldr í fyrir honum, he rather grew worse, his eyes grew weaker;
nú drepr ór hljóð (acc.) fyrst ór konunginum, the king became silent at once;
þá drap stall ór hjarta hans, his heart failed;
ofan drap flaugina, the vane was knocked down;
regn (acc.) drepr í gegnum et, the rain beats through (the thatch);

to put, thrust;
hendi drap á kampa, he put his hand to his beard;
~ fœti (fótum) í eð, to strike (knock) one’s foot against, stumble over (drap fótunum í þrøskuldinn ok lá fallinn);
~ höfði, to droop (with) the head (Egill drap höfðin niðr í feld sinn);
~ fingri í munn sér, to put the finger into the mouth;
~ hendi til es, við em, to give one a slap with the hand;
~ hendi við e-u, to wave away with the hand to refuse a kind offer (~ hendi við boðnu gulli);

to do, suffice;
þat er þér man ~, which will do for thee;
hefir oss þó dugat þessi átrúnaðr, this faith has served us very well;
fátt er svá illt at einugi dugi, few things are utterly useless;
mun þér eigi þat ~ at sofa hér, it will not do (is not safe) for thee to sleep here;

to keep (one) in ignorance of a thing;
~ e-n e-s (eigi skalt þú þat gera at ~ þik sjálfan sanninda);
~ e-s, to conceal, deny, disavow, Þórir dylr þess ekki, Thor does not deny it;
Eysteinn duldi þeirra orða fyrir sik, E. professed that he had never said any stuch thing;
allir duldu, at né eitt vissi til Hrapps, all pretended ignorance about Hrapp;
vera, ganga, duldr (duliðr, dulinn) e-s, to be unaware of, to be kept in ignorance of (hefir hon verit alls þessa duld; veit engi ætt mína ok ganga þess allir duldir);
dulið (dulin) ertu, Hyndla, H., thou art mistaken (deceived);
duliðr (dulinn) at e-m, mistaken about one;

refl., ~st, to conceal (hide) oneself;
at þat sé flugumenn ok vili ~st (disguise themselves) undir munka búnaði;
~st í e-u, to conceal a thing from oneself, be ignorant of = ganga duldr e-s (Eiríkr konungr þarf nú ekki at ~ í því, at);
~st við e-t, to refuse to acknowledge, to ignore;
trúit þessu eigi, meðan þér megit ~st, as long as you can ignore it, till you get full evidence;
en Sveinn duldist við þat, but S. shrank from believing it.

to gush, shower, pour;
blóð dynr ór sárum es, blood gushes out of his wounds;
dundi ákaft regn ór lopti, the rain poured down in streams;
dundu á þá (or þeim) vápnin, spjótin, the weapons (spears) showered upon them.

to found, raise (~ stað, kirkju);
~ her (lið) á hendr e-m, to raise troops against one;
~ e-n, to support, aid (efldi Dofri hann síðan til ríkis í Noregi);
~ e-n til rangs máls, to help one in an unjust cause;

 to grow strong (hann fann, at mótstöðumenn hans efldust);
~ at her, to gather, raise troops;
~ til ríkis, to win a kingdom (by force of arms);
poet., ~ við e-n, to marry into one’s family, = mægjast.

upper, opp. to neðri;
bera efra skjöld, to gain the victory;
verða e-m ~, to be superior to;
hit efra,
a) high above ground (fara hit efra í trjám sem apynjur eða íkornar);
b) the upper or inland road, opp. to ‘hit ytra’, along the shore (sumir fóru hit efra til Þríhyrningshálsa);
by land, opp. to the sea;
c) of the inner part of a building, opposed to ‘hit fremra’ or the part nearest the door (konungr settist í hásæti ok alskipat var hit efra ok hit fremra);

to have (~ börn, föður, móður, vin);
~ konu, to have her for wife;
hann átti Gró, he was married to G.;
hann gekk at ~ Þóru, he took Th. for his wife, he married Th.;
enga vil ek þessa ~, I will not marry any of these;
~ heima, to have a home, to live (þeir áttu heima austr í Mörk);
~ sér e-t = ~ e-t (Höskuldr átti sér dóttur, er Hallgerðr hét);
~ ván e-s, to have hope of a thing, to reckon upon;
~ hlut at or í e-u, to have a share in a thing, to be concerned in;
~ vald á e-u, to have within one’s power;

to be under obligation, be obliged, have to do a thing;
tólf menn, þeir er fylgð áttu með konungi, who were bound to attend the king’s person;
á ek þar fyrir at sjá, I am bound (I have) to see to that;
átti Hrútr för í Vestfjorðu, H. had to go to the V.;

to keep, hold;
~ fund, þing, samkvámu, stefnu, to hold a meeting;
~ kaupstefnu, to hod a market;
~ orrustu við e-n, to fight a battle with one;
~ högg við e-n, to exchange blows with one;
~ illt við e-n, to quarrel with;
~ tal (or mál) við e-n, to speak, converse with one;

with preps.:
~ e-t at e-m, to have something due from one, to expect from one (þat vil ek ~ at þér, at þú segir mér frá ferð þinni);
to deserve from one (ok á ek annat at þér);
þeir er mikit þóttust at sér ~, had much in their power;
~ e-t eptir, to have to do yet, to have left undone (þat áttu eptir, er erfiðast er, en þat er at deyja);
to leave behind one (andaðist ok átti eptir tvá sonu vaxna);
~ e-t saman, to own in common;
~ skap saman, to agree well, be of one mind;
eigi veit ek, hvárt við eigum heill saman, whether we shall live happy together;
~ saman, to quarrel, = ~ deild saman;
~ um við e-n, to have to deal with (við brögðótta áttu nú um);
þar sem við vini mína er um at ~, where my friends are concerned;
~ e-t undir e-m, to have in another’s hands;
Njáll átti mikit fé undir Starkaði ok í Sandgili, N. had much money out at interest with St. and at Sandgil, er sá eigi vel staddr, er líf sitt á undir þinum trúnaði, whose life depends on thy good faith;
~ mikit (lítit) undir sér, to have much (little) in one’s power;
far þú við marga menn, svá at þú eigir allt undir þér, that the whole matter rests in thy own hands;
hann sá, at hann átti ekki undir sér, that he had no influence;
~ við e-n, to have to do with, fight with (brátt fundu þeir, at þeir áttu þar ekki við sinn maka);
ekki á ek þetta við þik, this is no business between thee and me;
~ gott (illt) við e-n, to be on good (bad) terms with one;
~ við konu, to have intercourse with, = ~ lag (samræði) við konu;
recipr., ~st við, to deal with one another; fight, quarrel;
~st við deildir, to be engaged in strife;
áttust þeir höggvaskipti við, they exchanged blows with one another.

the same, one and the same (váru sveinarnir up fœddir báðir í einu þorpi);
allt í eina leið, all in one way;
~ … ok, the same as (í einu herbergi ok hinn);
allr ~, the very same, quite the same (þat er allt eitt ok himinn);
allt at einu, nevertheless, for all that (þó at þú þjónaðir illum, þó var hann allt at einu þinn herra);

indef. one, a certain (~ vetr, ~ dag, eitt kveld);
~ vinr Þóris, a certain friend of Th.;
before numbers, about, some;
einar fimm þúsundir, some five thousand;
einir … aðrir, some … others (einir tóku dúka ok aðrir rekkjublæjur);
~ ok ýmiss, one and another (einar ok ýmissar þjóðir);

gen. pl. ‘~a’ used in an intensive sense;
~a manna bezt, best of all (single) men;
~a verst, by far the worst;
~a sízt, by far the least, least of all;
engi er ~a hvatastr, no man is superior to all others;

alone (Guðrún skyldi ein ráða fyrir fé þeirra);
láta konu eina, to desert or divorce one’s wife;
with gen., hann varð ~ sinna manna, he was separated from his men;
if put after the noun ‘~’ generally denotes only, but;
segja þetta prett ~, to call this a mere trick;
vín eitt, wine only;
var þat (handklæði) raufar einar, all in holes, mere tatters;
fáir einir, only a few;
~ sér or sér ~, quite by oneself, alone (hann var ~ sér);
~ saman, ~ samt, quite alone;
kona eigi ein saman, not alone, with child;
at eins, only, but;
eigi at eins, not only;
því at eins, only in that case;
údauðr at eins, merely not dead, all but dead, barely alive;
at einu = at eins.

some, some one;
einnhvern dag, some day;
eitthvert sinn, einhverju sinni, sometime, once;
as a noun (meðan ~ várr búandanna er lífs);

refl., ~st, to end, come to an end (reiði mannsins endist á einu augabragði);
to last, hold out (berjast meðan dagrinn endist);
meðan mér ~st föng til, as long as my provisions last;
ef honum endist aldr til, if he lives so long;
meðan mér ~st lífdagar, meðan ek endumst, as long as I live;
to turn out, to end (well or ill), to do (enda mun þat fám bóndum vel ~st at synja mér mægðar).

denoting the aim and object of many verbs;
leita, spyrja, frétta, ~ e-u, to search, ask, inquire after;
líta ~ e-u, to look afler, attend to;
bíða ~ e-u, to wait for;
vaka ~ e-m, to sit up waiting for one;
segja ~ e-m, to report behind one’s back;

following the course of a track, road, etc., along;
niðr ~ hálsinum, down the hill;
~ endilongu, from one end to the other;
~ miðju, along the middle;

of time, after, in succession to (vár kom ~ vetr);
hvern dag ~ annan, one day after the other;
ár ~ ár, dag ~ dag, year by year, day by day;
~ þat, after that, thereafter;

denoting succession, inheritance;
taka e-t í arf ~ e-n, to inherit from one;
hann tók konungdóm ~ föður sinn, after his father;
vita þá skömm ~ sik, to leave such a bad report;
skaði mikill er ~ menn slíka, there is a great loss in such men;

after;
annat sumar ~, the second summer after;
um daginn ~, the day after;
~ um várit, later during the spring;
~ koma úsvinnum ráð, the fool is wise when too late;

behind;
bíða sitja ~, to wait, stay behind;
vera, standa ~, to remain behind, be left;
halda e-u ~, to keep back;
skammt get ek ~ þinnar æfi, I guess that little is left of thy life;

Mörðr hét maðr, er (nom.) kallaðr var gígja;
grös fögr, er (acc.) hón hafði í hendi;
aðra hluti þá, er (gen.) menn vildu visir verða;
þann einn son, er (dat.) hann ann lítit;

a personal or demonstr. pron. may be added to the rel. part., er þú, er þik; er hann, er hón, er hana, er hans, er hennar, er þeim, er þeiri, er þeira, etc.;
œrr ertu, Loki, er þú (who) yðra telr ljóta leiðstafi;
sá maðr, er hann vill, that man who wishes;
nema ein Goðrún, er hón æva grét, who never wept;
ekkja heitir sú, er búandi hennar (whose husband) varð sóttdauðr;
þann konung, er undir honum eru skatt-konungar, that king under whom are tributary kings;

of time, er, þá er, when;
ok er, and when;
en er, but when;
þar til er, until;
í því er, just when;
eptir (þat) er, when;
þegar er, as soon as (þegar er lýsti, stóð konungr upp);
síðan er, since;
meðan er, while;
næst er vér kómum, next when we came;
þá lét í hamrinum, sem er reið gengr, as when it thunders;

= at, that;
ok fannst þat á öllu, er hón þóttist vargefin, that she thought she was thrown away;
ek em þess sæll, er okkart félag sleit, I am happy that;
skyldi fara fyrst leyniliga, en þó kom þar, er allir vissu, but it came to this, that every one knew of it.

errand, message, mission, business;
eiga ~ við e-n, hafa ~ til e-s, to have business with one;
reka ~, to do an errand;
þess ~s, to that purpose;

to fall;
eigi fellr tré við fyrsta högg, a tree falls not with the first stroke;
~ af baki, to fall from horse back;
~ á kné, to fall on one’s knees;
~ áfram (á bak aptr), to fall forwards (backwards);
~ flatr, to fall prostrate;
~ til jarðar, to fall to the ground;
refl., láta ~st (= sik ~), to let oneself fall (þá lét Loki ~ í kné Skaða);

to flow, run (of water, stream, tide);
særinn fell út frá landi, ebbed;
féll sjór fyrir hellismunnann, the sea rose higher than the cave-mouth;
síðan féll sjór at, the tide rose;
þeir sá þá ós mikinn ~ í sjóinn, fall into the sea;
á fél (a river flowed) við skála Ásólfs;
var skipit svá hlaðit, at inn féll um söxin, that the sea rushed in at the prow;

to fail, be foiled;
sá eiðr fellr honum til útlegðar, if he fails in taking the oath, he shall be liable to outlawry;
~ á verkum sínum, to have been caught red-handed, to be justly slain;
~ or ~st at máli, sókn, to fail in one’s suit;
~ frá máli, to give it up;
fallinn at frændum, bereft of kinsmen;
dœmi ek fyrir dráp hans fallnar yðrar eignir, I sentence your estates to be forfieited for his slaughter;
refl., ef gerðarmenn láta ~st, if the umpires fail to do their duty;
þá fallust öllum Ásum orðtök ok svá hendr, then voice and hands alike failed the Gods;
féllust þeim allar kvéðjur, their greetings died on their lips;
vill sá eigi falust láta andsvör, he will not fail or falter in replying;

with adv., e-m fellr e-t þungt, létt, a thing falls heavily, lightly upon one (þetta mun ðr þungt ~);
féll þá keisaranum þyngra bardaginn, the battle turned against the emperor;
e-m fellr e-t nær, it falls nigh to one, touches one nearly;
henni féll meinit svá nær, at, the illness fell on her so sore, that;
mér fellr eigi firr en honum, it touches me no less than him;
hörmuliga fellr oss nú, at, it falls out sadly for us, that;

to please, suit;
kvað sér, þat vel ~ til attekta, said that it suited him well for drawing revenue from;
honum féll vel í eyru lofsorð konungs, the king’s praise was pleasant in his ears;
jarli féllst þat vel í eyru, the earl was well pleased to hear it;
mun mér illa ~, ef, it will displease me, if;
féll vel á með þeim, they were on good terms;
refl., honum féllst þat vel í skap, it suited his mind well, he was pleased with it;
féllst hvárt öðru vel í geð, they loved each other;

with preps. and advs.,
~ af, to fall, abate (féll af vindr, byrr);
~ á e-n, to befall one;
þær féllu lyktir í, at, the end was, that;
~ í e-t, to fall into;
~ í brot, to fall in a fit;
~ í óvit, to faint, swoon;
~ í villu, to fall into heresy;
~ í vald e-s, to fall into one’s power;
féll veðrit í logn, the storm calmed down;
~ niðr, to fall, drop;
mitt kvæði mun skjótt niðr ~, my poem will soon be forgotten;
féll svá niðr þeirra tal, their conversation dropped, they left off talking;
~ saman, to fill in with, agree;
þó at eigi félli alit saman með þeim, though they, did not agree in everything;
~ til, to occur, happen, fall out;
ef auðna fellr til, if luck will have it so;
litlu síðar féll til fagrt leiði, fair wind came on;
öll þingviti, er til ~, all the fines that may fall in, be due;
nema þörf falli til, unless need be;
sem sakir ~ til, as the case falls;
~ undir e-n, to fall to one’s lot (of inheritance, obligation);
arfr fellr undir e-n, devolves upon one;
~ út, to recede, of the tide (þá er út féll sjórinn);
~ við árar, to fall to at the oars.

the space between the arms, the breast and arms;
kom spjótit í ~ honum, the spear pierced his breast;
reka í ~ e-m, to throw in one’s face;
hafa e-t í ~i sér, to hold in one’s arms, to have in one’s power;
taka í ~ sér, to take into one’s arms (tók manninn í ~ sér ok bar út);
fœrast e-t í ~, to undertake a thing, take upon oneself;
fœrast e-t ór ~i, to throw off, refuse;

pl. means, opportunily;
engi föng eru önnur á, there is no other choice;
hafa föng á e-u, to be enabled to do a thing (höfðu eigi föng á at reka langt flóttann);
af (eptir) föngum, to the best of one’s power, according to one’s means.

to move, pass along, go;
gekk hann hvargi sem hann fór, he walked wherever he went;
~ heim (heiman), to go home (from home);
~ á fund e-s to visit one;
fjöld ek fór, I travelled much;
hann sagði, hversu orð fóru með þeim, what words passed between them;
absol., to go begging (ómagar, er þar eigu at ~ í því þingi);

with ‘ferð, leið’ or the like added in acc., gen., or dat.;
~ leiðar sinnar, to go one’s way, proceed on one’s journey (= ~ ferðar sinnar or ferða sinna, ~ ferð sina, ~ för sina, förum sínum);
~ þessa ferð, to make this journey;
~ fullum dagleiðum, to travel a full days journeys;
~ stefnuför, to go a-summoning;
~ bónorðsför, to go a-wooing;
~ sigrför, to go on the path of victory, to triumph;
~ góða för, to make a lucky journey;
fig., ~ ósigr, to be defeated;
~ mikinn skaða, to suffer great damage;
~ hneykju, skömm, to incur disgrace;
~ erendleysu, to fail in one’s errand;
with the road in acc. (~ fjöll ok dala);

with infin.;
~ sofa, to go to sleep (allir menn vóru sofa farnir);
~ vega, to go to fight;
~ leita, to go seeking (var leita farit);

with an a., etc.;
~ villr, to go astray;
~ haltr, to walk lame;
~ vanstiltr, to go out of one’s mind;
~ duldr e-s, to be unaware of;
~ andvígr e-m, to give battle;
~ leyniliga (leynt), to be kept secret;
eigi má þetta svá ~, this cannot go on in that way;
fjarri ferr þat, far from it, by no means;
fór þat fjarri, at ek vilda, I was far from desiring it;

to turn out, end;
fór þat sem líkligt var, it turned out as was likely (viz. ended ill);
svá fór, at, the end was, that;
ef svá ferr sem ek get til, if it turns out as I guess;
á sómu leið fór um aðra sendimenn, it went the same way with the other messengers;

to put an end to, destroy;
~ sér (sjálfr), to kill oneself;
~ lífi (fjörvi) e-s, to deprive one of life;
þú hefir sigr vegit ok Fáfni (dat.) um farit, killed F.;

with preps. and advs.:
~ af klæðum, to take off one’s clothes;
~ at e-m, to make an attack upon, to assault (eigi mundi í annat sinn vænna at ~ at jarlinum);
~ at e-u, to mind, pay heed to;
ekki fer ek at, þótt þú hafir svelt þik til fjár (it does not matter to me, I do not care, though);
to deal with a thing, proceed in a certain way;
svá skal at sókn ~, thus is the pleading to be proceeded with;
~ at lögum, úlögum, to proceed lawfully, unlawfully;
~ mjúkliga at, to proceed gently;
hér skulu við ~ at með ráðum, act with, deliberation;
impers. with dat., to do, behave;
illa hefir mér at farit, I have done my business badly; to go in pusuit (search) of (víkingar nökkurir þeir sem fóru at féföngum);
~ at fuglaveiðum, to go a-fowling;
~ at fé, to tend sheep;
~ á e-n, to come upon one;
sigu saman augu, þá er dauðinn fór á, when death seized him;
~ á hæl or hæli, to step back, retreat;
~ eptir e-m, to follow one;
~ eptir e-u, to go for, go to fetch (Snorri goði fór eptir líkinu; ~ eptir vatni); to accommodate oneself to, conform to (engi vildi eptir öðrum ~);
þau orð er eptir ~, the following words;
~ fram, to go on, take place;
ef eigi ferr gjald fram, if no payment takes place;
veizlan ferr vel fram, the feast went on well;
spyrr, hvat þar fœri fram, he asked, what was going on there;
~ fram ráðum e-s, to follow one’s advice;
allt mun þat sínu fram ~, it will take its own course;
kváðu þat engu gegna ok fóru sínu fram, took their own way;
segir honum, hversu þeir fóru fram, how they acted;
~ e-t fram, to do., perform a thing;
spyrr hann, hvat nú sé fram ~nda, what is to be done;
~ fyrir e-t, to pass for, be taken for (fari sá fyrir níðing, er);
~ hjá sér, to be beside oneself;
~ í e-t, to go into (~ í tunnu);
~ í sæng, rekkju, to go to bed;
~ í sess sinn, sæti sitt, to take one’s seat;
~ í klæði, to put on clothes, dress;
~ í vápn, brynju, to put on armour;
~ í lag, to go right or straight again (þá fóru brýnn hans í lag);
~ í vöxt, to increase;
~ í þurð, to wane;
~ í hernað, víking, to go a-freebooting;
nú ferr í úvænt efni, now matters look hopeless;
to happen, occur (alit þat, er í hafði farit um nóttina);
~ með e-t, to wield handle, manage;
fór Hroptr með Gungni, H. wielded (the spear) Gungnir;
~ með goðorð, to hold a goðorð;
~ með sök, to manage a lawsuit;
to practice, deal in;
~ með rán, to deal in robbery;
~ með spott ok háð, to go scoffing and mocking;
~ með galdra ok fjölkyngi, to practice sorcery;
to deal with, treat, handle (þú munt bezt ok hógligast með hann ~);
~ af hljóði með e-t, to keep matters secret;
~ með e-m, to go with one, follow one (ek skal með yðr ~ með allan minn styrk);
~ með e-u, to do (so and so) with a thing, to deal with, manage;
hvernig þeir skyldu ~ með vápnum sínum, what they were to do with their weapons;
sá maðr, er með arfinum ferr, who manages the inheritance;
~ með málum sínum, to manage one’s case;
~ vel með sínum háttum, to bear oneself well;
undarliga ~ munkar þessir með sér, these monks behave strangely;
~ með barni, to go with child;
impers., ferr með þeim heldr fáliga, they are on indifferent terms;
~ ór landi, to leave the country;
~ ór klæðum, fötum, to take off one’s clothes, undress;
~ saman, to go together; to shake, shudder;
fór en forna fold öll saman, shivered all through;
to concur, agree (hversu má þat saman f);
~ til svefns, to go to sleep (= ~ at sofa);
~ um e-t, to travel over (~ um fjall);
~ höndum um e-n, to stroke or touch one with the hands (hann fór höndum um þá, er sjúkir vóru);
~ mörgum orðum um e-t, to dilate upon a subject;
~ myrkt um e-t, to keep a matter dark;
~ undan, to excuse oneself (from doing a thing), to decline, refuse (hvat berr til, at þú ferr undan at gera mér veizluna);
borð ~ upp, the tables are removed;
~ út, to go from Norway to Iceland; to come to a close, run out (fóru svá út þessir fimm vetr);
~ útan, to go abroad (from Iceland);
~ við e-n, to treat one, deal with one in a certain way;
margs á, ek minnast, hve við mik fóruð, I have many things to remember of your dealings with me;
~ yfir e-t, to go through;
nú er yfir farit um landnám, now an account of the settlements has been given;
skjótt yfir at ~, to be brief.

esp. of a journey;
~ vel, illa, to go well, ill;
fórst þeim vel, they got on well;
fórst þeim þá seint um daginn, they got along slowly;
recipr., ~ hjá, to pass one another without meeting, = ~ á mis;
~ í móti, to march against one another;

to perish;
~ af sulti, hita, mœði, to die of hunger, heat, exhaustion;
of a ship, to founder (fórst þar byrðingrinn); to be drowned (alls fórust þar níu menn);
þá er bæði himinn ok jörð hefir farizt, when heaven and earth shall have passed away;
ferst nú vinátta ykkur, your friendship is done with;
~ fyrir, to come to nought (at síðr muni fyrir ~ nökkurt stórræði; fórst þat fyrir).

gone, undone;
þá er farnir vóru forstöðumenn Troju, when the defenders of Troy were dead and gone;
hans tafl var mjök svá farit, his gaine was almost lost;
~ af sulti ok mœði, famished and exhausted;
~ at e-u, destitute of (~ at vistum, lausafé);
tungl farit, a ‘dead’ moon, new moon;
sól var skamt farin, the sun was little advaned, not long risen;
þá var dagr alljós ok sól farin, broad day and the sun high in the sky;

so and so conditioned;
vel andlits ~, vel ~ í andliti, well-favoured;
mjök aldri ~, stricken in years;
vel orði (or orðum) ~, well-spoken, eloquent;
impers., e-m, er e-n veg farit, one is so and so;
er eigi einn veg farit úgæfu okkar, our ill-luck is not of one piece;
veðri var þannig farit, at, the weather was such that;
hversu landinu er farit, what the condition of the country is;
henni er þannig farit, at hón er mikil ey, löng, (viz. the island) is so shaped that it is large and long;
fig., of disposition, character;
er honum vel farit, he is a well-disposed man;
þeim var úlíkt farit at mörgu, they were unlike in many respects;
undarliga er yðr farit, ye are strange men;
adding the prep. til: nú er annan veg til farit, now matters are altered;
nú er svá til farit, at ek vil, now the case is, that I wish;

to get, gain, win;
sá fær er frjár, he that woos wins;
fá fljóðs ást to win a woman’s love;
hann bað konunnar ok fekk heitit hennar, he asked the woman in marriage and got the promise of her hand;
fá sitt eyrindi, to accomplish one’s errand;
fá haærra hlut, to get the better of it;
fá góðar viðtökur, to get a good reception;
fá skilning á e-u, to get knowledge of;

to suffer endure;
fá úsigr, to be defeated;
fá skaða, to suffer harm;
fá úvit, to fall senseless, to faint;
fá líflát, to fall lifeless;
fá bana, to come by one’s death;

to give, deliver to one, put into one’s hands;
fá mér (give me) leppa tvá ór hári þinu;
fáit nú konungi festu (give the king bail) þá er honum líki;
fá e-m sök, to charge one;
var sá sveinn fenginn í hendr okkr, delivered into our hands;
fá e-m e-t at geyma, to give a thing into one’s charge (= fá e-m e-t til geymslu);

with pp. following, to be able to;
fá e-n veiddan, to be able to catch one;
hon fœr með engu móti vakit þá, she could by no means awaken them;
þeir munu mik aldri fá sótt, they will never be able to overcome me;
fengu þeir honum ekki nát, they could not catch him: skaltu hvergi fá undan hokat, thou shall have no chance of sneaking away;
hann fekk þó eigi víss orðit, he could not make out for certain;

with gen., to get, take, gain, win;
þeir fengu fjár mikils, they took a rich booty;
vel er þess fengit, it is well earned, well done;
hann var eigi skáld, ok hann hafði ei þeirrar listar fengit, he had not received that gift: fá verðar, to take a meal;
hann tekk sér sveitar (he raised a band) ok gørðist illvirki;
fá konu, to get a wife, marry (hon var átján vetra, er þorsteinn fekk hennar);

impers., one can get or find;
vápn svá góð, at eigi fær önnur slík, that the like are not to be got;
at varla fái vitrara mann, that a wiser man is hardly to be found;
also, one may or can (do something);
þat skip fær vel varit eldi, that ship can well be guarded against fire;

with preps.:
fá af sér (with infin.) to bring oneself to;
þeir fengu af verra, they got the worse of it;
fá at veizlu, brúkaupi, blóti, to get provisions for a feast (hann fekk at blóti miklu);
sá dagr er at jólum skal fá, the day when preparations are to be made for Yule (cf. atfangadagr);
fá á e-u, to get hold of, grasp with the hand;
faðir Móða fekk á þremi, the father of M. caught hold of the brim;
fá e-n, to touch, affect one, move (opt fá á horskan lostfagrir, litir);
láta e-t á sik fá, to be (deeply) affected by, take it to heart;
drykkr fær á e-n, the drink intoxicates one (er drykkr fekk á Hákon jarl);
fá í e-t, to take hold of, grasp with the hand (= fá á e-u);
forðuðu fingrum, fengu í snœri, they took hold of the strings;
fá e-t or e-s til, to get, procure (var kirkja gör ok kennimanna til fengit);
fá e-n til at gøra e-t, to get one to do a thing;
þeir fengu menn til at ryðja skip sitt, they got men to clear their ship fá til e-s, to lay hold of;
þar var fjöld fjár, fengu til margir, there was wealth of money, and many took a share of it;

refl., fást í e-u, to be busy, exert oneself, engage in a matter (dróttningin mátti þar ekki í fást);
Helgi leitaði þá, ef Sigurðr vildi í fást við Þorvald, if S. would try with Th.;
segir hann ljúga ok fást í rógi, and deal in slander;
fást við e-t = f. í e-u;
f. við e-n, to have to do with, to contend with one (H. segist þá vilja … fást eigi við fjánda þenna);
to wrestle (grapple) with one (skaltu fást við blámann várn).

(fá, fáða, fáðr),  to draw, paint;
fá rúnar, to draw runes or magic characters;
vér höfum fáða unga brúði á vegg, we have painted the young bride on the wall;
gulli fáðr, gilded.

few;
með fá liði við fá menn, with few men;
fáir (= fáir menn), few;
fáir einir, only a few;
í fám orðum, in few, words;
the sing. often instead of pl., esp. in old sayings (~ er fagr, ef grætr; ~ bregðr hinu betra, ef hann veit hit verra);
neut. fátt, few things (fátt veit sá er sefr);
var eigi boðit færa en hundraði, no fewer than a hundred were invited;
fátt af þeirra mönnum, only a few of their men;
with gen., fátt manna, few men;
fátt góðs, but little good: þeir ugðu fátt at sér, they feared little for their own safety;
with numerals, fátt í, less than, short of, save;
vetri fátt í fjóra tigu, forty years all but one, thirty-nine years;
lítit fátt í fimm tigi vetra, little short of fifty years;
hálfum eyri fátt í átta merkr, eight marks less half an ounce;
sex menn it fæsta, six men at least;

bale, harm, mischief;
lesa ~ um e-n, to speak foul calumnies of one;
af ~i, from evil passion;
ef ek vissa þat ~ fyrir, if I could foresee that bale;
full skal signa ok við ~a sjá, and so prevent harm from it;
þat er ~ mikit (‘tis a bad omen), ef þú fœti drepr;

cattle, esp. sheep;
þeir ráku ~it (the sheep) upp á geilarnar;
gæta fjár, to herd or tend sheep;
ganganda ~, live stock, opp. to ‘dautt ~’, or ‘liggjanda ~,’ valuables, money;

property, money (hvárt sem ~ þat er land eðr annat ~);
fyrirgøra ~ ok fjörvi, to forfeit property and life;
~ er fjörvi firr, life is dearer than money;
~ veldr frænda rógi, money makes foes of kinsmen;
afla sér fjár ok frægðar (frama), to gain wealth and fame;
hér er ~ þat (the money), er Gunnarr greiddi;
þiggit þat, herra, ~ er í því, there is value in it;
pl. ~ (dat. fjám), property, means.

to hide, conceal (fálu þeir gullit í Rín; þær austr ok vestr enda fálu);
fel sverð þitt, sheathe thy sword;
yrkja fólgit, to use obscure phrases (in poetry);
impers., fal þá sýn (acc.) milli þeirra, they lost sight of one another;

~ e-m e-t, to make over, to give in trust or charge to one;
hann fal Óðni allan þann val, he gave all the slain to Odin;
mey frumunga fal hann (entrusted to) megi Gjúka;
~ e-m e-t á hendi, to commit a thing to one’s charge, to commend;
fálu sik ok sálir sínar guði almáttkum á hendi, they commended themselves and their souls to God Almighty;
~ e-t undir eið sinn (þegnskap sinn), to vouch upon one’s oath (upon one’s honour);
~ e-t undir e-m, to put under one’s charge;
er und einum mér öll um fólgin hodd Niflunga, the whole hoard of the Niflungs is in my hands alone;
man hér öll vár vinátta undir ~st, all our friendship will depend upon this;
~ e-n á brott, to put one out for alimentation;
~ e-n inni = ~ e-n á brott;
sá bóandi, er hann felr sik inni, the man with whom he boards and lodges;
~ búfé inni at e-m, to put out (cattle, sheep) to one to keep;

to hang up (= ~ upp);
~ á gálga, to hang on the gallows;
~ út til þerris, to hang out for drying;
~ e-t við e-t, to fasten to a thing;

in various fig. phrases,
~ trúnað á e-t, to believe in;
~ hug við e-t, to fix the mind upon;
~ yndi, to feel happy (in a place);
~ e-t í minni, to fix in the memory;
also absol., ~ kvæði, to learn a poem by heart;

with preps.:
~ á e-t, to come across, fall in with (= hitta á e-t);
impers., fann þat á, it could be perceived;
fann lítt á honum, hvárt, it was little to be seen whether, etc.;
~ at e-u, to find fault with, censure, blame;
~ e-t til, to bring forward, give as a reason (hvat finnr þú til þess?);

refl., ~st til e-s, to be pleased with;
impers., fannst Grími fátt til hans, Grim was little pleased with him;
láta sér lítit um ~st, to pay little heed to, rather dislike;
Ölvi fannst mikit um hann, Ölvir admired him much.

more;
fleira lið, more troops;
neut., fleira, more things (ekki sagði hann honum fleira);
with gen., fleira manna, more people (cfr. ‘margt manna’);
pl., hinir vóru þó miklu ~, more numerous;

 superl. most;
mun þat ~um manni úfœra þykkja, it will seem impossible to most people;
neut., flest, most things (flest í þessu bréfi);
~ allr (vel ~), flestir allir (vel flestir), almost all (flest allt stórmenni; flest öll hof).

foot, foot and leg;
spretta (støkkva) á fœtr, to start to one’s feet;
vera á fótum, to be out of bed, be up;
skjóta fótum undir sik, taka til fóta, to take to one’s heels;
eiga fótum fjör at launa, to save one’s life by running away;
hlaupa sem fœtr toga, to run as fast as feet can carry;
kominn af fótum fram, off one’s feet, decrepit;
hverr á fœtr öðrum, one after the other;

as adv. away;
hverfa ~, to turn away;
til ok ~, to and fro;
héðan í ~, hence, henceforlh;
þar út í frá, secondly, next.

to try;
~ íþróttar, to try a feat;
~ sín í móti e-m, to try one’s strength against another;
with infin., ~ at renna skeið, to try to run a race.

 frost;
~ var veðrs, it was frosty weather;
in pl., ~ mikil ok kuldar, much frost and cold;
~ ok snjóar, frost and snow.

before, at the head of, over;
vera ~ liði, to be over the troops;
vera ~ máli, to lead the case;
sitja ~ svörum, to undertake the defence;

of time, ago;
~ þrem nóttum, three nights ago;
~ stundu, a while ago;
~ löngu, long ago;
vera ~ e-u, to forebode (of a dream);

denoting obstacle, hindrance;
mikit gøri þer mér ~ þessu máli, you make this case hard for me;
varð honum lítit ~ því, it was a small matter for him;
Ásgrími þótti þungt ~, A. thought that things looked bad;

because of, for;
hon undi sér hvergri ~ verkjum, she had no rest for pains;
~ hræðslu, for fear;
illa fœrt ~ ísum, scarcely, passable for ice;
gáðu þeir eigi ~ veiðum at fá heyjanna, because of fishing, they neglected to make hay;
~ því at, because, since, as;

joined with adverbs ending in -an, governing acc. (~ austan, vestan, sunnan, norðan, útan innan, framan, handan, ofan, neðan);
~ austan, sunnan fjall, east, south of the fell;
~ neðan brú, below the bridge;
~ handan á, beyond the river;
~ innan garð, inside the fence;

with preps.:
~ e-t at e-m, to inflict on one;
~ skömm at e-m, to sneer at one;
refl., ~st at, to bestir oneself;
mega ekki at ~st, to be unable to do anything;
~ á e-n, to mock one;
refl., ~st á fœtr, to grow up;
~ e-n fram, to maintain, feed;
~ e-t í hljóðmæli, to hush up;
~ e-n í kaf, to plunge one under water;
~ sik í ætt, to vindicate one’s kinship (by a gallant deed);
~ e-n niðr = ~ e-n í kaf;
~ korn (sáð) niðr, to put down corn (seed), to sow;
~ e-t sundr, to split asunder;
~ e-n til kristni, to convert to Christianily;
~ til rétts máls, to turn into prose;
refl., ~st undan e-u, to quit (release) oneself of;
~ upp, to put up (~ upp vef);
to lift up (~ upp øxina);
to take out (~ upp mat ór katli);
~ út búðarveggi, to enlarge the walls of a booth.

to go;
~ heim, to go home;
~ braut, to go away;
~ til hvílu, to go to bed;
~ á skip, to go on board;
~ af skipi, to go ashore;
with infin., ~ sofa or at sofa, to go to sleep;
~ at eiga konu, to marry a woman;

to succeed;
ef þat gengr eigi, if that will not do;
impers., svá þykt, at þeim gekk þar eigi at fara, so close, that they could not go on there;
þeim gekk ekki fyrir nesit, they could not clear the ness;

to turn out, go in a specified way;
~ andæris, to go all wrong;
mart gengr verr en varir, many a thing goes worse than is looked for;
gekk þeim lítt atsókinn, they made little progress with the attack;
impers., e-m gengr vel (illa), one fares (goes on, gets on) well (badly);

with preps. and adverbs:
~ af e-u, to depart from, leave (þá gekk af honum móðrinn);
~ af vitinu, to go out of one’s wits;
~ af trú sinni, to apostatize;
to pass (síðan gengu af páskarnir);
to go off (gekk þegar af höfuðit);
to be left as surplus (þat er af skuldinni gekk);
nú gengr honum hey af, now he has some hay left;
~ af sér, to go to extremities, to go beyond oneself (mjök ~ þeir fóstbrœðr nú af sé);
~ aptr, to revert (return) to the former proprietor (síðan gengu þau lönd aptr undir Árna);
to be void, annulled (þá skal kaup aptr ~);
of a ghost, to walk again; of a door, to close, shut (gekk eigi aptr hurðin);
~ at e-m, to attack one;
~ at e-u, to agree to, accept a choice or offer (Flosi gekk fljótt at þessu öllu); to fit (skaltu fá mér lukla þá, sem ~ at kistum yðrum);
~ á e-t, to encroach upon (~ á ríki e-s); to break (~ á orð sín, eiða, grið, sættir, trygðir); to pierce, penetrate;
hann var í panzara, er ekki gekk á, that was proof against any weapons;
~ á vald e-s or e-m, ~ á hönd (hendr) e-m, to submit to, give oneself up to, surrender to one;
~ á bak e-u, to contravene;
~ eptir e-u, to go for, go to fetch (göngum heim eptir verðinu); to pursue, claim;
~ eptir, to prove true, be fulfilled (þetta gekk allt eptir, sem M. sagði fyrir);
~ frá e-u, to part with, lose (sumir munu ~ frá öllu fénu);
~ fram, to step forward;
~ fram vel, to go forward bravely, in a battle;
to come to pass, come into execution (skal þess bíða, er þetta gengr fram);
to increase (fé Hall gerðar gekk fram ok gørðist allmikit);
to depart this life (H. bóndi gengr fram til frænda sinna);
~ fyrir e-n, to present oneself before one (~ fyrir konung);
~ fyrir e-u, to take charge of, manage (var þar mart fólk, en húsbóndi gekk svá fyrir, at ekkert skorti); to yield to, be swayed by (hann gekk þá fyrir fortölum hennar);
~ í gegn e-m, to set oneself against one;
~ í gegn e-u, to confess, acknowledge;
maðr gengr í gegn, at á braut kveðst tekit hafa, the man confessed and said that he had taken it away;
~ í mál, to undertake a case;
~ með e-m (of a woman), to marry;
~ með barni, to be with child;
~ með burði (of animals), to be with young;
~ með e-u, to assist in, plead (~ með máli, bónorði);
~ milli (á m., í m.), to go between, intercede;
~ móti (á m., í m.) e-m, to go to meet one;
~ móti e-u, to resist, oppose;
to confess, = ~ í gegn, ~ við e-u;
~ nær e-m, to be troublesome to one (þótti hón œrit nær ~ Þórgerði);
~ e-m nær, to approach, come near to one (sá hefir á brott komizt, er næst gekk Gunnari um alla hluti);
~ saman, to marry;
of an agreement, bargain, to be brought about;
saman gekk kaupit með þeim, they came to a bargain;
~ sundr (í s.), to go asunder, part;
~ til, to go up to a thing (gangit til ok hyggit at); of the wind, to veer (veðrit gekk til útsuðrs);
e-m gengr e-t til e-s, one has some reason for doing a thing;
en þat gekk mér til þess (that was my reason), at ek ann þér eigi;
hversu hefir ykkr til gengit, how have you fared?
Loka gekk lítt til, it fared ill with L.;
~ um e-t, to go about a thing;
~ um beina, to wait upon guests;
~ um sættir, to go between, as peacemaker;
~ um e-n, to befall, happen to one (þess, er um margan gengr guma); of the wind, to go round, veer (gekk um veðrit ok styrmdi at þeim); to manage (fékk hón svá um gengit, at);
~ undan, to escape to absent oneself;
g. undir e-t, to take upon oneself, undertake (a duty);
~ undir e-n, to subject oneself to;
~ upp, to be wasted (of money);
to get loose, to he torn loose (þeir glímdu svá at upp gengu stokkar allir á húsinu);
of a storm, gale, to get up, rise (veðr gekk upp);
of an ice-bound river, áin var gengin upp, swollen with ice;
~ við staf, to walk with a stick;
~ við e-u or e-t, to avow;
~ yfir e-t, to go beyond, disregard (hann vildi eigi ~ yfir þat, er hann vissi réttast);
~ yfir e-n, to overcome, to befall, happen to one;
slíkt sem yfir hefir gengit, all that has happened;
eitt skal yfir okkr ~, we shall share one fate;

 to be altered, to change, of tradition (~ í munni);
láta e-t ~, to waive, give up;
e-m gengst hugr við, is moved to compassion by something (þá gekst Þorgerdi hugr við harmatölur hans);
~ at, to engage in a fight (þeir gengust at lengi);
~ á, to be set off against one another (á gengust vígin húskarlanna); to dash against one another;
á gengust eiðar, the oaths were broken;
~ nær, to come to close quarters;
~ við, to gain strength: áðr en við gengist hans bæn, before his prayer should be fulfilled;
E. hafði mikit við gengizt um menntir, E. had much improved himself in good breeding.

prevailing, being in vogue;
hafa mikinn gang, to be much in vogue;
heldr er vaxandi ~ at þeim, they (viz. the dreams) were rather on the increase;
svá mikill ~ var orðinn at eldinum, the fire had got to such a height.

mind, wits, senses;
heimta aptr ~ sitt, to come to one’s senses again;
vera gætinn at ~i, to be on one’s guard;
vita til síns ~s, to be master of one’s wits;

disposition, liking;
honum var vel í ~i til Freysteins, he was well disposed to Fr.;
þú fellst mér vel í ~, you are to my liking;
féllst hvárt öðru (féllust þau) vel í ~, they liked one another well.

to give (~ e-m e-t);
hann gaf þeim góðar gjafar, he gave them good gifts;
~ e-m ráð, to give one advice;
~ hljóð, to give a hearing;
~ e-m sök, e-t at sök, to lay to one’s charge, to bring a charge against one;
~ slög, to deal blows;
~ e-m drekka, to give one to drink;
impers., e-m gefr byr (byri), one gets a fair wind (gaf þeim byr ok sigla þeir í haf);
absol., gaf þeim vel, they got a fair wind;
ef fœri gefr á, if you get a chance;
ef yðr (dat.) gefr eigi missýi í þessu máli, if you are not mistaken in this matter;
þat gaf öllum vel skilja, it was clear for all to understand;

to give, grant;
~ heimleyfi, to grant furlough;
~ e-m grið, to grant quarter or pardon to one;
~ e-m líf, to grant one his life;

with preps.:
~ sér litit (mikit) af (or at) e-u, to take little (much) notice of;
~ á e-t, to pour water on;
fig. to press on (gefr Ormr þá á);
~ e-m til e-s, to give in return for a thing (~ fé til sátta);
impers., honum hafði vel gefit til (had good luck) um hefndina;
~ sér mikit (lítit) um e-t, to take great (little) interest in (= ~ sér mikit, lítit, af or at e-u);
~ sér fátt um e-t, to take coldly, take little notice of;
~ e-t upp, to give up (~ upp alla mótstöðu);
~ sik upp, to surrender;
~ upp gamalmenni, to give old people up, let them starve;
~ upp leiguna, to remit the rent;
~ upp sakir, to remit offences;
upp gefinn, exhausted;
~ e-t við e-u, to give in payment for;
~ sik við, to give in (þat er líkara, at ek ~ mik við);

vel (illa), to prove good (bad);
opt hafa mér vel gefizt yður ráð, your counsels have often proved good for me;
hefir þeim þat ok aldri vel gefizt, it has never turned out well;
~ gjöfum, to give gifts to one another;
~ á vald e-s, to give oneself into another’s power;
~ upp, to give in, surrender;
e-m gefst e-t yfir, one does wrong in a thing (ef göfgum mönnum gáfust stórir hlutir yfir).

to signify, mean, matter;
spurði, hverju gegndi úgleði sú, er hann hafði, asked what his sadness meant;
sumir mæltu í móti ok kváðu øngu ~, some denied it, and said it was of no use;
meira en hófi gegnir, more than is due, above measure;
~ tíðendum, to be of importance;

to get;
~ orðstír, to get fame;
~ sér e-t, to get for oneself (góðs um œðis ef sér ~ mætti);
~ gott af e-m, to get good of one;
with dat. of the person, ~ váluðum vel, to be kind to the poor;
~ e-m illa, to do harm to one;
impers., getr e-t, there is (got);
eigi getr slíkan (there is none such) í konungs herbergjum;

almost like an auxil. verb with infin.;
ek Gunnari gat at unna, I loved G.;
~ at lita, sjá (to get) to see;
without ‘at’, er slíkt getr fœða jóð, that shall rear such a child;

refl., ~st at e-u, to like;
því at mér gezt vel at þér, because I like thee well;
láta sér getit at e-u, to be pleased with;
eigi læt ek mér at einu getit, I am not pleased to have always the same, I want some change;

to guess;
~ gatu, to guess a riddle;
~ rétt, to guess right;
~ e-s til, to guess, suppose;
~ í hug e-m, to guess one’s thoughts;

 guess, conjecture (er þat ~ mín, at);
spá er spaks ~, a wiseman’s guess is a prophecy;
ætla ek eigi þat til getu, at hann sé þar, it is not likely that he is there;
leiða getum um e-t, to make a guess at.

with preps.:
~ at e-u, eptir e-u, to inquire into, try to flnd out;
also refl., ~st eptir e-u;
~ niðr, to dig down;
svá kyrr sem hann væri grafinn niðr, as if he had been rooted in the ground;
~ til e-s, to dig for (~ til vatns);
~ um e-t = ~ eptir e-u;
gróf hann svá undir þeim, at, he sounded them so that;
~ e-t upp, to find out.

with preps.:
~ á e-u, to catch hold of (H. greip á stafni);
~ á kýlinu, to touch upon a sore place;
~ til e-s, to seize (~ til sverðsins);
greip hundrinn til hans, the dog snapped at him;
~ upp, to catch up (Loki greip upp mikla stöng);
~ við orði, to commence speaking.

(-ar, -ir),  suspicion, doubt, uncertainty (e-m er ~ á e-u);
grafa grun á (um e-t), to suspect;
hafa grun á e-m um e-t, to suspect one of a thing;
e-m leikr ~ á, um e-t, one feels suspicious about a thing;
draga gruni á um e-t, to conceive a suspicion of;
renna grunum á e-t, to doubt, to guess at;
búa um grun, to be suspected;
um grun, by guile (sem þetta væri eigi um grun gört).

 to become, arise (þá gørðist hlátr);
sá atburðr gørðist, it came to pass;
gørðist með þeim félagskapr, they entered into fellowship;
~ konungr, to become king;
svá gørðist, at, it so happened, that;
impers., næsta gørist mér kynligt, I feel rather uneasy;
~ ferðar sinnar, to set out for a journey;
with infin., ár var þat er Guðrún gørðist at deyja, was nigh to death;
~ í, to occur, happen (sögdu þeir konungi, hvat í hafði görzt);
~ til e-s, to set about doing (þessir menn hafa görzt til svá mikils stórrœðis).

arbitration, award;
leggja mál í ~, to submit a case to arbitration;
taka menn til gørðar, to choose umpires;
segja or lúka upp ~, to deliver the arbitration.

to set in order, prepare, perform, etc.;
~ veizlu, to make a banquet;
~ seið, blót, to perform a sacrifice;
~ ferð, to make a journey;
~ ráð sitt, to make up one’s mind;
~ ráð með e-m, to take counsel with, advise one;

with acc. of an a.;
~ sik líkan e-m, to make oneself like to, imitate one;
~ sik reiðan, to take offence;
~ skjót-kørit, to make a quick choice;

to do, act;
~ gott (illt), to do good (evil);
hefir hann marga hluti gört stór-vel til min, I have received many great benefits at his hands;
~ e-m gagn, to give help to one;

impers. one becomes;
hann gørði fölvan, he turned pale;
veðr gørði hvast, a gale arose;
brið mikla gørði at þeim, they were overtaken by a storm;
mér gørir svefnhöfugt I grow sleepy;

with preps.:
~ mikit af sér, to distinguish oneself;
~ e-t af e-m, to extort (take)from one;
~ e-t af við e-n, to wrong a person, transgress against one (ek hefi engan hlut af gørt við þik);
~ góðan (mikinn) róm at máli e-s, to cheer (praise) another’s speech;
~ at e-u, to mend, put right (tekr bann kníf ok gørir at skónum);
to heal (kannt þú nókkut ~ at slíkum meinum);
hann gørði þat eina at, er hann átti, he did only what he ought;
slíkt gørir at, er sölin etr, so it happens when one eats seaweed;
~ at skapi e-s, to conform to one’s wishes;
~ at álitum, to take into consideration;
~ sér úgetit at e-u, to be displeased with;
~ á hluta e-s, to wrong one, do harm to;
~ fáleika á sik, to make oneself look sad;
~ eptir e-m, to send for one;
~ e-t eptir, to imitate;
~ ráð fyrir e-u, to suppose;
~ sér mikit (lítit) fyrir, to make great (small) efforts;
~ sér e-t hug, to resolve, plan;
~ sér gott hug, to be easy in mind;
~ milli (á m.) e-m, to decide between;
~ til e-rra, to make preparations for;
~ e-t til, to prepare, make ready, dress meat;
~ til e-s, to de serve a thing;
hvat hafðir þú til gört, what hadst thou done to deserve it?;
~ sœmdir til e-s, to confer honour upon one;
~ til saka við e-n, to transgress against one;
~ um mál, to arbitrate in a case (þær urðu málalyktir, at Þórdr skyldi ~ um);
~ mikit um sik, to make a great noise;
~ sér mikit um e-t, to make much of, admire;
~ upp, to rebuild, restore (~ upp hús, skála);
~ e-n upp, to upset one;
~ e-n útan, to banish, exile (Flosi var görr útan);
~ e-t við e-n, to do with one (þá var um rœtt, hvat við þá skyldi ~);
~ við e-u, to prevent;

to use (tvau net eru rý, ok ~ eigi höfð verit);
orð þau sem hann hafði um haft, which he had made use of;
~ fagrmæli við e-n, to flatter one;
~ hljóðmæli við e-n, to speak secretly to one;
~ tvimæli á e-u, to speak doubtfully of a thing;
~ viðrmæli um e-t, to use mocking words;
hann var mjök hafðr við mál manna, much used to, versed in, lawsuits;

to have, hold, maintain;
~ vináttu við e-n, to maintain friendship with one;
~ hættumikit, to run a great risk;
~ heilindi, to have good health;

to bring, carry;
~ e-n heim með sér, to bring one home;
hann hafði lög, út hingat ór Noregi, he brought laws hither from Norway;
~ sik (to betake oneself) til annara landa;

to get, gain, win;
hann hafði eigi svefn, he got no sleep;
hefir sá jafnan, er hættir, he wins that ventures;
~ gagn, sigr, to gain victor;
~ meira hlut, to get the upper hand, gain the day;
~ betr (verr), to get the better (worse) of it;
~ sitt mál, to win one’s suit;
~ tafl, to win the game;
~ erendi, to do one’s errand, succeed;
~ bana, to suffer death, to die;
~ sigr, to be worsted;
~ góðar viðtökur, to be well received;
~ tíðindi af e-m, to get tidings of, or from, one;
~ sœmd, óvirðing af e-m, to get honour, disgrace from one;
with gen., ~ e-s ekki, to fail to catch one (hann kemst á skóg undan, ok höfðu þeir hans ekki);
ekki munu vér hans ~ at sinni, we shall not catch him at present;

to behave, do, or fare, so an so esp. with an adv.;
~ vel, illa, vetr, to behave (do) well, badly, be worse;
~ sik vel, to behave;
~ vel, to be well off or happy;
~ hart, to be in a wretched plight;

~ e-n nær e-u, to expose one to (þú hafðir svá nær haft oss úfœru);
~ nær e-u, to come near to, esp. impers.;
nær hafði okkr nú, it was a narrow escape;
svá nær hafði hausinum, at, the shot so nearly touched the head, that;
ok er nær hafði, skipit mundi fljóta, when the ship was on the point of flloating;

as an auxiliary verb, in the earliest time with the pp. of transitive verbs in acc.;
hefir þú hamar um fólginn, hast thou hidden the hammer?;
ek hefi sendan mann, I have sent a man;
later with indecl. neut. pp.;
hefir þú eigi sét mik, hast thou not seen me?;

with preps.:
~ e-t at, to do, act;
hann tók af þér konuna, en þú hafðir ekki at, but thou didst not stir, didst take it tamely;
absol., viltu þess freista, ok vita hvat at hafi, wilt thou try and see what happens?;
~ e-t at hlífiskildi (skotspœni), to use as a shield (as a target);
~ e-n háði, hlátri, to mock, laugh at;
~ e-t at engu, vettugi, to hold for naught, take no notice of;
~ sakir á e-n have charges against one;
~ á rás, to take to one’s heels, run off;
~ e-t eptir, to do or repeat a thing after one;
~ e-t fram, to produce (vápn þorgils vóru fram höfð); to carry out, hold forth;
~ mál fram, to proceed with a suit;
var um búit, ekki fram haft, all was made ready but nothing done;
~ e-t frammi, í frammi, to use, make use of (~ í frammi kúgan);
ok öll lögmæt skil frammi ~, and discharge all on official duties;
~ e-t fyrir satt, to hold for true;
eigi em ek þar fyrir sönnu hafðr, I am not truly aimed for that, it is a false charge;
~ e-n fyrir sökum um e-t, to charge one with;
~ í hótum við e-n, to threaten one;
~ e-t með höndum, to have in hand;
höfum eiai sigrinn ór hendi, let not victory slip out of our hands;
~ ór við e-n, to behave so and so towards one (hefir þú illa ór haft við mik);
~ e-t til e-s to use for (höfðu þeir til varnar skot ok spjót); to be a reason or ground for;
vér hyggjum þat til þess haft vera, at þar hafi menn sézt, we believe the foundation of the story is that men have been seen there;
~ mikit (lítit) til síns máls, to have much (little) in support of one’s case;
~ e-t til, to have at hand, possess;
orð þau, sem hann hafði um haft, the words which he had used;
keisari hafði fátt um, did not say much;
~ e-n undir, to get one under, subdue one;
~ e-t uppi, to take (heave) up (~ uppi fœri, net);
Skarpheðinn hafði uppi øxina, S. heaved up the axe;
~ flokk uppi, to raise a party, to rebel;
~ uppi tafl, to play at a game;
~ e-n uppi, to bring one to light;
~ uppi rœður, to begin a discussion;
~ e-t úti, to have done, finished (~ úti sitt dagsverk);
~ við e-m, to be a match for one;
~ sik við, to exert oneself;
~ mikit (lítit) við, to make a great (little) display;
hann söng messu ok bafði mikit við, and made much of it;
hann bad jarl leita, bann hafði lítit við þat, he did it lightly;
haf ekki slíkt við, do not say so;
haf þú lítit við at eggja sonu þina, refrain from egging on thy sons;

 to dwell, abide;
þeir höfðust mjök í ferðum, they spent much of their life in travelling;
~ vel, to do well, thrive (vaxa ok vel ~);
~ at, to do;
Lambi sá hvat Steinarr hafðist at, what he was doing;
~ við, to dwell, stay (hér mun ek við ~);
~ vel við, to bear oneself well up;
~ orð við, to speak to one another.

to manage, arrange, with dat. (svá skulu vér ~ inngöngu vorri);
fénu var ~t til gæzlu, the money was taken into keeping;
with adv., hvernig skulum vér þá til ~, how shall we arrange it;
~ svá til, at, to arrange or contrive it so that;
~ e-m til e-s, to turn out so and so for one (þat ~ði Ólafi til mikils harms);

support, backing (hann hefir nú ~ mikit af konungi);
~ ok traust, help and support in need;
koma e-m at ~i, í ~, to be of use or help to one;
hér kemr illa í ~, it does little good;

with preps.:
~ á e-u, to hold, wield in the hand (~ á sverði);
to hold to a thing, go on with it, be busy about (~ á drykkju, á ferð sinni, á sýslu);
~ e-t af e-m, to hold (land, office) from or of one (þeir er höfðu haldið land af Danakonungi);
~ mikit af e-m, to make much of one;
~ eptir e-m, to pursue one;
~ e-u eptir, to keep back;
~ sik frá e-u, to keep oneself back from, refrain from;
~ e-u fram, to uphold, support;
~ e-u fyrir e-u, to withhold from one;
to protect against (héldu engar grindr fénu fyrir birninum);
~ e-n fyrir e-t, to hold, consider one to be so and so (síðan hélt konungr Erling fyrir tryggvan vin);
~ í e-t, to hold fast, grasp (þú skalt ~ í hurðarhringinn);
~ til e-s, to be the cause of, be conducive to;
heldr þar margt til þess, there are many reasons for this;
hélt til þess (conduced to it) góðgirni hans;
~ til e-s, to be bent on, fond of (~ mjök til skarts, til gleði);
~ til jafns við e-n, to bear up against one, to be a match for one;
~ um e-t, to grasp with the hand (= ~ hendi um e-t);
~ barni undir skírn, to hold at baptism;
~ e-u upp, to hold aloft, lift (~ upp höndum);
~ upp árum, to hold up the oars, cease pulling;
to uphold, maintain, support (~ upp hofum, kristninni);
to keep going (~ upp bardaga);
to discharge (~ upp kostnaði, bótum);
~ upp bœnum fyrir e-m, to pray for one;
~ e-u við, to maintain a thing;
~ við e-m, to stand against (hvar sem harm kom fram, hélt ekki við honum);
impers. to be on the point of;
hélt þá við atgöngu (acc.), they were near coming to fight;
heldr nú við hót, it is little short of threats;

 to hold oneself, stay (mátti hann eigi þar ~);
to hold out, continue, last (hélzt vinátta með þeim);
to be kept safe and sound;
menn allir héldust (all hands were saved) ok svá fé;
to be valid, stand (engi má ~ dómr hans);
~ á, to pull one against another, wrestle, fight;
impers., e-m helzt vel (illa) á e-u, one has good (bad) luck with a thing (mér helzt lítt á sauðamönnum);
~ við, to stay, remain;
~ við e-m, to resist, make a stand against one (hélzt þá ekki við honum).

to sway to the wrong side (= ~ til um. e-t);
~ sögu, to give an unfair report;
~ nökkurum orðum til, to let fall a few words;
~ eptir e-m, to be biassed in one’s favour;
~ sér, to lean with one’s body;
impers. to lie over, with dat. (~ði honum svá, at sjór féll inn á annat borð);
sólu ~r, the sun sinks;
vetri ~r, the winter is declining;
tafli ~r á e-n, the game turns against one;

refl., ~st, to lean with the body;
to lie over (þá tók mjök at ~st Ormrinn);
á þá ~ðist bardaginn, the battle turned against them;
~st eptir e-u, to sway towards a thing.

to heave, raise, lift (~ stein);
hann hóf upp augu sin, he lifted up his eyes;
~ sik á lopt, to make a leap;
~ handa, to lift the hands (for defense);
~ höfuðs, to lift the head, be undaunted;
~ graut, to lift the porridge, eat it with a spoon;

to begin;
~ mál sitt, to begin one’s speech;
~ ferð, to set out on a journey, to start;
~ flokk, to raise a party;
~ ákall, to raise a claim;
impers., hefr e-t = hefr upp e-t, it begins (hér hefr Kristnisögu);
refl., ~st, to begin, originate (hvaðan af hefir hafizt skáldrskarpr?);

with preps.:
~ e-t af e-u, to take it off;
impers., en er af henni hóf öngvit (acc.), when she recovered from her swoon;
þá hóf af mér vámur allar, all ailments left me;
refl., ~st af höndum e-m, to leave one;
~ á rás, to take to one’s feet (= hafa á rás);
refl., láta ~st fyrir, to retreat, withdraw;
~ munn sinn í sundr, to open one’s mouth;
impers., Birkibeina (acc.) hefr undan, the B. drew back;
~ e-t upp, to lift up (hann hóf orminn upp á hendi sér);
impers., hóf honum upp brýn (acc.), his face brightened;
~ e-t upp, to begin (= ~ 3);
Egill hóf upp kvæðit, E. began his poem;
impers., hér hefr upp Konunga-bók, here begins the K.;
refl., ~st upp, to begin (hér ~st upp landnám);
~st upp til ófriðar (með ófriði), to begin warfare;
láta ~st við, to lay to (naut.).

(-da, -dr),  to avenge, take vengeance;
with the thing and the person in gen.,
~ sára, to avenge the wounds,
~ bróður síns, to avenge one’s brother,
~ sín, to avenge oneself;
with dat. of the person,
~ e-m, to take vengeance on (skulu við ~ honum);
with the thing in gen. and the person in dat.;
áttu honum at ~ frændaláts, thou hast to avenge on him the death of a kinsman;
impers., e-m hefnir e-t, one pays (suffers) for a thing (svá hefndi honum þat mikla mikillæti);
with preps., ~ e-m fyrir e-t, ~ e-s a e-m, to avenge a thing on one;
refl., ~st, to avenge oneself (= ~ sín);
~st e-s, to avenge a thing;
~st á e-m, to avenge oneself on a person;
e-m hefnist e-t, one has to pay (suffer) for;
~st mun honum vist, the day of retribution will assuredly come to him.

to call on one;
~ e-n á brott, to call on one to be gone, bid one go (heitit mik héðan);
~ á e-n, to exhort one (in battle);
to invoke (~ á hinn heilaga Ólaf);
~ á e-n til e-s, to invoke (appeal to) one for a thing (hann hét á Þór til fulltings);

intrans., the pres. ‘heiti’ (not ‘heit’), to be hight, be called;
