sitja fyrir knjóm e-rri, to attend a woman in childbirth;

with dat. of the object, to make to come, to take, bring, carry, etc.;
hann skyldi ~ Þór í Geirröðargarða, he should make Th. come to G.;
hann kom Þórhaddi heilum yfir ána, he brought Th. safe across the river;
~ e-m í hel, to put one to death;
~ e-m til falls, to make one fall;
~ e-m í sætt við e-n, to reconcile one with another;
~ sér vel hjá e-m, to bring oneself into favour with, be agreeable to (þeir kómu sér vel við alla);
~ e-u til leiðar (til vegar), to effect, bring about;
~ orðum við e-n, to speak with a person (hann gørði sik svá reiðan, at ekki mátti orðum við hann ~);

with preps.:
~ e-u af sér, to get rid of (allt mun ek til vinna at ~ af mér yðvarri reiði);
~ e-u af, to abolish (Þvi hafði eigi orðit af komitmeði öllu);
~ at e-m, to come upon one (kómu þessir at honum fyrir Sjólandi með tveim skipum);
~ at hendi, to happen (mikill vandi er kominn at hendi);
impers., Gunnarr játaði því, en þá er at kom, vildi hann eigi, G. agreed to it, but when it came to the point he would not;
~ at e-u, to come at, regain, recover (~ at hamri);
~ sér at e-u, to bring oneself to (Þ. kom sér ekki at því);
~ á e-t, to come on, hit (höggit kom á lærit);
~ e-u á, to bring about, effect (máttu þeir øngum flutningum á ~);
~ kristni (dat.) á England, to christianize E.;
~ fram, to come forth, appear, emerge (sigldi E. suðr með landi ok kom fram í Danmörk); to be produced, brought forward (nú mun pat fram ~ sem ek sagða);
~ e-u fram, to bring about, effect (~ fram hefndum);
~ fyrir e-t, to be an equivalent for (fyrir víg Hjartar skyldi ~ víg Kols);
allt mun ~ fyrir eitt, it will all come to the same;
~ fyrir ekki, to come to naught, be of no avail;
e-m þykkir fyrir ván komit, at, one thinks it past all hope, that;
~ e-u fyrir, to destroy (hann kom hverjum hesti fyrir);
~ í e-t, to come into, enter;
mál koma í dóm, suits are brought up for judgement;
~ niðr, to come down;
hann reyndi eptir, hvar G. væri niðr kominn, what had become of G.;
kom þar niðr tal hennar, at hon sagði honum, hversu, the end of her talk was, that she told him how koma;
~ hart niðr, to pay dearly for it (ek hafða illa til gört, enda kom ek hart niðr);
~ saman, to come together, gather (er saman kom liðit); to agree;
þat kom saman (or ásamt) með þeim, they agreed on it;
impers., kom þeim vel saman (ásamt), they agreed well;
~ e-u saman, to bring about, effect;
~ saman sættum með e-m, to reconcile them;
~ til e-s, to come to a person or place (jarlinn kom með allan her sinn til Dyflinnar);
~ till ríkis, to come to, or succeed to, the throne;
~ til e-s, to cause: þat kemr til þess, at, the reason is, that; to help, avail: koma til lítils, to come to little, be of small avail (= ~ fyrir lítit); to concern: þetta mál kemr ekki til þín, this quarrel is no business of thine; þat er til mín kemr, so far as I am concerned; to mean, signify (Þ. kvezk skilja, hvar orð hans kómu til); to be of value: sverð þat, er til kom mörk gulls, that was worth a ‘mark’ of gold; mikit þykkir til e-s ~, one is much thought of, is thought to be of great importance;
~ til, to be born;
~ e-m undan, to help one to escape;
~ undir e-n, to come unto one;
ef undir oss skal ~ kjörit, if we are to choose;
~ e-m undir, to get one down, overcome one;
~ upp, to come up;
tungl kemr upp, the moon rises;
eldr kom upp, fire broke out;
kom þá upp af tali þeirra, at, the end of their talk was, that; to come out, become known (kom þat þá upp, at hann hafði beðit hennar);
~ e-u upp, to open (kerling tekr hörpuna ok vildi upp ~);
hann mátti lengi eigi orði upp ~, it was long before he could utter a word;
~ við e-t, to touch (komit var við hurðina);
þeir kómu við sker, they struck on a reef;
hann kemr við margar sögur, he appears in many sagas; to be added to (koma þær nætr við hinar fyrri);
~ við, to fit, be convenient, suit;
~ e-u við, to employ, make use of (ek mátta eigi boganum við ~); hann kom því við (he brought about), at engi skyldi fara með vápn; urðu þeir at flýja sem því kómu við, all fled that could;
~ sér við, to bring about, effect, be able to do (ek mun veita þér slíkt lið sem ek má mér við ~); to behave (hversu hann kom sér við í þessum málum);
~ yfir, to pass over (hvert kveld, er yfir kom);

 to come to the end, get through, reach (hann komst við svá búit í ríki sitt);
~ af, to escape, save one’s life (tveir drukknuðu kómust af);
~ at e-u, to get at a thing, procure;
eigi skaltu illa at ~, thou shalt not get it unfairly;
~ á fœtr, to get on one’s legs;
~ undan, to escape (alt þat lið, er undan komst);
~ út, to get out;
~ við, to be able (brenn allt ok bæl sem þú mátt við ~); to be touched (hann komst mjök við ok felldi tár);
~ við veðri, to get abroad (hann lét þat ekki við veðri ~);
~ yfir e-t, to overcome, get hold of (er hann komst yfir féit).

 come;
~ af e-m, descended from;
~ af sér, in a declining state, on the decline (kristnin var mjök svá af sér komin);
~ at andláti, dauða, breathing one’s last;
vóru þeir mjök at komnir, they were much exhausted;
vel (illa) ~, in good (bad) estate;
hann var vel til náms ~, he was in a good place for learning;
mér þykkir son minn hvergi betr ~, methinks my son is nowhere better off, in better hands;
~ á sik vel, in a good state, accomlished (~ á sik manna bezt);
vera á legg ~, to be grown up;
vera svá aldrs ~, to be of such an age;
hann sagði henni, hvar þá var komit, he told her how matters stood;
vera ~ til e-s, to be entitled to (þeir, er til einskis eru komnir);
rétt ~ til konungdóms, right heir to the kingdom.

gen. sing. from an obsolete ‘konr’, kind;
alls ~, of all kinds;
hvers ~, of every kind;
margs ~, of many kinds;
ýmiss ~, of sundry kinds;
sams ~, of the same kind.

to try, with gen.;
~ afls, magns, to try (put forth) one’s strength;
~ mans, to fall in love;
B. þurfti alls at ~, B. had to exert all his strength;
~ kapps, to strive hard;

choice, chance, opportunity;
~ er e-s, there is a chance (þat er hverjum manni boðit at leita sér lífs, meðan ~ er);
eiga e-s kost, to have a choice of (eiga slíkra manna kost);
eiga alls kosti við e-n, to have one altogether in one’s power;

adverb. usages:
þat er til kostar, ef, it is well done, if;
at þeim kosti, on that condition;
at öðrum kosti, else, otherwise;
at síðasta, efsta kosti, in the last instance, last emergency;
alls kostar, quite, in every respect;
eigi eins kostar, not very, not peculiarly;
annars kostar, as for the rest;
nökkurs kostar, in any wise;
þess kostar, in this case, thus.

(kref, krafða, krafðr and krafinn),  to crave, demand, claim (~ e-n e-s);
~ e-n máls, orða, to ask speech of one (engi þorði at ~ hann orða);
hann krafði dura, he asked to be let in, knocked at the door;
refl., ~st, to claim, with gen. (krafðist Hávarðr torfunnar).

spec. phrases;
~ hóf at um e-t, ~ hóf sitt, to know the proper mean, to behave with moderation;
uxarnir kunnu þó heim, the oxen found their way home;
~ enga mannraun to have no experience of men;
~ e-m þökk, aufusu, to be thankful, obliged to one;

to say, utter;
hann kvað eigi orð, he did not utter a word;
~ gleði-orð, to say a cheerful word;
with infin., hann kvað þat satt vera, he said it was true;
~ at orði, to express oneself, say, speak;

with preps.:
~ at, to say, state;
gramm. to pronounce, sound;
~ e-t at e-m, to inflict on;
mikill harmr er at oss kveðinn, great grief has been sent on us;
~ á, to fix, determine (~ á stefnudag);
impers. to state;
kveðr þar skýrt á þetta, it is there expressly stated;
to cancel, object to (~ á gögn, to cancel the evidence) to make up one’s mind, resolve;
~ e-t upp, to recite, declare (þat skulu lög vera, sem hann kveðr upp);
~ við, to reply, answer (hitki hann veit, hvat hann skal við ~, ef);
to utter a cry, etc. (hundrinn kvað við hátt);
to sound (því næst kvað lúðr við);

refl., ~st, to say of oneself;
þeir er biskupar kváðust vera, who said they were bishops;
hann kveðst eigi ríða mundu, he said he would not ride;
impers., mér kveðsk = ek kveð mér;
Kára kvaðsk (= Kári kvað sér) önnur ferð betri þykkja, K. said he thought another course preferable;
~st at, to exchange verses or songs.

to call on, summon (Þórvaldr kvaddi húskarla sína);
~ e-n e-s, to request (demand) of one;
~ matar, svefns, to call for food, sleep;
~ sér hljóðs, to call for a hearing;
~ þings, to convoke a meeting;
~ e-n e-s, to call on, summon, one to do something (vóru vér kvaddir at bera vitni þat);

with preps.:
~ e-n at e-u, to call on a person to do a thing, call his attention to (þik kveð ek at þessu);
~ e-n frá e-u, to exclude from, deprive of (ek hefi opt menn frá æfi kvadda, er eigi vildu hlýða mínum boðum);
~ e-n til e-s, to call on one for a thing (~ menn til ferðar);
~ e-n upp, to call on one to rise (síðan vaknaði Haraldr ok kvaddi upp menn sína); to summon to arms;
síðan safnaði hann liði ok kveðr upp almenning, after that he gathered men and roused the whole country;
~ e-n út, to call one out of the house (hann kvaddi út Höskuld ok Hrút).

(-ta, -tr),  to make known;
~ sér e-t, to make oneself acquainted with, study (~ sér kaupskap);
hverr mun ~ oss til konungs, who will show us the way to the king;
refl. ~st, to become known, come abroad;
~st e-u, to become acquainted with;
e-m kynnist e-t, one gets acquainted with;
~st e-t, to study (= ~ sér e-t);
~st við e-n, to make acquaintance with one.

due place, right position;
leggja stýri í ~, to ship the rudder;
ganga ór ~i, to go wrong;
fóru nú brýnn hans í ~, his brows became smooth and straight;
koma ~i á e-t, to put to rights, get a thing into order;
komast vel í ~, to fall into good order;

adverbial phrases:
í tvennu ~i, in two parts, double;
í öllu ~i, in every respect, quite;
í sumu (mörgu) ~i, in same (many) respects;
with compar. or superl., denoting degree;
í meira ~i, considerably, rather;
í fyrra ~i, rather early, among the earliest;
í verra ~i, among the worst;
í hljóðara ~i, rather silent;
í nærra ~i, rather close;
þann dag svaf Unnr í lengra ~i, U. slept that day longer than she was wont;
Helias var í fyrsta ~i spámanna, H. was one of the first of prophets;
í elzta ~i sona hans, among the oldest of his sons;
minnsta ~, the least share.

to set, put, place (~ hest á stali);
~ e-n í myrkvastofu, to thrust in prison (dungeon);
~ e-t í ljós, to make it known;
~ e-t af e-u, to take off (vóru þá látnir fjötrar af Hallfreði);

with infin. to let, make, cause;
látit mik vita, let me know;
ek lét drepa Þóri, I made Th. to be killed;
~ sér fátt um finnast, to disapprove;
hann lét fallast (= sik falla), he let himself fall;

with pp., in circumlocutory phrases;
hann lét verða farit, he went;
hann lét hana verða tekna, he seized her;
ellipt., omitting the infin., ~ um mælt, to let be said, to declare;
hann lét harðan Hunding veginn, he slew the stark H.;

to behave, comport oneself (forvitni er mér á hversu þeir ~);
hann lét illa í svefni, he was restless in his sleep;
~ allstórliga, to make oneself big;
~ hljótt yfir e-u, to keep silence about a thing;
~ mikit um sik, to puff oneself up;

with preps.:
~ af e-u, to leave off, desist from (sumir létu af blótum);
absol., ~ af, to cease;
~ af hendi, to let out of one’s hands, deliver up;
~ fé af, to kill (slaughter) cattle;
~ aptr, to shut (~ aptr hurðina);
~ at e-u, to yield to, comply with (allir þeir, er at mínum orðum ~);
~ at stjórn, to obey the helm (of a ship);
~ e-t eptir, to leave behind (þeir létu menu eptir at gæta skipa);
~ eptir e-m, to mimic, ape (mun Gísli hafa látit eptir fíflinu);
~ e-t eptir e-m, to grant, indulge one in a thing (lát þú þetta eptir mér);
~ fyrir e-u, to give way, yield (þeir munu verða fyrir at ~, ef vér leggjum sköruliga at);
~ í haf, to put out to sea;
~ e-t í mót e-m, not to indulge one;
~ e-t til, to grant (heimt mun brátt hit meira, ef þetta er til látit);
~ til við e-n, to yield (give way) to one (þeir báðu hann til ~ við konung);
~ undan e-m, to yield to one (ek skal hvergi undan þér ~);
~ upp, to open (~ upp hurð);
to let one get up (eptir þat lét K. þenna mann upp);
~ e-t uppi, to grant (ertu saklauss, ef þúlætr uppi vistina);
~ e-t út, to let out;
~ út (skip sitt), to put to sea (síðan létu þeir út ok sigldu til Noregs);
~ vel (illa) yfir e-u, to express approval (disapproval) of a thing (hann lætr vel yfir því);

to put;
~ eld í, to put fire to;
~ söðul á hest, to put a saddle on a horse;
~ árar upp, to lay up the oars, give up pulling;
~ ofan segi, to haul down, take in the sails;
~ at jörðu, at velli (or við jörðu, við velli), to overthrow, slay, kill;
~ hlut sinn, to lose one’s lot, be worsted;

to settle;
~ sakar, to settle strife;
~ lög, to lay down laws;
~ leið sína, to take a direction;
hann lagði mjök kvámur sínar í Ögr, he was in the habit of coming often to O.;

to lay out, pay, discharge;
~ at veði, to give as bail;
~ á hættu, to risk;
~ á mikinn kostnað, to run into great expenses;
~ líf á, to stake one’s life on a thing;
~ fé til höfuðs e-m, to set a price on one’s head;

to lay a ship’s course, stand of or on, sail, absol., or the ship in dat. or acc., lét hann blása herblástr ok ~ út ór höfninni, and sailed out of the harbour;
~ at, to land (lagði hann at við Sundólfsstaði);
in a naval battle, to attack (lögðu þeir þá at þeim);
~ undir land, to stand in towards land;
fig. to give in;
~ (skip) í rétt, to drift or run before the wind;

to set off, start;
~ á flótta, to take toftight;
~ eptir e-m, to pursue;
~ upp, to start on a journey;

impers. it turns, is driven in a direction (of smoke, smell, fire);
hingat leggr allan reykinn, all the smoke blows hitherward;
to freeze over, be covered with snow or ice (þá er ísa lagði á vötn);
~ nær, to be on the brink of;
nær lagði þat úfœru einu sinni, it had well nigh come to a disaster;

with preps.:
~ e-t af, to cede, give up (H. bróðir hans lagði af við hann sinn part í eyjunni);
to leave off, desist from (legg af héðan af versagørð, sagði erkibiskup);
~ af fénað, to slaughter cattle;
~ e-t aptr, to give back, return (báðu mik ~ aptr taflit);
~ at, ~ at landi, to land;
~ at e-m, to attack;
~ e-t á e-n, to impose, lay (a burden, tax) upon one (~ skatt, skyldir, yfirbót á e-n);
~ e-t á við e-n = ~ e-t á e-n;
~ stund, kapp, hug á e-t, to take pains about, great interest in, a thing;
~ ást, elsku, mætur á e-t, to feel love, affection, interest for a thing, to cherish a thing or person;
~ fæð, öfund, hatr á, to take dislike, envy, hatred to;
~ móti e-m, to oppose, contradict one;
~ e-t til, to furnish, contribute, as one’s share (hvern styrk hefir móðir mín til lagit með þér?);
~ fátt til, to say little, be reserved;
~ lof til, to give praise to;
~ gott (illt) til e-s, to lay a good (or ill) word to one, to interfere in a friendly (or unfriendly) manner;
~ e-t til lofs e-m, to put a thing to a person’s credit;
~ e-t til orðs, to talk about;
~ e-m e-t til ámælis, orðs, to blame one for a thing;
~ e-t undir or undir sik, to conquer, vanquish (Knútr konungr lagði allt land undir sik íNoregi);
~ e-t undir e-n, to submit a matter to a person, refer to (þeir höfðu lagit mál undir Njál);
~ undir trúnað e-s, to trust;
ok er þat mjök undir hann lagit, it depends much on him;
~ e-t undir þegnskap sinn, to assert on one’s honour;
~ e-t upp við e-n, to hand over to one;
~ e-t eigi langt upp, not to make much of, to make light of (eigi legg ek slíkt langt upp);
~ e-t við e-t, to add to (~ aðra tölu við aðra);
~ við líf sitt, höfuð sitt, to stake one’s life;
~ við sekt, to fix a fine;

 to lay oneself;
~ niðr, to lie down; to pass out of use, cease;
þeir höfðu lagizt til svefns, they were gone to sleep;
~ í sótt, to fall sick, take to bed (from illness);
~ á e-t, to fall upon (of robbers, beasts of prey);
~ á, to arise (mun sá orðrómr á ~, at);
~ fyrir, to take rest, lie down (lögðust þá fyrir bæði menn ok hestar af úviðri);
fyrir ~ um e-t, to give it up;
~ or ~ til sunds, to swim (Grettir lagðist nú inn á fjörðinn);
~ í hernað víking, to set out on a freebooting expedition;
~ út, to set out into the wilderness (as a highwayman);
~ e-t eigi undir höfuð, not to lay it under one’s pillow, not to neglect or forget it;
eigi lagðist mjök á með þeim brœðrum, they were not on good terms;
lítit leggst fyrir e-n, one has but a poor destiny (lítit lagðist nú fyrir kappann, því at hann kafnaði í stofureyk sem hundr);
recipr., ~ hendr á, to lay hands on one another;
~ hugi á, to take a liking for each other;
~ nær, to run close up to one another (of two boats);
~ niðr fé, to slaughter sheep (haf frjálst þat, sem þú hefir niður lagt af fé váru).

adverb. phrases:
koma e-u til ~ar or á ~, to bring about;
skipast á betri ~, to change to a better way;
á þá ~, in this wise, thus;
fram á ~, á ~ fram, further, all along; afterwards, for the time to come;

to play (við skulum nú ~ fyrst);
~ leik, to play a play;
~ tveim skjöldum, to play a double game;
~ at e-u, to play (amuse oneself) with (Katla sat ok lék at hafri sínum);
~ at tafli, to play at chess;
~ sér, to play (hann lék sér þá enn á gólfi með öðrum börnum);

of flame, fire, water, waves, to play lightly about or over, lick (eldr tók at ~ húsin);
eldrinn lék skjótt, the fire spread fast;
leikr hár hiti við himin sjálfan, the lofty blaze plays against the very heavens;
fig., e-m leikr e-t í skapi (í mun), one feels inclined to, has a mind to (þat leikr mér í skapi at kaupa Íslandsfar);

with preps.:
~ á e-n, to play a trick upon (mjök hefir þú á oss leikit);
fig., lék þat orð á, at, it was rumoured, that;
e-m leikr öfund á, to envy;
e-m leikr hugr á, to have a mind to;
þar leikr minn hugr á, my mind is bent upon that;
hón segir föður sínum um hvat at ~ er, she told her father how things stood;
~ e-n út, esp. in pp., to ill-treat (konungr sér nú Áka, hversu hann er út leikinn);
~ við e-n, to play with one;
~ við, to continue (meinit hafð lengi við leikit);

refl., ~st, to be performed, done;
ef þat má ~st, if this can be done;
leikst á e-n, it goes against one, he gets the worst of it (mjök hefir leikizt á minn hluta);
~st við, to play one against the other, play a match;
höfðu þeir leikizt við barna leikum, meðan þeir vóru ungir, they had been playmates.

(-s, -ar),  game, play, sport;
láta (vinna) leikinn, to lose (win) the game;
á nýja leik, anew;
við illan leik, narrowly, with a narrow escape (þeir kómust þar yfir við illan leik);
hann segir þeim hvat leika (gen.), hvat í leikum er, he told them what the game was, what was the matter;
~ er görr til e-s, a person is aimed at, is the mark of an attack;
gera leik til e-s, to mock one.

with preps.:
~ at e-m, to seek for a person, = ~ e-s (var þá ~t at lækninum);
~ at um e-t = ~ eptir um e-t;
~ á e-n, to attack or assail one, to blame or reprehend one;
~ á, to try, make an attempt to get a thing (hvar skulu vit á ~?);
~ á um kvánfang, to look for a wife;
~ eptir e-u, to seek for;
~ eptir máli, to follow a case, take it up;
~ eptir um e-t, to inquire into;
~ eptir við e-n, to inquire of a person;
~ fyrir sér, to save oneself by flight (leggjum á flótta, verðr nú hverr at ~ fyrir sér);
~ e-s í, to try, make an attempt (var þá margs í ~t);
~ til e-s, to seek for, = ~ e-s (aldri ~ði hann til griða né undankomu);
~ um e-t = ~ at um e-t, to examine (var þá ~t um sár manna);
to try to get or effect (~ um sættir);
~ e-s við e-n, to apply to one for a thing (~ ráða við e-n);
~ við e-t, to try;
~ við för, to try to get away;
~ við at gøra e-t, to try to do a thing (hann vildi við ~ at mæla);

host, folk, people;
fyrða l., the people;
þeir vóru allir eins liðs, they were all of one party;
vera einn sins liðs, to be alone;

with preps. and advs.:
~ á e-m, to lie heavy on, weigh upon, oppress (~ á mér hugir stórra manna);
~ á hálsi e-m, to hang on one’s neck, blame one;
~ á e-m, to be fated to one (þat lá á konungi, at hann skyldi eigi lifa um tíu vetr);
~ á e-u, to attend, be connected with (á þessum ráðum ~ stórmeinbugir);
to be urgent, of importance, pressing (A. kvað honum eigi á ~ þat at vita);
liggr honum ekki á, it does not matter to him;
~ á úknyttum, to pursue wicked courses;
~ eptir, to be left undone (skal ekki eptir ~ þat, sem vér megum þeim veita);
~ fyrir e-m, to lie in wait for;
~ fyrir, to be in store for, or open to, one;
þœtti mér þat ráð fyrir ~, at þú sendir menn, that the best thing would be to send men;
~ hjá e-rri, to lie with (lá ek hjá dóttur þinni);
~ í e-u, to stick or sink in (lágu hestarnir á kafi í snjónum);
~ í, to stick fast in mire or bog (liggr í hestrinn undir þeim);
~ í hernaði, víkingu, to be engaged in warfare;
~ niðri, to lie untold (nú skal þat eigi niðri ~, er honum er þó mest vegsemd í);
~ saman, to be adjacent (lágu saman skógar þeirra Lopts);
~ til e-s, to belong to (naut ok sauðir, lá þat til Atleyjar);
bœtr ~ til alls, there is atonement for every case;
to be due or proper (þótti þat til ~ at taka af honum tignina);
~ til byrjar, to lie by for a fair wind;
~ til hafs, to lie ready for sea (lá biskup til hafs sex vikur);
~ um e-n, to lie in wait for;
~ um e-t, to be bent upon (hann liggr um þat nótt ok dag at veita yðr líflát);
~ undir e-n or e-m, to be subject to, belong to (þessi lönd ~ undir Danakonung);
hlutr e-s liggr undir, one is worsted;
~ úti, to lie out, not in a house (sumir lágu úti á fjöllum með bú sín);
