~ e-t upp, to read up (þessi sömu bréf lét erkibiskup upp ~ í Danmörku);
~ við e-n, to be able to master one (muntu nú einn við mik ~);
~ e-t við sik, to make up one’s mind;
~ yfir e-u, to rule, govern;

	to be resolved, settled (eigi mun þetta ~ þessu sinni);
to turn out;
réðst mikit mannfall, there was a great slaughter;
~ at hjóni, to hire oneself out, enter service (réðst hann þá þar at hjóni);
~ á, to come to blows (svá kom, at þeir ~ á);
~ frá, to leave;
~ í e-t, to enter, undertake;
~ í hernað, to go on warfare;
hann réðst í flokk með þeim, he joined him self to their company;
~ í braut, to go away;
~ ór hernaði, give up, leave off freebooting;
~ til e-s, to remove to one (bið Una selja jörð sína ok ~ hingat til mín);
~ til ferðar með e-m, to undertake a journey with one;
~ til skips með e-m, to sail with one;
réðst til allgiptusamliga, it turned out very happily;
~ um við e-n, to consult with one (Pálnatóki réðst um við félaga sína);
vel hefir ór ráðizt, things have turned out well.

ride, riding (var honum þá fenginn hestr til ~ar);
vera einn í ~, to ride alone;
þeir vóru beztir hestar at ~, they were the best of riding-horses;
var þá ok sén ~ þeirra, they were seen riding;

to run (rakkar þar ~);
~ í köpp við e-n, to run a race with;
hón á þann hest, er rennr lopt ok lög, that runs through the air and over the sea;
~ e-m hvarf, to run out of one’s sight;

with preps.:
~ af e-m, to leave one, pass away from one (reiði rann af honum);
~ á e-n, to come over one;
svefn, svefnhöfgi rennr á e-n, one falls asleep;
reiði rennr á e-n, one gets angry;
þá rann á byrr, then a fair wind arose;
~ eptir e-m, to run after one (þá var runnit eptir þeim, er flóttann ráku);
~ frá e-m, to run away from, leave one;
~ í e-t, to run into;
e-m rennr í skap, one is much (deeply) affected (er eigi trútt, at mér hafi eigi í skap runnit sonardauðinn);
~ saman, to heal up (þá var saman runninn leggrinn);
~ undir, to assist, give support (margar stoðir runnu undir, bæði frændr ok vinir);
~ upp, to originate (var þess ván, at illr ávöxtr mundi upp ~ af illri rót);
of the sun or daylight, to rise;
sól (dagr) rennr upp (cf. 5);

to slip, let loose;
~ veiðarfœri, to let the fishing-line run out;
Tjörvi renndi fyrir hann törgu, T. flung a target in his way;
impers., atgeirinum renndi gegnum skjöldinn, the halberd was run through the shield;
~ e-u niðr, to swallow;
~ grunum á e-t, to suspect;

to clear, empty;
~ búrit, to empty the pantry;
~ skip, to unload a ship;
~ höfn, to clear the harbour, leave the haven;
impers., hvernig skjótt ruddi samnaðinn, how the flock dispersed;

with preps.:
~ e-u á e-t, to throw, toss upon (þeir ruddu viðinum á hurðina);
~ e-m í brott, to drive away, sweep off;
~ sér til ríkis, to clear the way to a kingdom, obtain it by conquest;
~ til e-s, to clear the way for a thing, prepare for (ok ruddu þeir til likgraptarins);
~ e-u upp, to tear up (þeir ruddu upp jörðu ok grjóti);

the same;
the def. form is used both with and without the preceding art. (inn, in, it);
í sama húsi, in the same house;
hann var s. í boðum sinum, the same, unaltered;
svá fór sem samt sé, it turned out the same way;
komast í samt lag, to get into the same condition as before;
with dat., the same as;
kom Guðrún eigi síðan í sömu rekkju Ólafi, into the same bed as Olaf;
it sama, the same, likewise;

to say, tell (seg þú mér þat, er ek spyr þik);
þeir sögðu, at þeir skyldu aldri upp gefast, they said they would never yield;
~ e-m leið, to tell the way, esp. on the sea, to pilot;
~ tíðendi, to tell news;
impers. it is told (hér segir frá Birni bunu);
sem áðr sagði, as was told before;
~nda er allt vin sínum, all can be told to a friend;

law phrases;
~ sik í þing, lög, to declare oneself member of a community;
~ sik ór þingi, lögum, to declare oneself out of, withdraw from, a community;
~ skilit við konu, to declare oneself separated from, divorce, one’s wife;
~ fram sök, to declare one’s case;
~ lög, to recite the law, of the lögsögumaðr;

with preps., ~ e-n af e-u, to declare one off a thing, take it from him;
~ e-t á e-n, to impose on (bœta at þeim hluta, sem lög segði á hann); to announce (~ á reiði, úsátt sína);
~ eptir e-m, to tell tales of one;
~ frá e-u, to tell, relate;
Unnr, er ek sagða þér frá, U. of whom I told thee;
~ fyrir e-u, to prescribe (svá var með öllu farit, sem hann hafði fyrir sagt);
~ fyrir skipi, to bid God-speed to a ship;
~ e-t fyrir, to predict, foretell (~ fyrir úorðna hluti);
~ e-u sundr, í sundr, to break up, dissolve (~ sundr friði, frændsemi, hjúskap);
~ til e-s, to tell, inform of (segit honum ekki til, hvat þér hafit gört við hrossit);
~ til nafns síns, ~ til sín, to tell (give) one’s name;
~ upp e-t, to pronounce (~ upp dóm, gørð);
~ upp lög, to proclaim the law (from the law-hill);
~ e-n upp, to give one up;
~ upp e-u, to declare at an end (~ upp friði, griðum);
~ upp þjónustu við e-n, to leave one’s service;

refl., ~st, to declare of oneself;
hann sagðist þá vaka, he said that he was awake;
kristnir menn ok heiðnir sögðust hvárir ór lögum annarra, they declared themselves each out of the other’s laws;
láta (sér) ~st, to let oneself be spoken to, listen to reason;
impers., e-m segist svá, one’s tale runs so.

to hand over, deliver;
~ e-m e-t (Ásta selr honum sverðit);
~ e-t í hendr e-m, to make over to one (hann seldi búit í hendr Þorsteini);
~ vápn ór hendi sér, to give up (deliver) one’s weapons;
~ e-t fram, to deliver up;
~ e-m e-t til varðveizlu, to commit to another’s keeping (þér skulut nú ~ mér til varðveizlu vápn yður);
hann kvazt hvárki vildu ~ grið né taka, he said that he would neither give nor receive pardon;
~ e-m laun, to give reward, pay;
~ fé at láni, to lend money;
~ á leigu, to put out at interest;
~ á frest, to give on credit;

to sell, part with (hann seldi land sitt);
~ e-t við litlu (miklu) verði, to sell for a small (great) sum;
~ mansali, to sell into bondage;
þótti þeim konungr út seldr, a done man (= fram seldr);

after adverbs:
þar ~, where;
þangat ~, whither;
þaðan ~, whence (muntu þar þykkja sómamaðr ~ þú kemr);
hvar ~ hann kom, wheresoever he came;

to shape, compose, arrange;
to bring or put into, order;
~ hljóðfœri, to tune instruments;
~ sætt, to make peace (fyrr en sættin væri samið);

to reform, mend, put right;
konungr bœtti þeirra ok samdi siðu, the king mended, reformed their faith and manners;
hefir þú heldr samit þik ór því sem var, thou hast rather improved thyself;
setja ok ~ dramb to compose and set down one’s pride;
~ sik eptir e-u, to adapt oneself to, conform oneself to (hefir þú samit þik mjök eptir siðvenju útlendra manna);
at þeir semdi sína frændsemi eptir því sem vera ætti, that they should restore their relationship to a proper footing;

reflex., ~st, to be settled, agreed on (þat mál samdist á þá leið, at);
e-t semst með e-m, it is agreed on between them (samdist þetta með þeim);
impers. (selja man ek yngra sveininn, sem okkr semst);
~st e-u, to take to (samdist hón skildi ok sverði en við sauma ok borða).

in a distributive sense, ~ hverr, ~ hvárr, each by himself, each separately;
ef ~ ferr hverr várr, if each of us goes by himself;
stundum báðir samt, stundum ~ hvárr þeirra, sometimes each of them singly;
þat lið, er honum fylgði, flýr ~ hvat, in all directions.

to seat, set, place, put (hann setti sveininn í kné konungi; hón var í haug sett);
~ e-n inn, to put in prison;
~ inn fénað, svín, hross, to pen up, take in;
~ dóm, to set a court;
~ tjöld, herbúðir, to set up tents;
~ grundvöll til kirkju, to lay the foundation of;
~ borð, to set up tables;
~ e-m gisla, to give one hostages;

impers. it settles;
þegar er niðt setti moldrykit (acc.), when the dust settled;
jarl (acc.) setti svá rauðan sem blóð (dreyrrauðan), the earl turned red as blood;
þá setti at honum hósta, a fit of coughing seized him;
þá setr at henni grát mikinn, she bursts into tears;

with preps. and advs.:
~ e-n af kirkju, to put out of the church, excommunicate;
~ e-n af ríki, to depose one;
~ e-n af lífi, af sinni eign, to deprive one of life, of one’s property;
~ e-n aptr, hold one back, check (hann setti þá harðliga aptr, er á Þráin leituðu);
~ at e-m, to attack;
~ e-t á skrá, to enter, set in a scroll;
~ á sik hjálm, to put on a helmet;
~ á, to push (H. bað Ketil ganga fyrir skut ok ~ á);
~ e-n eptir, to leave one behind;
~ fram skip, to launch a ship;
~ e-t fyrir, to prescribe (eptir fyrir-settri skipan);
~ þvert nei fyrir, to deny flatly (Þyri setti þvert nei fyrir, at hón myndi giptast gömlum konungi);
~ e-n í fjötur, bönd, to put in fetters;
hann hafði sett spjót í völlinn hjá sér, he had stuck his spear in the ground beside him;
~ e-n niðr, to make one sit down, put one down (H. þreif til hans ok setti hann niðr hjá sér);
~ niðr lík, to lay a corpse in earth, bury it;
~ niðr mál, deilu, vandræði, to settle it;
~ e-t saman, to put together, set up (~ bú saman);
to compose, write (eptir bókum þeim, er Snorri setti saman);
~ e-n til bókar, to set one to learn;
~ e-n til ríkis, to put one on the throne;
~ upp, to raise, erect, put up (~ upp skurðgoð);
~ upp segl, to hoist sail;
~ upp boga, to bend a bow;
~ skip upp, to draw a ship up, ashore;
~ skip út, to launch, = ~ skip fram;
~ e-t við e-u, to set against (þat þótti höfðingjum ofrausn ok settu mjök hug sinn við);
to bet (ek set við hundrað marka silfrs, at hann ríðr mik eigi af baki);
~ e-n yfir e-t, to put one over, at the head of (H. konungr setti Eystein jarl yfir Vestfold);

 to seat oneself, take a seat;
~ niðr, to sit down (þeir settust niðr á völlinn);
~ á tal við e-n, to sit down to talk with a person;
to take up one’s abode (fór hann í Odda ok settist þar);
~ um kyrrt, to settle oneself to rest;
~ at í ríki sínu, to settle in one’s kingdom;
~ aptr af brottferðinni, to desist from the journey;
~ eptir, to remain behind;
ek hefi hér upp sezt at þér, I have taken up my abode with thee;
~ fyrir, to withstand, oppose;
~ fyrir e-t, to put oneself at the head of, take in one’s hand (síðan settist Sturla fyrir mál þeirra);
~ hjá e-u, not to meddle with, take no part in (settist Sturla hjá öllum vandræðum, er þar af gerðust);
~ um borg, to lay siege to a town;
~ upp, to raise oneself to a sitting posture;
~ yfir e-t, to seize upon (nú ~ þeir yfir staðinn ok alla staðarins eign);
of the sun, ~, to set, go down (þat er víða á því landi, at sól sezt eigi um nætr);
dagr sezt, the day ends;
sólu (degi) er sett, the sun (day) has set.

to sail;
~ af landi, to stand off the land;
~ at landi, to stand in to land;
~ í haf, á haf, til hafs, to stand out to sea;
~ á skip, to run into a ship;
~ meira, to set more sails;
~ um e-n, to sail by one;
~ um Eyrarsund, to sail through the Sound;
~ um, to get under weigh, set sail (þegar er þeir höfðu um siglt, þá gaf þeim byr);
with the course in acc., þat er sögn manna, at Skopti hafi fyrstr Norðmanna siglt Njörvasund, was the first Northman to sail through N.;
with the weather in acc. (þeir ~ norðr um Sognsæ byr góðan ok bjart veðr);
veðr ~nda, weather fit for sailing;

with hvárr and hverr in a distributive sense;
tók sitt langskip hvárr þeira, each of the two took a long ship;
~ vetr þá hvárr heimboð at öðrum, they visited each other, winter about;
sínu ~i at hvárs búum, alternately on each other’s estates;
~ veg hvárr, one each way;
þykkir nökkut ~ veg hváru, each took his own view of the matter;
ferr ~ veg hverr um skóginn, they (all) went each his own way in the wood;
skulu vaka ~ þriðjung nætr hverir tveir, two and two in turn.

 time;
eitt ~, einu ~i, one time, once (þat var eitt ~, at Egill gekk til elda at verma sik);
eitthvert ~, einhverju ~i = eitt ~, einu ~i;
einhverju ~i bar svá til, at, one time it happened that;
ekki ~, engu ~i, never;
aldri ~, never more (aldri skaltu koma í mína rekkju ~ síðan);
þat ~, that time;
þessu ~i, this time;
(í) annat ~, öðru ~i, a second time, again;
hit fyrra ~, the first time;
hit þriðja ~, for the third time;
at ~i, for the present (þeir skilja tal sitt at ~i);
um ~, once (veg þú aldri meirr í inn sama knérunn en um ~);
for this one time (ek mun leysa þik ór vandræði þessu um ~);
um ~s sakir, for this once (eigi vil ek synja þér um ~s sakir þessa);
dat. pl., sjau ~um, seven times;
endr ok ~um, now and then, from time to time;
nökkurum ~um, several times.

to mind, care for, heed;
ekki sinni ek hégóma þínum, I heed not thy idle talk;
ekki sinni ek fé (ef nökkurr vill ganga í málit), I do not mind the money;
hón ~ði um engan hlut, she cared about nothing;
vera e-m ~ndi, to be attentive to one, take care of.

to stay, sojourn;
~ heima, to stay at home (sitr Gunnarr nú heima nökkura hríð);
~ kyrr, to remain quiet, stay at home;
~ í festum, to sit as one’s betrothed (of the bride between the betrothal and wedding);
to reside (Haraldr konungr sat optast á Rogalandi);
to sit fishing (þeir vóru komnir á þær vastir, er hann var vanr at ~);

with preps.:
~ at e-u, to sit busy with a thing;
~ at sumbli, to sit at a banquet;
~ at tafli, to sit at chess;
~ at fé, nautum, to tend sheep, cattle;
~ at málum, to sit over a case, debate it;
~ á stefnu, to be in the chair at a meeting;
~ á sannindum, to withhold (conceal) the truth;
~ á sér, to control oneself, keep down one’s temper (Hallgerðr sat mjök á sér um vetrinn);
~ á svikrædum, svikum við e-n, to plot against one;
~ fyrir, to be on the spot (úvíst er at vita, hvar úvinir ~ á fleti fyrir);
~ fyrir e-u, to be a hindrance to (~ fyrir sœmd e-s);
to be exposed to, have to bear (~ fyrir hvers manns ámæli);
~ fyrir ádrykkju e-s, to be one’s drinking-mate;
~ fyrir svörum, to stand questions, be the spokesman;
~ fyrir málum, to lead the discussion;
~ fyrir e-m, to lie in ambush (in wait) for;
~ hjá e-u, to be present at (~ hjá ráðagerðum e-s);
to sit idly by (þú munt þó drepa vilja bróður minn, ok er þat skömm, ef ek sit hjá);
~ inni, to sit in prison;
~ til e-s, to wait;
er þar til at ~, we may wait till then;
~ um e-t, to watch for an opportunity;
þeir sátu um at rengja, they watched to find a flaw in the proceedings;
to plot against (hann sitr um ríki hans);
~ um líf e-s, to seek one’s life;
~ um e-n, to lie in wait for, waylay;
~ um e-u, to be busy with (~ um nauðsynjamálum);
~ um borðum, to sit at table;
~ undir e-u, to be subject to;
~ yfir e-u, to sit over a thing, be busy with (ekki mun ek lengr yfir þessu ~);
~ yfir drykkju, borðum, to sit drinking, at table;
to attend to (~ yfir málum manna);
~ yfir kvæðum, to listen to songs;
~ yfir e-u, to take possession of;
láta slíka ~ yfir váru, to let such persons withhold our property from us;
to bear down (hann sat yfir virðland);
~ yfir skörðum hlut, to suffer a loss of right;
~ yfir sjúkum manni, to sit up with (nurse) a sick person, esp. a woman in labour;

with preps.:
~ af e-m, to look away from, be (do) without (ek þóttumst ekki mega af þér ~ sakir ástríkis);
~ á e-t, to look on (nú em ek í höll kominn á þik sjálfan at ~);
to take in hand, take care of (Þorgils kvað sér eigi skylt at ~ á þat mál);
~ á með e-m, to take care of (þess vil ek biðja, at þér ~it á með Högna syni mínum);
impers., sér á, it can be seen (lítt sér þat þá á, at þú hafir fundit Svein konung);
~ eptir e-u, ~ eptir um e-t, to look after (var lítt sét eptir um skattlöndin þau, er fjarri lágu);
eiga eptir e-u at ~, to have to look after a thing (hann mun þar eptir hefndum eiga at ~);
~ fram, to see into the future (fram sé ek lengra);
~ e-t fyrir, to foresee (hann þóttist ~ fyrir, at hann mundi fella fénað sinn);
~ fyrir e-u, to take care of, provide for (verðr hverr fyrir sér at ~);
man ek þá ~ fyrir málinu, then I will look after the matter;
~ fyrir e-m, to put one out of the way;
~ í e-t, to see into, see through (sér hann ekki í þetta);
to take into consideration (Hrafnkell sá eigi mjök í kostnað);
~ til e-s, to become aware of (ef þér ~it þá ekki til minna ferða, þá fari þér heim);
to look for, expect (~ til trausts, launa, fulltings);
to look after, see to, take care of (guð sér til kristni sinnar);
~ til með e-m, to take care of, see to one;
~ um e-t, to see after. take care of (~ um bú, fémál e-s);
~ um með e-m = ~ til með e-m (Höskuldr sá um með honum);
~ um fyrir e-u, to look after, see to (eigi mun nú fyrir öllu verða um sét);
~ við e-m, to look at one (konungr sá við honum ok mælti);
to beware of, guard oneself against (mun oss vandgætt til við honum at ~);
sé nú við, beware!;
~ við um e-t, to guard oneself, be cautious as to (hafa þessir menn meirr sét við um kaupin en ek hugða);
~ yfir e-t, to look over, survey, look after (~ yfir akra sína);
to see a way to (hann sá eigi yfir, at þeir kœmist til bœja);
eigi mátti yfir ~, hvern veg hníga mundi orrostan, one could not see how the fight would end;

 to fear (fátt hygg ek yðr ~);
láta á ~, to let it appear, show signs of (þeir þögðu yfir ok létu ekki á ~);
~ fyrir, to look before one, be cautious (hitt mundi mitt ráð at hrapa ekki ferðinni, ~ heldr fyrir);
~ lítt fyrir, to be impetuous, rash (er Agli of mjök ættgengt at ~ lítt fyrir);
~ um, to look about (þá litu allir út nema konungr, hann stóð ok sást eigi um);
impers., at þeim hefði kynliga um sézt, that they had made a strange oversight in this;
e-m sést yfir, one makes a mistake, overlooks a thing;
þeim mun hafa yfir sézt, they must have made a mistake;
recipr., ~, to see one another;
vit munum aldri ~ síðan, we shall never see one another more;
ef þit sjáist tveir á, if ye fight it out among yourselves;
~ til, to look to one another (sást til síðan, áðr í sundr hyrfi);
pass., þeir sást aldri síðan, they were never seen afterwards.

condition of mind, temper, mood;
þat er ekki mitt ~, it is not my turn of mind;
vera (með) illu ~i, to be in an angry mood;
bregða ~i við e-t, renna e-m í ~, to take to heart, be much affected by (rann honum mjök í ~ sá atburðr);
koma ~i við e-n, to agree with, het on with (engi kann við mik ~i koma);
koma ~i (eiga ~) saman, to have minds alike (þit eigit meirr ~ saman);
hafa ekki ~ til e-s, to have no mind to do a thing;
hógværr í ~i, mild of temper;
lítill í ~i, of small mind, faint-hearted;
gera sér e-t í ~, to take into one’s head;
vera e-m (vel) at ~i, vera nær ~i e-s, to be to one’s mind;
e-t fellr e-m vel í ~, one likes, is pleased with;
gera e-t til ~s e-m, to do a thing to please one.

race, run;
renna ~ við e-n, to run a race with one;
ríða á ~, to ride at full speed;
hlaupa á ~, to set off running (hann kastar niðr verkfœrum ok hleypr á ~ heim til bœjar);
gera (or renna) ~ at e-u, to make a run at a thing;
skapa (or skopa) ~, to take a run;
taka e-t á ~i, to catch it on the move (G. tók knöttinn á ~i);

space of time (þat var eitt ~, at);
nökkut ~, for some time (Njáll þagnaði nökkut ~);
um ~, for a while;
annat ~, after a while, every now and then (hann hafði niðri aðra höndina at jörðu ok bregðr henni annat ~ at nösum);
vera á œsku ~i, á léttasta ~i aldrs, to be in the prime of life;
vera af œsku ~i, to be no longer a young man.

to make to slam, clash, with dat.;
~ aptr hurðu, to slam the door;
~ lófum saman, to clap the hands;
~ hrömmum yfir, to clutch;
~ e-u niðr, to throw down with a clash;

to strike, smite;
þær skelldu skip mitt, they shattered my ship;
~ af honum höndina, to cut off his hand;
~ fót undan, to smite off the foot;

with preps. and advs.:
~ e-t af, to cut off;
~ af manni, to be reserved or reticent;
~ lítt af manni, to speak one’s mind;
~ niðr kvikfé, to slaughter the live stock (for want of fodder);
~ ór e-u, to decide, settle (~ ór vanda-málum);
nú er þat vili várr, at einn veg skeri ór, that the case be settled;
~ upp herör, þingboð, to dispatch a war-arrow, gathering-stick;
~ upp akr, to reap a field;

refl., ~st, to stretch, branch, of a fjord, valley;
fjörðr skarst langt inn í landit, stretched far into the land;
höfðarnir skárust á víxl, the headlands stretched across, overlapped one another;
s. í setgeira-brœkr, to put on a mzn’s breeches;
ef nökkut skerst í, if anything happens;
skarst allt í odda með þeim, þat sem við bar, they fell at odds about everything that happened;
s. ór e-u máli, to withdraw (shrink) from a cause (gangi nú allir til mín ok sveri eiða, at engi skerist ór þessu máli);
s. undan e-u, to refuse, decline doing a thing (mun lokit okkrum samförum, ef þú skerst undan förinni);
s. undan, to hang back (Þeir fýstu hann at sættast, en hann skarst undan).

impers.; þar er leidir (acc.) skildi, þá skildi ok slóðina, where the roads parted, there the tracks too parted;
mundi ~ vegu þeirra, their ways would diverge;
með þessu skilr skipti þeirra, thus ended their dealings;
it differs, mikit (acc.) skilr hamingju okkra, there is a wide difference between our fortunes;
þá skilr á um e-t, they disagree about a thing;
ef skrár skilr á, if the scrolls differ;

to set apart, reserve for one (þó at konungr hafi mér skilit eignir minar eða landsvist í Orkneyjum);
eiga e-t skilit, to have reserved, stipulated (þat átta ek skilit við þik, at);
~ sér e-t, to reserve to oneself (jarðir hafði hann hygt ok skilit sér allar landskyldir);
~ e-t í sætt, to lay down, stipulate, in an agreement (þat var skilit í sæt- várri, at);

with preps.:
~ e-t á við e-n, to stipulate (þó vil ek ~ á við þik einn hlut);
~ e-t eptir, to leave behind;
~ e-t frá e-u, to separate from (hann skildi sik sjálfr frá ríki ok fór í klaustr);
to exeinpt from (hverr maðr skyldi gjalda konungi fimm aura, sá er eigi væri frá því skiliðr);
~ fyrir e-u, to formulate, dictate (~ fyrir eiðstafinum);
~ fyrir heiti, to formulate, pronounce a vow;
~ með mönnum, to part, separate (nótt skildi þá með þeim);
impers., skilr með þeim, they part (eptir þetta skildi með þeim);
~ e-t í sundr, to part asunder (hinir skildu í sundr skipin ok gerðu hlið í millum skipanna);
~ e-t til, to stipulate (vil ek ok til ~, at);
~ e-t undan, to except, to make a reservation for (at undan skildum, heraðssektum);
~ e-t undan e-m, to deprive one of;
~ e-t undir e-n, to leave it to one (Njáll kvaðst þat vilja ~ undir Höskuld);
~ e-n undir sætt, to include one in an agreement (hversu marga menn viltu ~ undir sættir okkar);
~ undir eið, to make an oath with reservation;
~ við e-n, to part with, put away;
~ við konu, bónda, to divorce one’s wife, one’s husband;
segja skilit við konu, to declare oneself separated from;

 to separate, break up (skildist þá ok riðlaðist fylkingin);
~ við e-n, e-t, to part from, forsake (hann kveðst aldri við Kára skyldu ~);
recipr. to part company (skildust þeir með blíðskap, feðgar);
impers., e-m skilst e-t, one perceives, understands (nú skaltu vita, hvárt mér hafi skilizt);
láta sér e-t ~, to let oneself be convinced of (Björn lét sér þat eigi ~).

þat skiptir engu (litlu, miklu), it is of no (little, great) importance, it makes no (little, great) difference;
mik skiptir engu, it is of no moment to me, does not concern me;
s. máli, to be of importance (þótti henni allmiklu máli s., at þér tœkist stórmannliga);
eiga máli at s. um e-t, to have a right to deal with, be concerned about, a thing;
þat mun tveimr s., it will turn out in one of two ways;
sér Pálnatoki, at mun tveimr um s., that it will turn out one way or the other;
þat skipti mörgum hundruðum, it was a matter of (it amounted to) many hundreds;
sitr Ólafr nú at búi sínu, svá at vetrum skipti, for several years;

with preps., s. sér af e-u, to take part in, concern oneself with a thing (Glúmr skipti sér ekki af um búsýslu);
s. e-u fyrir e-t, to exchange a thing for another;
undarliga skiptit ér til, ye do strangely with things;
impers., skiptir e-n veg til, it turns out, comes to pass (þannig skipti til sem úlíkligra mundi þykkja);
s. um e-u, to change;
s. um trúnaði sínum, to go over to the other side;
s. um e-t, to change (s. um bústað, lánardróttna, nafn);
s. um, to come to a crisis, turn one way or other (skjótt mun um s.);
s. e-u við e-n, to exchange with one another (s. höggum við e-n);
s. orðum við e-n, to bandy words with one;
s. ríki við e-n, to share the kingdom with (sá hann engan annan sinn kost en s. ríki við Harald);

refl., ~st, to divide themselves, disperse (skiptust þeir, snøru sumir norðr); to change (þá skiptust tungur á Englandi, er Vilhjálmr bastarðr vann England);
recipr., ~st e-u við, to make an exchange;
s. gjöfum (höggum) við, to exchange presents (blows);
s. við um róðr, to row by turns.

to shoot with a weapon, with dat. (~ öru, spjóti, kólfi);
vera skotinn spjóti í gegnum, to be shot through with a spear;
~ af boga, to shoot with a bow;
with the object shot at in acc. (~ dýr, mann, sel, fugl);
~ at e-m, til e-s, to shoot at one;
~ til hœfis, to shoot at a mark;

to shoot push or shove quickly;
~ brú af, to draw the bridge off or away;
~ skildi fyrir sik, to put a shield before one;
~ loku fyrir, to shoot the bolt, lock the door;
~ frá lokum, to unlock;
~ e-u fyrir borð, to ‘shoot’ overboard;
~ skipum á vatn, to launch ships;
~ báti, to launch a boat from the shore;
~ útan báti, to shove out a boat;
~ hesti uridir e-n, to put a horse under one, to mount him;
var mér hér skotit á land, I was put ashore here;
~ e-u niðr, to thrust it down (hann skaut svá fast niðr skildinum, at);
~ e-m brott or undan, to let one escape;
~ undan peningum, to abstract, embezzle money;
~ e-u í hug e-m to suggest to one (þá skaut guð því ráði í hug þeim);
~ upp hvítum skildi, to hoist a white shield;
~ upp vita, ~ eldi í vita, to light up a beacon;
~ land-tjaldi, to pitch a tent;
~ á fylking, to draw up in battle array;
~ á husþingi, to call a meeting together;
~ á eyrendi, to make a speech;
~ fótum undir sik, to take to one’s heels, to run;
barnit skaut öndu upp, the child began to breathe;
~ e-u of öxl, to throw off one’s shoulder;

impers., e-u skýtr upp, it shoots up, emerges, comes forth;
upp skýtr jörðunni þá ór sænum, then the earth rises from the sea;
skaut upp jörðu dag frá degi, the earth appeared day by day (as the snow melted);
þó at þér skyti því í hug, though it shot into thy mind, occurred to thee;
þeim skaut skelk í bringu, they were panic-stricken;
sem kólfi skyti, swift as a dart;

 to shoot, start, move quickly;
hann skauzt á fœtr, he started to his feet;
Björn skauzt aptr at baki Kára, B. sprang back again behind K.;
at menn hans skytist eigi frá honum, lest his men should slip away, desert;
hann vildi ljósta Gretti, en hann skauzt undan, he avoided the blow;
~ e-m, to fail;
margir skutust honum, many forsook him;
skutust þá margir við þórð í trúnaðinum, many proved false to Thord;
hann var nökkut við aldr, ok skauzt á fótum, and tottered on his legs;
e-m skýzt yfir um e-t, one over-looks, fails to notice a thing (þeim hafði yfir skotizt um þetta);
recipr., skótast á, to exchange shots.

(gen. skjaldar, dat. skildi; pl. skildir, acc. skjöldu),  m. shield;
hafa e-n at skildi fyrir sér, to have another as a shield before one;
bera efra (hærra) skjöld, to have the best of it, to gain the day;
þjóna undir þann skjöld, to serve under that standard;
leika tveim skjöldum, to play a double game;
koma í opna skjöldu, to attack in flank (from the left);
skjóta skildi yfir e-n, to protect one.

 (gen. skammar, pl. skammir),  f. shame;
lifa við ~, to live in shame;
er þat ~ at sitja hjá, it is a shame to sit idly by;
fœra ~ at e-m, gøra e-m ~, to bring dishonour on one;
bíða ~, to suffer disgrace;
veiða at ~, to be put to shame.

~ hörpu, fiðlu, to strike the harp, fiddle;
~ slag, to strike up a tune;
~ leik, to strike up, begin, a game;
~ vef, to strike the web, to weave;

fig., ~ kaupi, to strike a bargain;
~ máli í sátt, to refer a matter to arbitration;
~ fylking, to draw up a line of battle;
~ hring um, to surround;
~ manngarð, mannhring, to form a ring of men round;
~ eldi í e-t, to set fire to;
~ landtjöldum, to pitch a tent, or also, to strike a tent, take it down;
~ festum, to unmoor a ship;
~ netjum, to put out the nets;
~ hundum lausum, to slip the hounds;

with preps.:
~ e-t af, to cut off;
~ e-n af, to kill, slaughter;
~ á e-t, to take to a thing;
~ á glens ok glúmur, to take to play and sport;
~ á heit, to take to making a vow;
~ e-u á sik, to take upon one-self;
~ á sik sótt, to feign illness;
~ á sik úlfúð, to show anger or ill-will;
ekki skaltu slíku á þik ~, do not betake thyself to that;
impers., sló á hann hlátri, he was taken with a fit of laughter;
sló ótta á marga, many were seized with fear;
því slær á, at, it so happens that;
ljóssi sleri (= sló) fyrir hann, a light flashed upon him;
~ í deilu, to begin quarrelling (eitt kveld, er þeir drukku, slógu þeir í deilu mikla);
impers., slær í e-t, it arises;
slær þegar í bardaga, it came to a fight;
~ niðr e-u, to put an end to;
nú er niðr slegit allri vináttu, now there is an end to all friendship;
~ sér niðr, to lie down, take to one’s bed;
~ e-n niðr, to kill;
~ e-u saman, to join (þeir ~ þá saman öllu liðinu í eina fylking);
~ til e-s, to aim a blow at one, strike at one;
~ undan höfuð-bendunum, to slacken the stays;
~ e-u upp, to spread a report;
~ upp herópi, to raise the war-cry;
impers., loganum sló upp ór keröldunum, the flame burst out of the vessels;
~ út e-u, to pour out (þá er full er mundlaugin, gengr hón ok slær út eitrinu);
~ e-u við, to take into use (þá var slegit við öllum búnaði);
~ við segli, to spread the sail;
ek hefi þó náliga öllu við slegit, því er ek hefi í minni fest, I have put forth almost all that I recollected;
~ beizli við hest, to put a bridle on a horse;
e-u slær yfir, it comes over, arises (slær yfir þoku svá myrkri, at engi þeirra sá annan);

~ aptr, to draw back (gæta þess, at eigi slœgist aptr liðit);
~ á e-t, to take to a thing (~ á svikræði við e-n);
~ á tal við e-n, to enter into conversation with;
~ á bak e-m, to go behind another;
~ fram, to move (rush) forward;
slóst hón fram at eldinum, she rushed to the fireside;
~ í för með e-m, to join one in a journey;
~ í sveit með e-m, to join one’s party;
slógust í Suðreyjar víkingar, vikings invaded the Hebrides;
~ til ferðar með e-m = ~ í för með e-m.

to turn, with dat.;
snýr jarl þangat herinum, the earl turns his host thither;
~ úfriði á hendr e-m, to begin hostilities against one;
~ aptr ferð sinni, to turn back;

to turn, go;
sneru þeir þá yfir ána, then they went across the river;
þeir snerú í móti þeim ok börðust við þá, they turned against them and fought with them;
~ aptr, to turn back;
~ brott, to go away (maðrinn sneri þá brott);

with preps.,
~ at e-m, to turn on one;
snúm vér nú at þeim, let us turn upon them;
~ at brúðhlaupi, to prepare for;
halt svá hendi yfir honum, at øngri hefnd sé til hans snúit, protect him so that no vengeance may befall him;
~ e-u til leiðar, to bring about;
skiptir mik miklu, hversu þú vilt til ~, what turn thou wilt take;
~ e-u um, to turn upside down, upset (um snýr þú öllum, sætunum); to change completely (hón kvaðst hafa ætlat at ~ þar um lands-lagi öllu);
~ undan, draw back, retreat, flee (en er Baglar sá þat, þá sneru þeir undan);

 to turn oneself (hafði Gunnarr snúizt í hauginum);
~ at e-m, í móti e-m, við e-m, to turn upon one, face about, to meet an attack;
svá snerist, at þeir kómust í engan lífs-háska, it turned out so, that they got into no danger of life;
~ til leiðar, to turn to the right way;
~ til e-s um e-t, to turn to one for a thing (er þat líkast, at hann snúist til várrar ættar um vinfengit);
~ undan, to draw back (síðan vildi A. undan ~).

 (pl. -ir),  f. sickness, illness, disease;
taka (or fá) s., to fall ill, be taken ill;
kasta á sik s., to feign illness;
kenna (or kenna sér) sóttar, to feel the symptoms of illness;
pl. pains of labour (at sú mær hafði miklar sóttir).

to start, spring;
s. af baki, to spring off horseback;
s. á fœtr, s. upp, to start to one’s feet, jump up;
spratt upp lássinn, up sprang the lock;
spratt henni fótr ok féll hón, she slipped and fell;
spratt þat upp af heimamönnum, at, it was rumoured that;

to ask;
G. spurði, hvat hann vildi þá láta at gera, G. asked what he wished them to do;
with gen., ~ e-n tíðenda, to ask tidings of one;
~ e-n ráðs, to ask advice of, consult one;
H. spurði margs ór brennunni, H. asked much about the burning;
with preps., ~ e-n af e-u, at e-u, at um e-t, um e-t, to ask one about a thing;
~ at e-m, to inquire after one;
~ e-n at nafni, to ask one his name;
~ eptir, to ask, inquire (spurði hón eptir, hvat íslenzkra manna væri á skipi);
spurði Höskuldr dóttur sína ekki eprit, H. did not ask his daughter’s consent;

refl., ~st, to be heard of or reported, to get abroad;
til Þórveigar spurðist þat, at hon lá hætt, it was told of Th. that she was very ill;
impers., mér spyrst á þann veg, I am told;
hefir til þessa skips aldregi spurzt, nothing has since been heard of this ship;
~st fyrir um e-t, to inquire about (þeir spurðust þá fyrir um ferðir Ólafs konungs);
recipr. to ask one another (~st tíðenda).

‘stead’, place, spot;
fimmtán í hvárum stað, fifteen in each place;
í einum stað í Englandi, somewhere in England;
skipta í tvá, fjóra staði, to divide into two, four parts;
fara af stað, to go away, leave;
hafa sik af stað, to absent oneself;
bíða e-s ór stað, to wait on the spot, wait till one is attacked (Baglar biðu eigi högganna ór stað, ok flýðu þeir upp fyrir norðan bœinn);
ráða e-u til staðar, to settle;
gefa e-u stað, to stop;
þeir gefa eigi stað ferðinni, they stopped not on their journey;
gefa staðar, to stop, halt;
þá er sá íss gaf staðar ok rann eigi, when that ice stopped and flowed no more;
nema stað or staðar, to stop (hér munum vér stað nema);
leita staðar, to seek a place to ease oneself;

adverbially, í stað, þegar í stað, on the spot, at once;
rétt í stað, just now;
í marga staði, in many respects;
í alla staði, in every respect;
í engan stað, no-ways;
í annan stað, on the other hand, secondly;
í staðinn, instead;
alls staðar, everywhere;
annars staðar, elsewhere;
nökkurs staðar, anywhere;

to stand, opp. to sitja or liggja (hann stóð við vegginn);
koma ~ndi niðr, to come down on one’s feet;
skal mik niðr setja ~nda, in a standing position;
~ fast, to stand fast;
~ höllum fœti, to stand slanting;

to stand in a certain way, project, trend (fjögur horn ok stóðu fagrt, hit þriðja stóð í lopt upp);
stendr inn straumrinn, the tide (current) stands in;
blóðbogi stóð ór hvárutveggja eyranu, a stream of blood gushed out of both his ears;
kallar hann betr ~ veðrit at fara landhallt, that the wind stands better for making land;
stóð vindr af landi, the wind blew from the land;
~ grunnt, to be shallow (vinátta okkur stendr grunnt);

with preps.:
~ af e-u, to proceed from, be caused by (opt stendr illt af kvenna hjali);
vil ok ek eigi, at af mér standi brigð okkarrar vináttu, nor do I wish to be the cause of a breach in our friendship;
~ af e-u við e-n, to give up, cede to one;
impers., segir hann honum, hvernig af stóð um ferð hans, how the matter stood as to his journey;
~ at e-m, to attack (var við sjálft, at þeir mundu ~ at prestinum);
to stand by one, on one’s side (ek veit eigi víst, hvaðan G. inn ríki stendr at);
~ á e-u, to stand on, insist on (statt ei á því, er þér er bannat);
impers., stendr á illu einu með þeim, they are on very bad terms;
~ á e-m, to hang over one (sú skóggangssök, er á þér stendr); to refer to (þat heilræði stóð upp á þenna sama sendiboða);
~ eptir, to remain, be left;
~ fyrir e-u, to stand in the way of (þeir kváðu geip hennar ekki skyldu ~ fyrir þingreið þeira);
~ e-m fyrir þrifum, to stand in the way of one’s thriving;
to stand before one, protect one (vér skulum Egil af lífi taka, en hlífa engum, er fyrir honum vill ~);
~ hjá e-m, to stand by one, assist one;
~ í e-u, to be engaged in, busied with (~ í bardögum, einvígum, málum, kvánbœnum);
impers., stendr í deilu með þeim, there is a quarrel between them;
~ með blóma, to be in a flourishing condition;
~ móti (á móti, í móti) e-u, to stand against;
~ saman, to stand together, be gathered, amassed (þar stóð auðr mikill saman);
~ til e-s, to tend towards;
~ til umbótar, to stand for mending, need it;
sem bœn yður stendr til, as your prayer tends to;
eptir þeim efnum, sem honum þœtti til ~, according to the merits of the case;
eptir því sem lög stóðu til, as the law was (taka þeir allir við bótum, sem lög stóðu til);
impers., stendr til e-s, it is to be expected, feared (til langra meina mun ~, ef);
~ e-m til e-s, to assist, help one (B. segir, hversu Ó. hafði honum til staðit);
~ undan, to be lacking (mikit stendr undan við hann í vinfenginu);
~ vel undir e-t, to support well, back it up well (munu margir vel undir þat ~);
~ undir e-m, to be in one’s possession, keeping (féit stendr undir honum);
~ upp, to stand up from a seat (þá stóð S. upp ok mælti hátt); to rise from bed (~ upp ok klæðast); to be left standing (fimm einir menn stóðu upp á skipinu);
~ uppi, to be left standing (K. hinn auðgi flýði ok allt lið hans, þat er uppi stóð; hús þau, er uppi stóðu);
to be laid up ashore, of a ship (stigu þeir á skip þat, er þar hafði uppi staðit um vetrinn);
of a corpse, to lie on the bier (lík Kjartans stóð uppi í viku í Hjarðarholti);
of a bow, to be kept bent (boginn má eigi einart uppi ~);
~ við e-u, to withstand (víkingar svá harðfengir, at ekki stendr við);
impers., stendr við e-t, it is on the verge of (þeir áttu svá harða leika, at við meiðingar stóð);
~ yfir e-u, to be present at (heldr vildi hann þenna kjósa en ~ yfir drápi Þorgils frænda síns); to extend (þar er þeira ríki stendr yfir);
~ yfir, to stand over, last (hversu lengi skal fjárbón sjá yfir ~?);
í þessum griðum ok svardögum, sem yfir ~, which now stand, are in force;

to be able to stand, keep one’s feet (T. rendi fyrir hann törgu, ok steðjaði hann yfir upp, ok stóðst þó); to be valid, hold good (í öðru skulu þín ráð ~, en eigi hér um); to stand, bear, tolerate;
Kári stóðst þetta eigi, K. could not stand this;
~ e-m, to be equal to, be a match for (Ö. var svá frœkinn maðr, at fáir stóðust honum);
~ af e-m, to proceed, arise from one;
af henni mun ~ allt it illa, from her will arise all kind of ill;
svá stenzt af um ferð mína, the matter stands so as to my journey;
recipr., ~ á, to stand opposite one another (bœr hans stóðst á ok konungs atsetr); to correspond, answer to each other, in regard to size, duration etc. (þat stóðst á nesit þvert ok fylking þeira);
stóðst þat (allt féit) á endum ok þat, er G. átti at gjalda fyrir sik, it came just to what G. had to pay for himself;
stenzt helfr í móti með þeim hjónum, they were rather at sixes and sevens, did not agree well.

(-s, ar; pl. -ar, -ir, acc. -a, -u),  m. path;
ryðja stíginn, to clear the way;
taka úkunna stíga, to walk in unknown paths;
stemma stíga fyrir e-m, to bar one’s way, cut one off;
kanna úkunna stigu, to visit unknown paths (foreign lands);
af hverjum stig, from everywhere.

to arrange, settle;
vel er þessu í hóf stillt, this is very justly settled;
~ gørðinni, to moderate, regulate the arbitration;
~ til um e-t, to arrange (hversu þeir skyldi til ~ um ferðina);
~ svá til, at, to manage, that (nú skulum vér ~ svá til, at þeir nái eigi at renna);

big, great, of size (~ fiskr, stórt dýr);
stór veðr, rough weather, great gales;
stór sær, high sea;
gørði þá stórt á firðinum, the sea rose high;

a certain length of time, a while;
var s. til dags, it was a while before daybreak;
er á leið ~ina, in the course of time, after a while;
þat var s. ein, it was but a short time;
er ~ir líða (fram), as time goes on;
dvelja af ~ir, to kill the time;
lítil er líðandi ~, brief is the fleeting hour;

adverbial phrases;
af ~u, ere long, soon;
af annarri ~u, ‘the next while’, ere long;
á lítilli ~u, in a little while;
fyrir ~u, a while ago;
langri ~u fyrr, a long while before;
um s., for a while;
litla (skamma) s., for a short while;
allar ~ir, always;
alla s., all the time;
s. af s., s. frá s., little by little, by degrees;
~um (dat.), at times, sometimes

joined with another particle, ~ … ok, both … and;
~ starf ok torveldi, both toil and trouble;
~ ráns-maðrinn ok okrkarlinn, the robber as well as the usurer;
ok ~, and also, as also;
sumarit ok ~ um vetrinn, the summer, as also the winter;
fögr augu ok ~ snarlig, fair eyes and also sharp;

followed by an adjective and ‘at’, ~ ríkr, góðr, mikill, margir, fáir … at, so mighty, good, great, many, few … that;
~ at, so that;
kaldr (sjúkr) ~, at, so cold (sick) that;
contracted ‘~t’, ~t ek muna, that (so far as) I remember;
with a gen., Hallfreðr er ~ manna, at ek skil sízt, H. is such a man as I never can make out;
hón er ~ kvenna (= hón er ~ af konum), at mér er mest um at eiga, she is just such a woman as I most want;

joined to an a. or adv., about, pretty much;
slíkar ~ fortölur, somewhat such persuasion;
þat mun þó ~ nær fara, yet it will be just on the verge of that;
nakkvat ~, somewhat so;
fegnir nakkvat (nökkut) ~, rather glad;
mjök ~, almost, very nearly, all but (mjök ~ kominn at bana).

charge, the offence charged;
sönn ~, a true charge;
gera sakar á hendr sér, to incur charges;
gera sakir við e-n, to do offence or harm to one;
sannr at ~, proved (found) guilty;
gefa e-m (upp) sakir, to remit a charge;
gefa e-m ~ (sakar) á e-u, gefa e-m e-t at ~, to make a charge against one;
fœrast undan ~um, to plead not guilty;
vera (bundinn) í ~um við e-n, to have done offence to one (konungr tók stór gjöld af bóndum þeim, er honum þóttu í ~um við sik);

suit, action, in court;
eiga ~ á e-u, to have a ground for complaint;
ek á sjálfr ~ á því, that is my own affair;
sækja e-n ~um, to prosecute one;
svæfa allar sakar, to settle all causes;
fara með ~ina, to conduct the suit;
segja fram ~ sína, to declare ones suit;

cause, reason (þótti konungi sakir til, þótt hann hefði eigi komit);
fyrir hverja ~, for the sake of what, why?
fyrir þá (þessa) ~, for that reason, therefore;
af þeim ~um, from that cause (svá lauk þessu, at húsfreyja lézt af þeim ~um);
fyrir e-s sakir or ~um, for the sake of, because of;
fyrir ástar sakir, for love’s sake;
with respect to (skyldi boðit verða sem vegligast, bæði fyrir tilfanga sakir ok fjölmennis);
fyrir mínum ~um, for my sake, for my part;
um sakar þínar, for thy sake;
of time, um nokkurra nátta sakir, for a few nights;
of (um) stundar sakir, for a while;
um sinns sakir, for this once;
um nætr sakir, for one night;
sakir e-s, ~um e-s = fyrir sakir e-s, fyrir ~um e-s.

to take, catch, seize (tóku þeir laxinn ok otrinn ok báru með sér);
G. tók inni vinstri hendi spjótit á lopti, G. caught the spear with his left hand;
man hón ~ fé okkart allt með ráni, she will take all our goods by force;
~ e-n höndum, to seize one, take captive;
tökum vápn vár, let us take to our weapons;

fig., ~ trú, to take the faith, become a Christian;
~ skírn, to be baptized;
~ hvíld, to take a rest;
~ flótta, to take to flight;
~ rœðu, umrœðu, to begin a parley;
~ ráð, to take a counsel (= ~ til ráðs);
~ e-n orðum, to address one;
~ sættir or sættum, to accept terms;
~ þenna kost, to take this choice;
~ stefnu, to fix a meeting;
~ boði, to accept an offer;
~ sótt, to be taken ill;
~ úgleði, to get out of spirits;
~ andviðri, to meet with contrary winds;
~ konung, to take, elect a king;
~ konu, to take a wife;
~ úkunna stigu, to take to unknown ways;
~ e-n or e-m vel, to receive one well;
~ e-t þvert, to take a thing crossly, deny flatly;
~ upp höndum, to raise the hands;

to reach, stretch forth, touch;
fremri hyrnan tók viðbeinit, the upper horn caught the collar bone;
því at ek tek eigi heim í kveld, for I shall not reach home to-night;
hárit tók ofan á belti, the hair came down to her waist;

impers. it is taken;
þá tók af veðrit (acc.) then the storm abated;
kom á fótinn, svá at af tók, the stroke came on his leg, so that it was cut off;
sýnina tekr frá e-m, one becomes blind;
tók út skip Þangbrands, Th.’s ship drifted out;
um várit er sumarhita tók, when the summer heat set in;

with preps, and advs., ~ e-n af lífi, lífdögum, ~ e-n af, to take one’s life, put to death;
~ e-n af nafni ok veldi, to deprive one of his title and power;
~ af hesti, to take (the saddle) off a horse;
~ e-t af e-m, to take a thing from one, deprive one of (er vér tókum seglit af honum, þá grét hann);
~ af sér ópit, to cease weeping;
~ e-t af e-m, to get frotn one (tekr hann af öllu fólki mikil lof);
~ mikinn (mikil), lítinn (lítil) af e-u, to make (say) much, little of;
hón tók lítil af öllu, she said little about it, took it coldly;
øngan tek ek af um liðveizlu við þik, I will not pledge myself as to helping thee;
~ e-t af, to choose, take;
G. bauð þér góð boð, en þú vildir engi af ~, G. made thee good offers, but thou wouldst take none of them;
fara sem fœtr mega af ~, at the top of one’s speed;
hann sigldi suðr sem af tók, as fast as possible;
to abolish, do away with (lagði á þat allan hug al ~ af heiðni ok fornar venjur);
~ e-t aptr, to take back, render void (~ aptr þat, er ek gef); to recall (~ aptr orð, heil sín);
~ á e-u, to touch (hón tók á augum hans);
~ vel, auðvelliga, lítt, illa á e-u, to take (a thing) well, in good part, ill, in ill part (fluttu þeir þetta fyrir jarli, en hann tók vel á);
~ e-t á sik, to take upon oneself (kvaðst heldr vilja ~ þat á sik at gefa honum annát augat);
tóku þeir á sik svefn mikinn, they fell fast asleep;
~ arf eptir e-n, to inherit one;
~ e-t eptir, to get in return;
með því at þú gerir svá, sem ek býð þér, skaltu nökkut eptir ~, thou shalt have some reward;
~ e-t frá e-m, to take a thing away from one (þeir tóku spjótin frá þeim ok báru út á ána);
~ e-n frá e-u, to deprive one of (~ e-n frá landi, ríki);
~ e-t fyrir e-t, to take in return for (hann keypti sveinana ok tók fyrir þá vesl gott ok slagning); to take for, look upon as (lökum vér þat allt fyrir satt; því tek ek þat fyrir gaman);
~ fyrir e-t, to refuse (tók E. eigi fyrir útanferð at sumri);
~ hendi í e-t, to thrust one’s hand into;
~ í hönd e-m, to shake hands with one;
~ í móti, to offer resistance (þeir brendu víða bygðina, en bœndr tóku ekki í móti);
~ niðr, to pull down, demolish (~ niðr til grundvallar allt þat verk); to graze a little, = ~ til jarðar (þeir láta nú ~ niðr hesta sína);
~ niðri, to touch (feel) the bottom;
~ ofan, to take down (Högni tekr ofan atgeirinn); to pull down (hann hafði látil ~ ofan skála sinn);
~ í sundr, to cut asunder;
impers., slœmdi sverðinu til hans, svá at í sundr tók manninn, so that the man was cleft asunder;
~ til e-s, to take to (tóku þá margir til at níða hann);
~ til máls (orðs, orða), to begin to speak;
nú er þar til máls at ~, at, now we must take up the story at this point, that;
~ til varnar, to begin the defence;
~ til e-s, to have recourse to, resort to (~ e-t til ráðs, bragðs); to concern (þetta mál, er til konungs tók);
láta e-t til sín ~, to let it concern oneself, meddle with (Gísli lét fátt til sín ~);
~ e-n til e-s, to choose, elect (Ólafr var til konungs tekinn um allt land);
absol., ~ til, to begin (hann hélt allt austr um Svínasund, þá tók til vald Svíakonungs);
~ e-t til, to take to, do;
ef hann tekr nökkut illt til, if he takes to any ill;
~ um e-t, to take hold of, grasp (nú skaltu ~ um fót honum);
~ e-t undan, to take away;
impers., undan kúnni tók nyt alla, the cow ceased to give milk;
~ undan, to run away, escape (B. tók undan með rás);
~ undir e-t, to take hold under a thing;
hann tók undir kverkina ok kyssti hana, he took her by the chin and kissed her;
to undertake, take upon oneself;
H. kvaðst ekki ~ mundu undir vandræði þeira, H. said he would have nothing to do with their troubles;
~ undir e-t með e-m, to back, help one in a thing (vil ek, at þér takit undir þetta mál með mér);
þau tóku undir þetta léttiliga, they seconded it readily;
hann tók seinliga undir, he was slow to answer;
~ undir, to echo, resound (fjöllin tóku undir);
~ e-t undir sik, to take on hand (Gizurr tók undir sik málit); to lay hold of (hann tekr undir sik eignir þær, er K. átti í Noregi);
~ e-t upp, to pick up (S. tók upp hanzka sinn);
~ upp fé fyrir e-m, to seize on, confiscate;
~ upp borð, to set up the tables before a meal, but also to remove them after a meal;
~ upp bygð sína, to remove one’s abode;
hón tekr mart þat upp, er fjarri er mínum vilja, she takes much in hand that is far from my will;
drykk ok vistir, svá sem skipit tók upp, as the ship could take;
~ upp ný goðorð, to establish new priesthoods;
~ upp verknað, to take up work;
~ upp stœrð, to take to pride;
~ upp sök, to take up a case;
~ upp draum, to interpret a dream;
~ e-t upp, to choose (seg nú skjótt, hvern kost þú vill upp ~);
absol., ~ upp, to extend, rise (rekkjustokkr tekr upp á millum rúma okkarra);
~ út, to run out (E. tók út ór stofunni);
~ við e-u, to receive (A. hafði tekit við föðurarf sínum);
~ vel við e-m, to receive one well, give one a hearty welcome;
~ við trú, to take the faith;
þeir tóku vel við, they made a bold resistance;
tók við hvárr af öðrum, one took up where the other left off;
~ yfir e-t, to extend over (hann skal eignast af Englandi þat, sem uxahúð tekr yfir);
impers. to come to an end, succeed (kveðst nú vænta, at nú mundi yfir ~);
þeir munu allt til vinna at yfir taki við oss, to get the better of us;

 to take place, begin (tókst orrusta);
ráð þau skyldu ~ at öðru sumri, the wedding should take place next summer;
~ með þeim góðar ástir, they came to love one another much;
to be brought about, take effect, succeed;
þat tókst honum, he succeeded;
~ til, to happen (ef svá vill til ~);
~ e-t á hendr, to take on one’s hands (mun ek þat á hendr ~ at fylkja þar fyrir liði mínu);
láta af ~, to let oneself be deprived of;
recipr., ~ (at) orðum, to speak to one another;
~ á, ~ fangbrögðum, to wrestle;
~ í hendr, to shake hands.

talk, parley, conversation;
vera (sitja) á ~i við e-n, to be (sit) talking with (ek hefi verit á ~i við Hrafn ok Sturlu);
eiga ~ við e-n, to have a talk with;
taka ~ sitt, taka ~ með sér, to begin to talk together;
skilja ~it, to cease talking;
hélt biskup mikit ~ af honum, held much talk with him;
taka annat ~, to change the conversation to another subject;

with preps., ~ e-t af, to dissuade;
~ e-t af fyrir e-m, to dissuade one from;
~ at e-u, to blame, find fault with, object to (man ek ekki at ~, þó at þú trúir á þat goð, er þér líkar);
~ á e-n, to rebuke, blame;
~ e-t eptir e-m, to grudge one a thing (ekki tel ek mat eptir ykkr);
~ fyrir e-m, to try to persuade one (S. boðaði Þangbrandi heiðni ok taldi lengi fyrir honum);
~ trú fyrir e-m, to preach the gospel to one;
~ e-t ofan, to dissuade from;
~ til e-s, to claim (taldi hann til ríkis);
~ e-t upp, to enumerate, reckon up (þá taldi Þ. upp konur þær, sem vóru í Borgarfirði úgiptar);

(tvær, tvau), card. numb. two;
höggva tveim höndum, with both hands;
höggva í tvau, in two, asunder;
tveimr er tveggja hugr, two men two minds;
tveim megin Slésvíkr, on both sides of Slesvik.

fig., ~ mátti skilja, thence it could be understood;
skulu vér ~ at vera, we will be on that side;
~ af veit ek, therefrom I know;
allan helming, eða ~ af meira, a full half and even more;

there, at that place (~ var fjölmenni mikit);
~ er, ~ sem, there where;
þeir þóttust þar eiga allt traust er hann var, they thought that all their hope was there where he was;
fig. there, in that case, at that point (lýkr þar viðskiptum þeira);
~ sem, ~ er, whereas; þú gerir þik góðan, ~ sem þú ert þjófr ok morðingi, whereas thou art both thief and murderer;
mun F. annat ráð taka, þar sem hann hefir drepit Njál föðurbróður minn, seeing that he has killed N.;

with prep. = an oblique case of the demonstrative pronoun ~ af, therefrom, from that, = af því ~ at, thereat;
~ á, thereupon;
~ eptir, after that, thereafter;
~ fyrir, therefore;
~ í, therein;
~ í móti, in return (~ í móti vil ek eiga hálft dýrit);
~ með, therewith, besides;
~ ór, there from, there out of;
~ til, thereto;
~ til er, to the place where (O. gekk ~ til, er H. var); till, until (konungr ríðr langa leið til er hann finnr eitt hús);
~ um, about that, = um þat (verið eigi ~ um hugsjúkir);
~ undir, there underneath;
~ út í frá, furthermore, besides;
~ við, thereby, by that;
~ yfir, there above.

at once, forthwith (hón gekk ~ til hans);
þá var hann ~ fjarri, then he was already far away;
~ á unga aldri, when quite young;
~ í stað, at once;
Þ. bar merkit ~ eptir honum, immediately after him;

to receive, accept of;
~ e-t af e-m, at e-m, to receive (get) from one, at one’s hands;
~ e-t at gjöf, to receive as a present;
~ e-n undan, to get one relieved, set free (ef þér vilit þessa menn undan ~);

 (pl. -ir),  f. hard strugggle, great exertion, labour, hard task (ek mun láta þik vinna til ráðahags þessa ~ir nökkurar);
þola ~ir, to suffer hardshps;
gera ~ til e-s, to try hard;
berjast til ~ar, to fight to the end.

 obstinacy (Hrafn harðnaði í sínu þrái við biskup);
í ~ e-m, in defiance of, in spite of (gerði þat hverr í ~ öðrum);
honum þótti þat mjök gert í ~ sér, in his despite, in order to thwart him;
ganga á ~, to veer round and become contrary, of wind (því næst gekk veðr á ~).

(-tta, -ttr),  to prosecute a thing stoutly to strive hard in a thing;
~ leik, rás við e-n, to contend in a game, run a race with one;
~ kapp-sigling, to sail a race;
~ drykkju við e-n, to vie in drinking with one;
~ á drykkjuna, to strive hard to drink;
~ e-t með kappmælum, to dispute eagerly;
~ lög um e-t, to contend at law;
jarlarnir þreyttu þetta með sér, it came to high words between them;
þeir þreyttu at komast inn í borgina, they tried hard to get into the burgh;
~ hest sporum, to prick the horse with the spurs.

 from þrjóta;
at an end, past, gone (get ek, at þrotin sé þín mesta gæfa);
~ at drykk, out of drink;
hestrinn var ~, quite exhausted;
en er allir vóru þrotnir á at biðja hann til, when all were tired of begging him.

athwart, transverse, opp. to endilangr (tjalda um þveran skálann);
lá hverr um annan þveran, in heaps;
fara ~ á fœti at e-u, to be unwilling for a thing;
beita þvert, to sail close-hauled, near the wind;

cross, obstinate;
hann kvazt ekki ~ vera í því at selja skipit, he said he would not be hard about selling the ship;
setja þvert nei fyrir, to deny flatly;
ek vil þó eigi þvert taka, ef þér er lítit um, I will not take it crossly, if it is not to thy mind.

with dat. it seems to one, one thinks (þykki mér ráð, at þú farir at finna Gizur hvíta);
e-m þykkir at e-u, one feels hurt at, takes it to heart, is displeased with (þótti mönnum mikit at um víg Kjartans);
mér þykkir fyrir (or fyr) e-u, I dislike, am unwilling to (mér þykkir meira fyrir en öðrum mönnum at vega menn);
e-m þykkir mikit um e-t, one takes it much to heart (honum þótti svá mikit um fall Ólafs konungs, at);
e-m þykkir mikit undir e-u, one thinks it of great importance (mikit þótti spökum mönnum undir því, at);
impers., vilda ek, at þér þœtti eigi verr, I wish that thou wouuldst not take it amiss;
þótti sinn veg hvárum, they disagreed;
þykki mér sem undan sé gaflveggrinn, it seems to me as though the gable-wall were down;
þótti mér þeir sœkja at (= sem þeir sœkti at), methought they pressed hard on me;

refl., ~st, to seem to oneself, think oneself, think (en ek þykkjumst þó mjök neyddr til hafa verit);
hann þykkist einn vita allt, he thinks he alone knows everything;
þykkist hann mjök fyrir öðrum mönnum, he thinks himself far above other men;
en Brynhildr þykkist brúðr var-gefin, but B. will think she is ill-matched.

(gen. þarfar, pl. þarfar),  f. want, need, necessity;
e-s er ~, a thing is wanted, is necessary (hón kvað þess litla ~);
e-m er e-s ~, e-m er ~ á e-u, one is in need of;
þá er ~ verðr, when wanted;
ef gørast þarfar þess, if need be;
þola ~ e-s, to be in want of;
fjár síns, er fengit hefir, skylit maðr ~ þola, a man should not stint himself of money he has made;
~um meirr, more than wanted, excessively;
e-t kemr vel í þarfar, it comes to good use, comes in when wanted;
vinna ~, to suffice;
kunna ~ (with infin.), to long to, feel impelled to.

to fear, suspect (eigi uggi ek brœðr þína);
eigi uggi (imperat.) þú Ísungsbana, fear thou not the slayer of Í.;
~ ekki (lítt, fátt) at sér, to apprehend no danger, be off one’s guard;
er þat ~nda, at, it is to be feared that;

to enjoy, be happy in, content with a thing;
þeir undu illa sínum hlut, they were much dissatisfied with their lot;
~ lífi, to enjoy life;
~ vel við ráð sitt, to be content with one’s lot;
uni (imperat.) þú vel við, be content!
~ sér, ~ við sik, to be happy, feel at ease;
~ lítt eptir e-n, to feel deeply the loss (death) of a person.

from under, from beneath;
hann hjó ~ honum fótinn, he cut his leg off;
hann spratt upp ~ borðinu, he sprang up from (sitting at) the table;
hann spratt upp ~ garðinum, from under the fence;
halda skipum ~ landi, to stand aivayfrom the shore;

under;
þá brast í sundr jörð ~ hesti hans, the earth burst asunder under his horse;
þungr ~ árum, heavy to row;
þeir leita þeirra ok finna þá ~ eyju einni, they seek for them and find them under an island;
~ þeim hesti var alinn Eiðfaxi, that horse was the sire of E.;

ellipt. or adverbial usages;
vóru þau (Njáll ok Bergþóra) úbrunnin ~, they were unburned underneath;
meðan töður manna eru ~, whilst the hay is lying (mown, but not got in);
ef þér þœtti nökkut ~ um mik, if thou hast cared at all for me;
sól (dagr) er ~, the sun (day) is down, under the horizon;
væri oss mikit ~, at vér fengim liðsinni hans, it were worth much to us to get his help;

fig. leggja ~ sik, to lay under oneself, to subjugate;
þjóna ~ e-n, to serve under one;
þessa laxveiði gaf hann ~ kirkjuna, he made it over to the church;
bera fé ~ e-n, to bribe one;
leggja virðing konungs ~ vápn mín, to let it depend on my weapons;
ef ~ oss skal koma kjörit, if the choice is to be left with us;
Hjört þótti mér þeir hafa ~, H. me thought they got under;
ef kona tekr mann ~ bónda sinn, if she be untrue to her husband;
bjóða fé í leigu ~ sik, to offer to pay money for one’s passage.

up;
~ ok niðr, ~ ok ofan, up and down;
íkorni renn ~ ok niðr eptir askinum, a squirrel springs up and down the tree;
grípa ~, to pick up;
~ með, up along;
líta ~, to look up;
búa (gera) ~ hvílu, to make up a bed;
drepa ~ eld, to strike fire;

with the notion of consuming;
drekka (eta) ~, to drink (eat) up;
ausa ~, to bale out;
skipta ~, to share it all out;

~ á, upon, on;
~ á trú þina, upon thy faith;
~ á Ebresku, in Hebrew;
sjá, horfa ~ á, to look upon;
hugsa ~ á, to yearn for, to be bent on.

up;
sitja ~, to sit up;
hafa ~ øxina, to lift the axe;
standa ~, to be left standing (þeir, er þá stóðu ~);
to lie ashore, of a ship;
var ~ röst mikil á firðinum, the current rose high;
eiga vef ~, to have a loom up, to be at work weaving;
vera snemma ~, to be up early;
láta e-t ~, to come forth with;

to be of importance (ef þér þykkir ~ um mína vináttu);
~ e-n, to concern one, be of importance to one (at segja þér þat, er þik ~r);
~ miklu, litlu, engu, to matter much, little, naught;
hann kvað þá engu þat ~, said it was no business of theirs;
~r engu um vára aptrkomu, it does not matter whether we come back or not;

to guard, watch, defend (~ land, alla vega);
~ e-m e-t, to ward a thing off from a person withhold it from him;
~ mér bátinn: to forbid me the boat, by force;
~ e-m, with infin. to hinder, prevent (~r hann fé váru at komast yfir ána);

to be liable to, punishable by (~r þat fjörbaugsgarð);
hvat ~r, þótt vér reynim, what risk though we try?;
~ e-m e-t, to entail as a penalty upon one (spurði, hvat konu ~ði, ef hón væri í brókum jafnan svá sem karlar);
~ við lög, to be punishable by law (þat ætla ek við lög ~ at vinna á Mikjálsdegi);
ok lét ~ þriggja marka útlegð, he asked for a fine of three marks;

to keep, preserve, with dat. (~ þessum steini);
later with acc., svá skal ek þik ~, at þik skal ekki saka, I shall take such good care of thee, that no harm shall come to thee;
~ sik við e-u, frá e-u, to abstain from;
ef maðr tekr grið ok varðveitir þat ekki, if a man takes up an abode and does not keep it;

(pl. -ir),  f. hope, expectation, prospect;
mér er ~, at, I expect that;
er ~ e-s, it is to be expected;
sliks var ~, this was to be looked for;
þeim var ills ~ at Þór, they apprehended evil from Th.;
sem hann hafði áðr sagt á ~, as he had given to understand;
sem ~ var at, as was to be expected;
vita sér engis ótta ~ir, to apprehend no danger;
konungs var þangat ~, the king was expected there;
e-t stendr til ~ar, it bids fair;
e-t er at ~um, it is what could be looked for (ok er þat at ~um við skaplyndi Þorgeirs);
vita ~ til e-s, to expect;
hann vissi enga ~, at, he had no expectation that;
eiga barn í ~um, to have a child in prospect;
draga e-t í ~, to hold out a prospect of;
mörgum þykkir fyrir ~ komit, many think it is past all hope;
þá er allar ~ir vóru rannsakaðar, when all likely places were searched;
dat., ~u, with compar., ~u bráðara, skjótara, sooner than expected;
~u verr, worse than might be expected.

 (gen. pl. ~ja),  n. pledge, surety;
setja e-t í ~, at ~i, to pledge, give in pledge;
hann bauð at ~i alla eign sína, he offered all his property as a pledge;
leysa (út) ~, to redeem a pledge;
hafa e-t í ~i, to have at stake;
vera í ~i, to be at stake (líf mitt er í ~i).

(vel; valda; valiðr, valdr, valinn),  to choose, select, pick out (v. e-n til fylgdar við sik);
v. um e-t, to choose between;
er gott um at v., there is plenty of choice;
eiga um tvá kosti at v., to have two alternatives to choose between;
v. e-m hæðilig (hörð) orð, to speak ignominiously to (or of) one;
hafa valit líð, to have picked troops;
refl., ~st til e-s, to come forward (völdust margir göfgir menn til þessar ferðar).

~ e-m, to happen to, befall one (slikt verðr opt ungum mönnum);
þat varð Skarphéðni, at stökk í sundr skóþvengr hans, it happened to S. that his shoe-string snapped asunder;
sjaldan verðr víti vörum, the wary man will seldom make a slip;
e-m verðr þörf e-s, one comes to be in need of;

to happen to be, occur;
í lœk þann, er þar verðr, in the brook that happens to be there;
varð fyrir þeim fjörðr, they came on a fjord;
~ á leið e-s, to be on one’s path, happen to one;

~ brottu, to leave, absent oneself (þeir sá þann sinn kost líkastan at ~ á brottu);
~ úti, to go away (verð úti ok drag ongan spott at oss);
to perish in a storm from cold (sumir urðu úti);
þeim þótti honum seint heim ~, they thought that he was long in coming home;

followed by a noun, a., pp., adv., as predicate, to become;
þá verðr þat þinn bani, it will be thy death;
~ glaðr, hryggr, reiðr, to become glad, sad, angry;
~ dauðr to die (áðr Haraldr inn hárfagri yrði dauðr) with participles;
ok varð ekki eptir honum gengit, he was not pursued;
~ þeir ekki fundnir, they could not be found;
blóð hans varð ekki stöðvat, the blood could not be staunched;
þeim varð litit til hafs, they happened to look seaward;
impers., e-m verðr bilt, one is amazed;
Kolbeini varð ekki fyrir, K. lost his head, was paralysed;
with adverbs; hann varð vel við skaða sinn, he bore his loss well, like a man;
jarl varð illa við þetta, the earl was vexed by this;

with infin., denoting necessity, one must, needs, is forced, obliged to do;
þat verðr hverr at vinna, er ætlat er, every one must do the work that is set before him;
þar er bera verðr til grjót, where stones have to be carried;
verð ek nú flýja, now I must flee;

with preps., ~ af e-u, to come to pass (var um rœtt, at hann skyldi leita fara, en eigi varð af);
varð ekki af ferðinni, the journey came to nought was given up;
verðr þetta af, at hann tekr við sveinunum, the end was that at last he took the boys;
starf ok kostnaðr varð af þessu, trouble and expenses arose from this;
livat verðr af e-u, what becomes of;
hvat varð af húnum mínum, what has become of my cubs?;
~ at e-u, to become (~ at undri, undrsjónum);
veiztu, hvat þér mun ~ at bana, knowest thou what will be the cause of thy death?;
~ at engu, to come to nothing;
~ á, to come on, happen;
þvat sem á yrði síðan, whatever might happen later on;
e-m verðr á, one makes a blunder, mistake (þótti þér ekki á ~ fyrir honum, er hann náði eigi fénu?);
~ eptir, to be left (honum varð þar eptir geit ok hafr);
~ fyrir e-u, to meet with (~ fyrir goða reiði);
to forebode (~ fyrir stórfundum);
~ fyrir e-m, to be in one’s way, as a hindrance (því meira sem oss verðr fyrir, því harðara skulu þér niðr koma);
~ í, to happen (tókust nú upp leikar sem ekki hefði í orðit);
~ til e-s, to come forth to do a thing, be ready to;
en sá er nefndr Hermóðr, er til þeirar farar varð, who undertook this journey;
~ við e-m, to respond to (bið ek þik, at þú verðir við mér, þó at engi sé verðleiki til).

to throw, with dat. (hann varp af sér skildinum);
hestrinn féll ok varp honum af baki, threw him off;
hann verpr sér í söðulinn, he throws himself into the saddle;
~ af sér klæðum, to throw off the clothes;
~ mœðiliga öndinni, to draw a deep sigh;
~ orðum (or orði) á e-n, to address (þessi varp orðum á konung ok spurði);
væntir mik þess, at margir verpi þar góðum orðum á mik, that many men will have good words to say of me;
~ e-n inni, to shut one in;
impers. to be thrown;
þar varp út údaun miklum, a great stench came out;

to cast up (~ haug eptir fornum sið);
þeir urpu haug eptir Gunnar, they raised a mound over G.;
~ aptr hauginn, to shut the cairn;
~ vef, to warp a web (sá er orpinn vefr ýta þörmum);

pp., orpinn;
sandi orpinn, covered with sand;
aldri orpinn, bent with age;
uppi orpinn fyrir e-m, quite overwhelmed, at one’s mercy;

against;
hann sló honum niðr v. steininum, he dashed his head against the stone;
hús liggja v. velli, the houses lie in ruins;
kasta sér niðr v. velli, to cast oneself down on the ground;
er inn efri kjöptr v. himni, en inn neðri við jörðu, the upper jaw touches the heaven, the lower the earth;
hann hjó hann upp v. garðinum, he smote him close by the fence;
skera af sér strenginn við øxinni, to cut the string, asunder against the axe;

ellipt. usages;
stinga v. fótum, to stop;
hrífa v., to catch hold;
búast v., to make oneself ready;
risa v., to withstand;
hvatz hann fiðr v., whatsoever he may object;

by, at, close to (sníða skeggit við hökuna);
skjöldr við skjöld, shield to shield;
v. Sandhólaferju, at Sandholferry;
v. veginn, by the wayside;
v. ána, by the river;
draga segl v. hún, to hoist the sail to the top;
festa e-n v. meið, tré, to fasten to a pole, tree;
binda v. fót e-s, to bind up a broken leg;
dró upp flóka v. austr, in the east;

of time, towards, at;
v. solar-setr, at sunset;
v. sól, with the sun, at sunrise;
v. aptan, towards evening;
vera v. aldr, to be stricken in years;

at, by (vera heima v. bú sitt);
Hrútr var v. skip um sumarit, H. stayed by his ship during the summer;
sitja v. stýri, to sit at the rudder;
styðja sik v. e-t, to lean on;
ganga v. staf, to walk with a staff;
vera v. e-t, to be present at;
sitja v. drykk, to sit at drink;

of cause, by, at;
falla v. högg, to fall by a stroke;
sigla v. stjörnuljós, to sail by starlight;
verða reiðr v. e-t, to become wroth at;

according to, after (gera klæði v. vöxt e-s);
v. sik, in proportion;
hann var skapaðr allr v. sik, well shaped, symmetrical;
vita, hvat v. sik væri, to know what was the matter;

ellipt. usages;
bregða við, to start;
hann þagði v., he remained silent;
fá v. þrjú skip, to add three ships;
þurfa v., to need;
bjarga, hjálpa e-u v., to help, put right;
koma e-u við, to bring about.

 kind towards one’s friends;
neut. vingott, friendliness (heldr vil ek gjalda féit, ok mun vingott í móti koma);
vingott var með þeim, they were good friends;
eiga vingott við e-n, to be good friends with a person.

to work, perform, do;
~ verk sin, to do one’s work;
þat verðr hverr at ~, er ætlat er, every one must do the work that is set before him;
þér hafið mikit stórvirki unnit, ye have done a great deed;
~ eið, sœri, to take an oath;
~ bœtr á e-u, to redress;
~ e-m beinleika, beina, to show hospitality to one;
~ e-m geig, bana, to work harm, death, to one;
~ e-m úsœmd, to bring shame, disgrace on one;
~ e-m bót (illt), to do one good (harm);
~ e-m hefnd, to take revenge on;

followed by an a. or pp., to make (~ e-n sáran, reiðan, barðan, felldan);
vann hann yfirkominn Harald, he succeeded in vanquishing H.;
~ hefnt, to take revenge;
~ annat, to prove;

with preps., ~ at e-u, to be busy with (~ at heyi);
~ at svínum, sauðfé, to tend swine, sheep;
fá ekki at unnit, to effect nothing (ekki munu þér fá at unnit svá búit);
~ at e-m, to do away with, kill (þat var markat á tjaldinu, at Sigurðr vann at Fáfni);
~ e-t á, to do, effect (höfum vér mikit á unnit í várri ferð);
~ á e-m, to do one bodily injury, = ~ áverka á e-m (með þann hug at ~ á Gunnari);
~ e-t til e-s, to do one thing in order to obtain or effect another (vildi hann ~ þat til sættar með þeim brœðrum);
þat vil ek til ~, that I am ready to do;
~ til e-s, to make oneself worthy of, deserve (~ til dauða);
~ til fjár ok frægðar, to act so as to gain money and fame;
~ við e-u, to withstand (mátti hann ok eigi við sköpum ~ né sínu aldrlagi);
~ e-n yfir, to overcome;

refl., ~st, to last, suffice;
meðan dagrinn vannst, as long as the day lasted;
Illugi elti hann meðan eyin vannst, to the end of the island;
festrin vannst eigi til jarðar, the rope was not long enough to touch the ground;
þar sátu konur úti frá sem vannst, as there was room;
ek vinnst eigi til þér at launa, I am unable to reward thee;
ekki unnust þau mjök fyrir, they did little to support themselves;
recipr., ~st á, to wound one another.

fig., to value, estimate (þat kann engi ~ nema guð einn);
with gen., ~ e-t mikils, to rate highly, think much of;
~ lítils, to think or make light of;
~ e-t engis (einskis), to make nothing of;
~ e-t með sjálfum sér, to bethink oneself of, consider;
konungr virði mest skáld sín, he held his poets in the highest estimation;
virði jarl hann vel, the earl showed him great honour;
~ til, to pay heed to, regard;
hann kveðst vilja ~ til enn heilaga Jacobum postola, he said he would respect the sanctity of St. James;
~ e-t til e-s, to consider (deem) as (~ e-t til útrúleika við e-n);
~ e-t vel, illa fyrir e-m, to put it to his credit, discredit;

refl., ~st vel, to be highly esteemed (hann virðist hvervetna vel);
impers., virðist öllum mönnum vel til hans, everybody liked him;
virðist oss svá, sem minnkaðist vár sœmd í því, it seems to us, we think that …;
virðist hann konunginum afbragðsmaðr, the king thought him to be an exceptional man.

to know;
engi vissi skapara sinn, no one knew his maker;
þeir er vel mart vitu, those who know many things;
~ sik saklausan, to know oneself to be innocent;
veizt þú, hvat þér man verða at bana, dost thou know what will be the cause of thy death?;
~ skyn á e-m, to know one;
Flosi kvaðst eigi ~ skyn á, hverir lögmenn væri beztir, F. said that he knew nothing about who were the best lawyers;
~ ván or vánir e-s, to expect, look for (G. vissi slíks matar þar ekki ván);
~ fram, to be prescient, know the future;
veit þat trúa min, upon my faith!;

to look, be turned towards;
~ upp, fram, aptr, to look (be turned) upwards, forwards, backwards;
~ móti sólu, at sjánum, to face the sun, the sea;
~ til norðrættar, to look north;
ormahöfuð öll vitu inn í húsit, all the heads of the serpents look into the hall;
with gen., þeir fundu vínvið allt þar sem holta vissi, they found the vine wherever there was woodland;

to mean, have such and such bearing;
hvat veit óp þetta, what means this shouting?;
eigi veit þannig við, that is not the case;
seg mér hit sanna, hversu við veit, how things stand;
ok rœddu um þat, at nú mundi vel ~, that things were likely to take a good turn;

with preps.:
~ af e-u, to know of (ekki vissi á. af kaupi þeira);
~ e-t at sér, to know oneself to be guilty of;
~ e-t eptir sér = ~ e-t at sér (~ eptir sér slíkan glœp);
~ e-t frá sér, to be conscious (sensible) of (ek var svá syfjaðr, at ek vissa fátt frá mér);
~ e-t fyrir, to know beforehand (ørlög sín viti engi maðr fyrir);
~ til e-s, ~ til um e-t, to know of (ekki vissu landsmenn til um ferð Þórólfs);
~ ekki til sín, ~ ekki til manna, to have lost consciousness, one’s senses;
recipr. to know of an another (þeir vissust jafnan til í hafinu);
~ um e-t, to make inquiries about (fara at ~ um e-t);
to know about (eigi veit ek um gaman þetta);
~ e-t við e-n, to get to know a thing, from one (mun ek ~ við skipverja mina, hvat þeim sýnist ráð).

 wide, large, extensive (víðir skógar);
á víða vega, widely abroad, broadcast;
víðs vegar, in all directions (flýja, dreifast víðs vegar);
víðs fjarri, far away.

to move, turn, with dat. (sveinninn mátti hvergi ~ höfðinu);
hann veik sér hjá dyrunum, he passed by the door;
~ e-m af hendi, to turn one off, dismiss;
~ e-u af sér, to decline (A. veik því af sér);
~ e-u til ráða (atkvæða) e-s, to hand it over to one’s decision;
~ tali, rœðu, máli til e-s, to turn one’s speech to one, address oneself to one;
~ til e-s, to mention, refer to (hefr hann svá kvæðit, at hann víkr til Ólafs konungs);
~ til við e-n, to broach it to one;
~ á e-t, to hint at (Þ. víkr á nökkut í Þorgeirsdrápu á misþokka þeira);
~ á við e-n = ~ til við e-n;
~ svá bœkr til, at, the books indicate that;

to move, go (þeir ~ þegar eptir þessum mönnum);
~ aptr, to return (þeir víku aptr ok leita þeira);
hann veik heim, he returned home;
~ til norðrættar, to trend north (þat ríki víkr til norðrættar);
~ inn til hafnar, to veer round and seek harbour (önnur skipin viku inn til hafna af leiðinni);
~ eptir e-m, to follow one’s example;
~ eptir e-u, to yield to (~ eptir freistingu fjándans);

impers. to turn, trend;
landinu víkr til landnorðrs, the land trends to north-east;
svá veik viðr veginum, at þar var hraungata mikil, the road was of this nature;
nú veik svá við, at liðit fór yfir á eina mikla, now it came to pass;
nú víkr sögunni vestr til Breiðafjarðardala, now the story turns west to B.;
en þar veik annan veg af, it turned out quite another way;

refl., ~st, to turn oneself;
~st aptr, to return;
hón sat ok veikst eigi, she sat and stirred not;
~st eptir e-u, to turn after, imitate;
~st undan e-u, to evade, decline;
~st undan við e-n, to refuse one;
~st undir hlýðni við e-n, to do homage to;
~st við e-t, to respond to (kvað hann vel hafa vikizt við sína nauðsyn).

(að, rarely -ta, -t),  to show, point out, indicate;
~ e-m leið, to show one the way;
~ augum í e-n, to direct, fix the eyes on one;
~ hundi at mann í, to set a hound on a person;
~ e-m til sætis, to show one where to sit;
~ e-m til landskostar, to direct one to the best of the land;
þeir vísuðu honum til Kols, they showed him the way to Kol, told him where he was to be found;
~ e-m til vegar, to show one the road;
~ e-m frá, to send one away, reject an application;
~ á e-t, to point at, indicate (fleiri eru þau tíðindi, er kvæðit ~r á);
~ svá til, at, to indicate (~ ok svá til enskar bœkr, at);
impers., ~r svá til í sögu Bjarnar, it is indicated, referred to, in the story of B.;
with infin., ~ e-m at gera e-t, to tell, prompt one to do a thing.

  delight, happiness;
njóta yndis, to enjoy happiness, live in bliss;
þeira samfarar urðu ekki at ~, their married life was not happy;
nema, festa ~ á e-m stað, to feel happy in a place;
ok hvárki nam hann ~ á Íslandi né í Nóregi, he found no rest in Iceland or in Norway.

(neut. ýmist; pl. ~ir, ~ar, usually contracted ýmsir or ymsir, ýmsar or ymsar, neut. ýmis, ýmsi or ýms),  a. now this, now that (ýmist hann hugði);
hann gerði ýmist, hjó eða lagði, he cut and thrust alternately;
esp. pl. various, sundry, now these, now those, by turns (höfðu ymsir sigr);
í ymsum stöðum, in various places;
reka kaupferðir til ~a landa, to go on trading expeditions to various countries;
einir ok ~ir, one and another, sundry;
allir ok þó ~ir, all by turn;
ýmist … eða, now … now, sometimes … sometime (Bolli var ýmist í Tungu eða at Helga felli).
